{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'pwd && rg -n \"虘|虐钟|好.{0,6}读为 hào|好.*hào\" research pipeline content'","aggregated_output":"/home/catalin/.profile: line 29: /tmp/mfc-uv-bin/env: No such file or directory\n/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:6:虐钟; the target first graph has a closed lower component, matching the same\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:7:book's 虘 headword rather than the open-right lower part of its 虐 headword.\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:8:The adjacent scan graph is 钟, not 鐘. A proposed 虘鐘 replacement was therefore\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:15:to the 虘 control. The coordinator independently inspected the original target\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:29:The producer now repairs only raw offsets [1438,1439), 虐 → 虘. Its actual\nresearch/ocr-morphology-and-mixed-script-20261003.md:31:producer text at [1438,1440) is 虘钟, and the current consumer record ending\nresearch/hao-publication-held-for-actual-glyph-20261003.md:12:虘鐘 and says no corpus correction was made. The producer/consumer now actually\nresearch/hao-publication-held-for-actual-glyph-20261003.md:13:have first-only 虐→虘, preserving printed 钟. The record must be superseded by\ncontent/entries/597D.json:598:      \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:697:      \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:976:        \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:1075:        \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:1326:          \"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:1334:          \"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:1377:          \"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\",\ncontent/entries/597D.json:1382:          \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:107:      \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:206:      \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\",\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:457:        \"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:465:        \"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:508:        \"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\",\ncontent/dossiers/597D.json:513:        \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"\ncontent/research/597D.json:16:      \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\ncontent/research/597D.json:106:      \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\",\ncontent/research/597D.json:318:      \"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\ncontent/research/597D.json:326:      \"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\ncontent/research/597D.json:369:      \"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\",\ncontent/research/597D.json:374:      \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/source_checkpoint.json:30:            \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/initial-followup/research-repair-1/prompt.txt:180:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X-874259a2b827e31d1455\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-e79ea1cfe5b5aa959a40\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6339f9c0072cd12d52a\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-f6f7d68f09a02c854d24\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2829f816c26a5d71ec70\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-253281f9589a65c2d2a6\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-dd1f049d3ded734931ba\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; scanned headword and continuation checked on PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The scanned entry identifies 小 as pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny grains of dust or sand, calling it an early graph of 沙; it says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and gives that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions. It cites 《粹》1004 for 小雨, then describes extensions to low status, contempt, and youth. It says the current form was corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, with similar use in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its explanation continues onto printed p. 65. Direct scan inspection confirms the cited headword and passage. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and the text’s citation 《粹》1004. The scan supports what this author asserts; it does not settle the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》 (2012), PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-803fd94611bb57f92df9\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early use and adjacent 少 entry\", \"text\": \"The database reports two analyses of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敘倫. It also describes 小 and 少 as an originally shared graph. Its examples include oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣. The page provides no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or usages, so it cannot alone establish a chronology among the senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"source\": \"金真姬,《常用漢字二百字探源》\", \"field\": \"小, 字形探源／字義探源, PDF pp. 20–21; printed pp. 26–27\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry reports disagreement about what the three dots represent, including raindrops, scattered objects or fine smallness. It favors an abstract indication of smallness and says later lengthened dots prompted Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 analysis, which it rejects as based on the altered form. It cites oracle-bone 小雨, 小風 and 小牢 as uses for ‘small.’ This supports a competing scholarly account, not a settled referent for the dots.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》, PDF pp. 20–21\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5ac63eb9bb056d4dc7cb\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; it says the lower dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions, distinguishing it from 小. This is an explicit shared-origin analysis, while other consulted sources retain competing analyses of 小’s dots.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b8ded469510aa9455741\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-cfcd778e20a67d7860ec\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小 formation, early uses and adjacent 少, 沙 and 雀 records\", \"text\": \"The database reports competing interpretations of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation, and that oracle-bone forms could use four dots for 小/少. It reports 小雨, 小食 (afternoon), 小采 (dusk), and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments. Its 沙 entry discusses several analyses of 沙 forms; thus the page does not establish that 沙 was borrowed phonetically from 小. Its 雀 entry says 小 contributes both ‘small bird’ and sound, while separately reporting varied early uses and graphic forms.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-4029d7e014bdb7910f42\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as tiny sand grains scattered around, expressing smallness, and says the middle dot in small-seal script became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks. This is one source’s interpretation, not independent proof of the depicted referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fe9ea9767e1da9fa626c\", \"source\": \"教育部《異體字字典》, Taiwan Ministry of Education\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary preserves Xu Shen’s historical account: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It lists modern senses including small, make smaller, brief, young, lower-ranked, humble self-reference, slight degree and despise, with citations, but does not establish a chronological order among them.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-9cae30a34adfac4e5d42\", \"source\": \"Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology”\", \"field\": \"少 and 小 sound relationship; discussion and reconstruction comparison, pp. 2–6, 16\", \"text\": \"The inspected study summarizes two positions on the proposed sound relationship around 少: Zhengzhang and Pan allow relationships among 少, 小 and related forms through reconstructed nasal initials; Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relationship, with Schuessler treating 小 as semantic rather than phonetic in the cited discussion. The study also documents that early spellings 少 and 小 can both represent the words ‘small’ and ‘few’ in some oracle-bone, bronze and Warring States materials, with orthographic practice differing across regions and periods. This supports caution about inferring a phonetic relation from shared early spelling or present form alone.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshangguyinnitsuite\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-142dfc94245bc8e052bc\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1\", \"field\": \"Search result for author-hosted reconstruction table; PDF could not be opened in this invocation\", \"text\": \"Search surfaced the authors’ version 1.1 table PDF, but the browser failed to open it. No specific 小, 少, 沙 or 雀 row was independently inspected in this invocation, so no table readings are asserted here.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"access_failure\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形 甲骨文 金文 三点 沙 小 少 字源\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK’s 小 record, Academia Sinica’s 少 evolution page, and the PDF entry for 小. These sources support competing interpretations of 小’s early marks and report a relationship between 小 and 少.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 少 甲骨文 同字 分化 說文 小 甲骨 用例 CUHK Character database\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CUHK page and checked its 小 formation and early-use account, the linked 少 discussion, and its 沙 and 雀 component notes.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形演變 甲骨文 少 sand grains smallness Academia Sinica; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少; 小 Shuowen oracle bone character origin dots sand grains scholarly\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the Sinica, CUHK and Taiwan Ministry of Education pages and inspected their character-specific content. They confirm competing accounts of the earliest marks, the historical Shuowen analysis, cited early uses and the 小–少 discussion. Wiktionary opened but was not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 少 沙 Baxter Sagart table; Old Chinese 小 少 沙 reconstruction Zhengzhang Baxter Sagart sound relationship; 小 少 原始字 甲骨文 字形 分化 郭沫若 商承祚 于省吾\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshanggitsunitesh?\", \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found Baxter and Sagart’s version 1.1 table, but opening it returned an internal error; no table row was verified. Opened Nohara’s study and inspected its discussion of competing phonetic analyses and early orthographic usage for 少 and 小. The Oxford page verified the book’s publication metadata only and was not used for character claims.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live scan inspection of supplied 字源 PDF pages 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected both supplied original scan images directly. PDF p. 76 contains the 小 headword; p. 77 continues its adjacent 少 discussion. Scan readings were checked for 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大 and 《粹》1004. No OCR error was identified in the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat page could not be opened; bibliographic details are from the supplied source registration, not the catalogue.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\", \"字源’s claim that 小’s marks depict sand grains and that 小 was an early graph of 沙 is this author’s analysis; consulted references also support an abstract smallness-sign account. The original depicted referent remains unsettled. No inspected source establishes a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on the name or pronunciation of depicted sand grains.\", \"The 字源 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 and other early meanings, but the underlying inscriptions were not inspected here; no absolute artifact dates or demonstrated semantic bridge for the time and status uses were established.\", \"字源 PDF page 76 is printed p. 64; its continuation is PDF page 77, printed p. 65. Both scan images were directly inspected. No specific OCR correction was identified in the cited 小 passage.\", \"The local reading row for 小 is current kMandarin metadata, not evidence about ancient sound relationships. The official individual Unicode Unihan record endpoint was not accessed in this research invocation.\", \"The CUHK page attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but this invocation did not independently establish a historical sound relationship using inspected component and host readings in the same reconstruction system. Keep that role source-attributed and unresolved.\", \"The earliest marks’ specific referent remains disputed: inspected sources present both abstract marks for smallness and fine sand grains. No inspected evidence establishes the sand object’s name or shows that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using that name.\", \"The book’s cited oracle-bone 小雨 example was not checked against an inscription facsimile; no absolute artifact date was verified. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"Accounts support some early graphic and orthographic overlap between 小 and 少, but the specific development and lexical relationship remain contested. Nohara’s study reports competing phonological analyses; its ResearchGate page was inspected, but the underlying cited Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart pages were not independently opened here.\", \"The official individual Unihan endpoint and the Baxter–Sagart table could not be inspected in this invocation. No current or reconstructed reading rows are newly asserted; local rows in the input remain local data and are not treated as external verification.\", \"The 《字源》 scan backs the author’s analysis but does not settle it. No specific OCR correction was identified for the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat catalogue URL could not be opened; publication metadata were supplied in the input.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its present form is three strokes: a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. Early marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, but their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"E6\", \"E7\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"小 has no settled account of what its early marks represent: analyses describe abstract signs of smallness, raindrops or scattered objects, or fine sand grains. The sand-grain account treats 小 as an early graph of 沙, but neither the depicted referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before they were differentiated; one account says 少’s lower dot became a diagonal stroke. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s analysis of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical interpretation, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"X-803fd94611bb57f92df9\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"X-5ac63eb9bb056d4dc7cb\"], \"type\": \"disputed\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"E4\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}, {\"id\": \"小~少:variant_of\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"variant_of\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before later differentiation; one account says 少’s lower mark became a diagonal stroke. This supports a qualified graphic relationship, not a direction in which one character derives from the other.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small” and is written with a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. The early marks are interpreted either as signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"E6\", \"E7\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; In the authored article, cite the retained book evidence ID on the specific supported claim; placing a new book record only in the dossier is not article enrichment. Use evidence_ids arrays, never inline labels. If the book corroborates existing prose, preserve that prose and add only the supported citation before fresh reviews. if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"additional_research_context\": {\"purpose\": \"Genuinely integrate consulted 字源 evidence into the explanation with fresh independent reviews. Previous research is verified but uncited.\", \"verified_uncited_book_records\": [{\"research_output\": \"initial-followup/research/result.json\", \"research_receipt_hash\": \"277f9ded3184345f610494a1b4e2fd365ebe24133b4b8fac1cbd612e9e03c4f4\", \"research_result_hash\": \"44a2e6be132e01ec5b0cd11730b3aedf1d129031e3b4fd7a7858bd3609dcacc0\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b8ded469510aa9455741\"]}], \"instruction\": \"Read the page record and original scan; attach its exact retained evidence ID to the specific supported article claim in evidence_ids metadata. If existing prose already accurately states that claim, unchanged prose plus the supporting citation is sufficient. Do not put evidence IDs or source names into explanatory prose. Do not assert original meaning or earliest priority unless the source establishes it. Other authoritative sources remain welcome; book citation requires genuine relevant support.\"}, \"additional_context_policy\": \"These source leads and prior findings are research tasks, not preverified dossier evidence. Consult the referenced sources directly, check exact graph identity and scope, and add cited evidence for useful claims before authorship. Recheck reviewer hypotheses; modern structure does not establish historical roles. Record inaccessible sources as gaps.\", \"unfinished_draft_provenance\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/book-citation-integration/ziyuan-2012/5C0F\", \"article_hash\": \"3830174141a77649fcd89fcff8d1f9b9bf6541522ddf76f9ffab2dfb233834af\", \"dossier_hash\": \"ccd0119c02d34950e094226f73a4647c9e621f79aac9300c200daebaeffdae68\", \"previous_state_hash\": \"e722e9b4b37889a4fced3f184416a6ae45d26181dc78824c45ffa3389f7e3878\", \"canonical_article_hash\": \"ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749\", \"canonical_dossier_hash\": \"a69304c4a3535766b281e20fb125b3ecc8231c64ed859ca694e6c69f0d62c3be\", \"requires_fresh_research_and_reviews\": true, \"prior_review_proposals_hash\": \"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\"}, \"prior_review_proposals\": [], \"continuation_policy\": \"This is an unapproved draft, not reusable approval. Recheck source-dependent claims against current source scans/corpus. Prior revise findings are hypotheses to recheck, including possible source identity errors; do not obey them merely because a previous verifier repeated them. Preserve prior supported work and obtain fresh factual/readability reviews.\", \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"review_existing_glyphs\": false, \"task\": \"Resolve the supplied evidence gaps before editing. Add accurate citable records for required claims; preserve existing supported content and source provenance.\", \"research_task\": \"Repair the exact research-data validation errors. Preserve valid evidence and actual search records. Correct a bad URL only after inspecting the real official HTTP(S) page; otherwise remove that evidence item and record the gap. Never turn a repository path into a URL.\", \"previous_research\": {\"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"field\": \"小 headword and adjacent 少 entry; PDF pp. 76–77, printed pp. 64–65\", \"text\": \"Directly inspected both source scans. The 小 headword calls 小 pictographic, interprets its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and as an early graph of 沙, and says it extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大; it reports that meaning in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt, and youth, and says the bronze form had become corrupted, prompting Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account. The adjacent 少 entry describes 小 and 少 as originally one graph used alike in oracle-bone texts and later differentiated. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies the author’s account, not the disputed sand interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early uses, 少, 沙 and 雀 records\", \"text\": \"The database presents two analyses for 小’s early marks: abstract dots signifying smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It reports shared early graphic use of 小 and 少, oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and later bronze examples. It says 小 in 雀 contributes both ‘small bird’ and sound. Its 沙 discussion gives competing readings of early forms, so it does not establish a sound borrowing of 沙 from 小. The cited examples have no absolute dates on this page.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"source\": \"Masaki Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology,” 中国語学 262 (2015), pp. 57–75\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese relationship between 少 and 小; discussion and reconstruction, especially pp. 57–75\", \"text\": \"Nohara reviews proposals linking 少 with 小 through phonetic-series connections and reconstructions involving a nasal onset, and counterarguments based on excavated texts and Min dialects. He argues for *st- for 少 and concludes that the evidence he examines does not support a phonetic relationship with 小. This cautions against deriving a sound relationship from shared early spelling or graphic affinity alone; it does not disprove the reported shared-graph account.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"source\": \"Repository supplied local primary reading rows\", \"field\": \"pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv, kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied local rows give 小 xiǎo, 少 shǎo and 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata, not evidence of ancient sound relationships. The required official individual Unihan pages for U+5C0F, U+5C11 and U+96C0 could not be opened in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unicode Unihan individual character lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 甲骨文 字形 三点 沙 小 少 字源 商承祚 于省吾 馬敘倫\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected CUHK’s 小 record and linked 少, 沙 and 雀 discussions. It reports competing analyses, examples of early uses and a source-attributed sound role in 雀.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 少 Old Chinese phonological relationship Nohara shao reconstruction\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Nohara’s study. Its conclusion supports caution about a phonetic relationship between 少 and 小 based on the evidence reviewed.\"}, {\"query\": \"Official Unihan individual-record endpoints for 小, 少 and 雀\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C11\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=96C0\"], \"outcome\": \"Attempted all three per-character pages; each was inaccessible through the web tool. Current readings are reported only from supplied local rows.\"}, {\"query\": \"Local 字源 corpus locator and source scans for 小\", \"urls\": [\"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\"], \"outcome\": \"Located the 小 headword in provisional OCR, then directly inspected both attached page images. The headword and continuation are readable; no OCR correction was identified in the cited passage. The book gives a sand-grain analysis, which remains disputed by the consulted references.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The marks’ original referent remains disputed: sources offer abstract signs of smallness or fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using the name of the depicted object.\", \"字源 reports oracle-bone 小雨 and later time and status-related uses, but no inscription facsimile or absolute artifact dates were checked here; the semantic bridges and chronology remain unresolved.\", \"The shared-graph account for 小 and 少 is reported by multiple sources, but the graphic and lexical relationship remains distinct from a demonstrated phonetic relationship. Nohara’s study argues against such a phonetic link on its examined evidence.\", \"CUHK attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but a historical sound relationship was not established here. Local reading rows alone do not demonstrate that role.\", \"The official individual Unihan lookup pages for 小, 少 and 雀 were inaccessible in this run; readings are reported only from supplied local rows.\", \"The WorldCat catalogue URL could not be opened; book edition metadata are from the supplied registration. Both attached scans were directly inspected, with PDF pages 76–77 corresponding to printed pages 64–65.\"]}, \"research_validation_findings\": [\"Search audit requires actual HTTP(S) URLs\"], \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}, \"attached_source_scans\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"attachment_index\": 1, \"sha256\": \"01ac480a495cc967c1affd418da7968d809784f57f0b0a82dbfdc21289a479b2\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"attachment_index\": 2, \"sha256\": \"70e48f562d95c0df6f03b3ac696c51ebffc7e338bd2c526d02b6e4de9b174c43\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}]}\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/initial-followup/research/prompt.txt:180:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X-874259a2b827e31d1455\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-e79ea1cfe5b5aa959a40\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6339f9c0072cd12d52a\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-f6f7d68f09a02c854d24\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2829f816c26a5d71ec70\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-253281f9589a65c2d2a6\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-dd1f049d3ded734931ba\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; scanned headword and continuation checked on PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The scanned entry identifies 小 as pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny grains of dust or sand, calling it an early graph of 沙; it says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and gives that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions. It cites 《粹》1004 for 小雨, then describes extensions to low status, contempt, and youth. It says the current form was corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, with similar use in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its explanation continues onto printed p. 65. Direct scan inspection confirms the cited headword and passage. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and the text’s citation 《粹》1004. The scan supports what this author asserts; it does not settle the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》 (2012), PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-803fd94611bb57f92df9\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early use and adjacent 少 entry\", \"text\": \"The database reports two analyses of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敘倫. It also describes 小 and 少 as an originally shared graph. Its examples include oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣. The page provides no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or usages, so it cannot alone establish a chronology among the senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"source\": \"金真姬,《常用漢字二百字探源》\", \"field\": \"小, 字形探源／字義探源, PDF pp. 20–21; printed pp. 26–27\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry reports disagreement about what the three dots represent, including raindrops, scattered objects or fine smallness. It favors an abstract indication of smallness and says later lengthened dots prompted Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 analysis, which it rejects as based on the altered form. It cites oracle-bone 小雨, 小風 and 小牢 as uses for ‘small.’ This supports a competing scholarly account, not a settled referent for the dots.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》, PDF pp. 20–21\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5ac63eb9bb056d4dc7cb\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; it says the lower dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions, distinguishing it from 小. This is an explicit shared-origin analysis, while other consulted sources retain competing analyses of 小’s dots.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b8ded469510aa9455741\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-cfcd778e20a67d7860ec\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小 formation, early uses and adjacent 少, 沙 and 雀 records\", \"text\": \"The database reports competing interpretations of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation, and that oracle-bone forms could use four dots for 小/少. It reports 小雨, 小食 (afternoon), 小采 (dusk), and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments. Its 沙 entry discusses several analyses of 沙 forms; thus the page does not establish that 沙 was borrowed phonetically from 小. Its 雀 entry says 小 contributes both ‘small bird’ and sound, while separately reporting varied early uses and graphic forms.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-4029d7e014bdb7910f42\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as tiny sand grains scattered around, expressing smallness, and says the middle dot in small-seal script became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks. This is one source’s interpretation, not independent proof of the depicted referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fe9ea9767e1da9fa626c\", \"source\": \"教育部《異體字字典》, Taiwan Ministry of Education\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary preserves Xu Shen’s historical account: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It lists modern senses including small, make smaller, brief, young, lower-ranked, humble self-reference, slight degree and despise, with citations, but does not establish a chronological order among them.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-9cae30a34adfac4e5d42\", \"source\": \"Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology”\", \"field\": \"少 and 小 sound relationship; discussion and reconstruction comparison, pp. 2–6, 16\", \"text\": \"The inspected study summarizes two positions on the proposed sound relationship around 少: Zhengzhang and Pan allow relationships among 少, 小 and related forms through reconstructed nasal initials; Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relationship, with Schuessler treating 小 as semantic rather than phonetic in the cited discussion. The study also documents that early spellings 少 and 小 can both represent the words ‘small’ and ‘few’ in some oracle-bone, bronze and Warring States materials, with orthographic practice differing across regions and periods. This supports caution about inferring a phonetic relation from shared early spelling or present form alone.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshangguyinnitsuite\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"X-142dfc94245bc8e052bc\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1\", \"field\": \"Search result for author-hosted reconstruction table; PDF could not be opened in this invocation\", \"text\": \"Search surfaced the authors’ version 1.1 table PDF, but the browser failed to open it. No specific 小, 少, 沙 or 雀 row was independently inspected in this invocation, so no table readings are asserted here.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"access_failure\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形 甲骨文 金文 三点 沙 小 少 字源\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK’s 小 record, Academia Sinica’s 少 evolution page, and the PDF entry for 小. These sources support competing interpretations of 小’s early marks and report a relationship between 小 and 少.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 少 甲骨文 同字 分化 說文 小 甲骨 用例 CUHK Character database\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CUHK page and checked its 小 formation and early-use account, the linked 少 discussion, and its 沙 and 雀 component notes.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形演變 甲骨文 少 sand grains smallness Academia Sinica; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少; 小 Shuowen oracle bone character origin dots sand grains scholarly\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the Sinica, CUHK and Taiwan Ministry of Education pages and inspected their character-specific content. They confirm competing accounts of the earliest marks, the historical Shuowen analysis, cited early uses and the 小–少 discussion. Wiktionary opened but was not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 少 沙 Baxter Sagart table; Old Chinese 小 少 沙 reconstruction Zhengzhang Baxter Sagart sound relationship; 小 少 原始字 甲骨文 字形 分化 郭沫若 商承祚 于省吾\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshanggitsunitesh?\", \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found Baxter and Sagart’s version 1.1 table, but opening it returned an internal error; no table row was verified. Opened Nohara’s study and inspected its discussion of competing phonetic analyses and early orthographic usage for 少 and 小. The Oxford page verified the book’s publication metadata only and was not used for character claims.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live scan inspection of supplied 字源 PDF pages 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected both supplied original scan images directly. PDF p. 76 contains the 小 headword; p. 77 continues its adjacent 少 discussion. Scan readings were checked for 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大 and 《粹》1004. No OCR error was identified in the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat page could not be opened; bibliographic details are from the supplied source registration, not the catalogue.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\", \"字源’s claim that 小’s marks depict sand grains and that 小 was an early graph of 沙 is this author’s analysis; consulted references also support an abstract smallness-sign account. The original depicted referent remains unsettled. No inspected source establishes a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on the name or pronunciation of depicted sand grains.\", \"The 字源 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 and other early meanings, but the underlying inscriptions were not inspected here; no absolute artifact dates or demonstrated semantic bridge for the time and status uses were established.\", \"字源 PDF page 76 is printed p. 64; its continuation is PDF page 77, printed p. 65. Both scan images were directly inspected. No specific OCR correction was identified in the cited 小 passage.\", \"The local reading row for 小 is current kMandarin metadata, not evidence about ancient sound relationships. The official individual Unicode Unihan record endpoint was not accessed in this research invocation.\", \"The CUHK page attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but this invocation did not independently establish a historical sound relationship using inspected component and host readings in the same reconstruction system. Keep that role source-attributed and unresolved.\", \"The earliest marks’ specific referent remains disputed: inspected sources present both abstract marks for smallness and fine sand grains. No inspected evidence establishes the sand object’s name or shows that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using that name.\", \"The book’s cited oracle-bone 小雨 example was not checked against an inscription facsimile; no absolute artifact date was verified. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"Accounts support some early graphic and orthographic overlap between 小 and 少, but the specific development and lexical relationship remain contested. Nohara’s study reports competing phonological analyses; its ResearchGate page was inspected, but the underlying cited Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart pages were not independently opened here.\", \"The official individual Unihan endpoint and the Baxter–Sagart table could not be inspected in this invocation. No current or reconstructed reading rows are newly asserted; local rows in the input remain local data and are not treated as external verification.\", \"The 《字源》 scan backs the author’s analysis but does not settle it. No specific OCR correction was identified for the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat catalogue URL could not be opened; publication metadata were supplied in the input.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its present form is three strokes: a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. Early marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, but their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"E6\", \"E7\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"小 has no settled account of what its early marks represent: analyses describe abstract signs of smallness, raindrops or scattered objects, or fine sand grains. The sand-grain account treats 小 as an early graph of 沙, but neither the depicted referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before they were differentiated; one account says 少’s lower dot became a diagonal stroke. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s analysis of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical interpretation, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"X-803fd94611bb57f92df9\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"X-5ac63eb9bb056d4dc7cb\"], \"type\": \"disputed\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"E4\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}, {\"id\": \"小~少:variant_of\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"variant_of\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before later differentiation; one account says 少’s lower mark became a diagonal stroke. This supports a qualified graphic relationship, not a direction in which one character derives from the other.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small” and is written with a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. The early marks are interpreted either as signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"X-5385acd88b51f476d32e\", \"E6\", \"E7\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; In the authored article, cite the retained book evidence ID on the specific supported claim; placing a new book record only in the dossier is not article enrichment. Use evidence_ids arrays, never inline labels. If the book corroborates existing prose, preserve that prose and add only the supported citation before fresh reviews. if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"additional_research_context\": {\"purpose\": \"Genuinely integrate consulted 字源 evidence into the explanation with fresh independent reviews. Previous research is verified but uncited.\", \"verified_uncited_book_records\": [{\"research_output\": \"initial-followup/research/result.json\", \"research_receipt_hash\": \"277f9ded3184345f610494a1b4e2fd365ebe24133b4b8fac1cbd612e9e03c4f4\", \"research_result_hash\": \"44a2e6be132e01ec5b0cd11730b3aedf1d129031e3b4fd7a7858bd3609dcacc0\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b8ded469510aa9455741\"]}], \"instruction\": \"Read the page record and original scan; attach its exact retained evidence ID to the specific supported article claim in evidence_ids metadata. If existing prose already accurately states that claim, unchanged prose plus the supporting citation is sufficient. Do not put evidence IDs or source names into explanatory prose. Do not assert original meaning or earliest priority unless the source establishes it. Other authoritative sources remain welcome; book citation requires genuine relevant support.\"}, \"additional_context_policy\": \"These source leads and prior findings are research tasks, not preverified dossier evidence. Consult the referenced sources directly, check exact graph identity and scope, and add cited evidence for useful claims before authorship. Recheck reviewer hypotheses; modern structure does not establish historical roles. Record inaccessible sources as gaps.\", \"unfinished_draft_provenance\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/book-citation-integration/ziyuan-2012/5C0F\", \"article_hash\": \"3830174141a77649fcd89fcff8d1f9b9bf6541522ddf76f9ffab2dfb233834af\", \"dossier_hash\": \"ccd0119c02d34950e094226f73a4647c9e621f79aac9300c200daebaeffdae68\", \"previous_state_hash\": \"e722e9b4b37889a4fced3f184416a6ae45d26181dc78824c45ffa3389f7e3878\", \"canonical_article_hash\": \"ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749\", \"canonical_dossier_hash\": \"a69304c4a3535766b281e20fb125b3ecc8231c64ed859ca694e6c69f0d62c3be\", \"requires_fresh_research_and_reviews\": true, \"prior_review_proposals_hash\": \"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\"}, \"prior_review_proposals\": [], \"continuation_policy\": \"This is an unapproved draft, not reusable approval. Recheck source-dependent claims against current source scans/corpus. Prior revise findings are hypotheses to recheck, including possible source identity errors; do not obey them merely because a previous verifier repeated them. Preserve prior supported work and obtain fresh factual/readability reviews.\", \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"review_existing_glyphs\": false, \"task\": \"Resolve the supplied evidence gaps before editing. Add accurate citable records for required claims; preserve existing supported content and source provenance.\", \"research_task\": \"Resolve these specific review findings. Keep supported evidence and glyph selections unless correction is needed. Inspect additional local or external sources as needed; revise glyph captions/selection when findings require it. Report remaining gaps honestly.\", \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}, \"attached_source_scans\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"attachment_index\": 1, \"sha256\": \"01ac480a495cc967c1affd418da7968d809784f57f0b0a82dbfdc21289a479b2\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"attachment_index\": 2, \"sha256\": \"70e48f562d95c0df6f03b3ac696c51ebffc7e338bd2c526d02b6e4de9b174c43\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}]}\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/source_feedback.json:42:          \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/round-0/editor/patch-repair-1/prompt.txt:298:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; scanned headword and continuation checked on PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The scanned entry identifies 小 as pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny grains of dust or sand, calling it an early graph of 沙; it says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and gives that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions. It cites 《粹》1004 for 小雨, then describes extensions to low status, contempt, and youth. It says the current form was corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, with similar use in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its explanation continues onto printed p. 65. Direct scan inspection confirms the cited headword and passage. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and the text’s citation 《粹》1004. The scan supports what this author asserts; it does not settle the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》 (2012), PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early use and adjacent 少 entry\", \"text\": \"The database reports two analyses of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敘倫. It also describes 小 and 少 as an originally shared graph. Its examples include oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣. The page provides no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or usages, so it cannot alone establish a chronology among the senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"金真姬,《常用漢字二百字探源》\", \"field\": \"小, 字形探源／字義探源, PDF pp. 20–21; printed pp. 26–27\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry reports disagreement about what the three dots represent, including raindrops, scattered objects or fine smallness. It favors an abstract indication of smallness and says later lengthened dots prompted Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 analysis, which it rejects as based on the altered form. It cites oracle-bone 小雨, 小風 and 小牢 as uses for ‘small.’ This supports a competing scholarly account, not a settled referent for the dots.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》, PDF pp. 20–21\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; it says the lower dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions, distinguishing it from 小. This is an explicit shared-origin analysis, while other consulted sources retain competing analyses of 小’s dots.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小 formation, early uses and adjacent 少, 沙 and 雀 records\", \"text\": \"The database reports competing interpretations of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation, and that oracle-bone forms could use four dots for 小/少. It reports 小雨, 小食 (afternoon), 小采 (dusk), and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments. Its 沙 entry discusses several analyses of 沙 forms; thus the page does not establish that 沙 was borrowed phonetically from 小. Its 雀 entry says 小 contributes both ‘small bird’ and sound, while separately reporting varied early uses and graphic forms.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as tiny sand grains scattered around, expressing smallness, and says the middle dot in small-seal script became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks. This is one source’s interpretation, not independent proof of the depicted referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref038\", \"source\": \"教育部《異體字字典》, Taiwan Ministry of Education\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary preserves Xu Shen’s historical account: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It lists modern senses including small, make smaller, brief, young, lower-ranked, humble self-reference, slight degree and despise, with citations, but does not establish a chronological order among them.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref039\", \"source\": \"Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology”\", \"field\": \"少 and 小 sound relationship; discussion and reconstruction comparison, pp. 2–6, 16\", \"text\": \"The inspected study summarizes two positions on the proposed sound relationship around 少: Zhengzhang and Pan allow relationships among 少, 小 and related forms through reconstructed nasal initials; Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relationship, with Schuessler treating 小 as semantic rather than phonetic in the cited discussion. The study also documents that early spellings 少 and 小 can both represent the words ‘small’ and ‘few’ in some oracle-bone, bronze and Warring States materials, with orthographic practice differing across regions and periods. This supports caution about inferring a phonetic relation from shared early spelling or present form alone.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshangguyinnitsuite\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref040\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1\", \"field\": \"Search result for author-hosted reconstruction table; PDF could not be opened in this invocation\", \"text\": \"Search surfaced the authors’ version 1.1 table PDF, but the browser failed to open it. No specific 小, 少, 沙 or 雀 row was independently inspected in this invocation, so no table readings are asserted here.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"access_failure\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref041\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64\", \"text\": \"The entry calls 小 pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny dust or sand particles, an early graph of 沙. It says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and cites oracle-bone and bronze uses, including 《粹》1004 小雨. It also describes extensions to low status, contempt and youth, and says the bronze form had become corrupted, prompting Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 explanation. Both attached page images were inspected; the printed headword, cited forms 小, 沙, 大, 八, 丨 and 《粹》1004 are legible. This verifies the author’s account, not the disputed sand interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 2012, PDF p. 76; printed p. 64\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref042\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"field\": \"Adjacent 少 entry; PDF p. 76 and continuation p. 77, printed pp. 64–65\", \"text\": \"The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and were used alike in oracle-bone texts, then differentiated; it describes 少’s final diagonal stroke as a distinguishing mark. The entry continues onto printed p. 65. Both source scans were inspected; the relevant 少 and 小 forms are legible. This is the book author’s graphic account, not proof of a historical sound relationship.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref043\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小, 少 and 沙 records: formation, cited uses and graph components\", \"text\": \"The 小 record presents two analyses of its early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It reports 小雨, 小食 as an afternoon expression, 小采 as a dusk expression, and bronze uses including 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute artifact dates. Its 少 record says early forms could overlap and that three-dot and four-dot spellings later distinguished 小 and 少. The 沙 account reports competing analyses of bronze uses, so the cited pages do not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref044\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database asserts that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning, and says the lowest dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions. This supports that interpretation as a source-attributed account; CUHK presents competing analyses of 小’s marks.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref045\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as scattered fine sand grains expressing smallness and says the middle dot became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks in small-seal script. This is the database’s interpretation, not independent proof of the marks’ referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref046\", \"source\": \"Masaki Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology,” 中国語学 262 (2015), pp. 57–75\", \"field\": \"少–小 phonetic relationship and early orthographic usage, especially PDF pp. 59–65\", \"text\": \"Nohara reviews competing Old Chinese analyses. Zhengzhang and Pan allow links among 少, 小 and related forms via proposed nasal initials, while Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relation in the discussion Nohara cites. Nohara argues that excavated-text spelling and sound evidence do not warrant treating the early graphic overlap as proof of a phonetic relationship. The paper also documents that 少 and 小 spellings could represent both ‘few’ and ‘small’ in some early materials, with regional and period differences.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形 甲骨文 金文 三点 沙 小 少 字源\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK’s 小 record, Academia Sinica’s 少 evolution page, and the PDF entry for 小. These sources support competing interpretations of 小’s early marks and report a relationship between 小 and 少.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 少 甲骨文 同字 分化 說文 小 甲骨 用例 CUHK Character database\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CUHK page and checked its 小 formation and early-use account, the linked 少 discussion, and its 沙 and 雀 component notes.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形演變 甲骨文 少 sand grains smallness Academia Sinica; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少; 小 Shuowen oracle bone character origin dots sand grains scholarly\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the Sinica, CUHK and Taiwan Ministry of Education pages and inspected their character-specific content. They confirm competing accounts of the earliest marks, the historical Shuowen analysis, cited early uses and the 小–少 discussion. Wiktionary opened but was not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 少 沙 Baxter Sagart table; Old Chinese 小 少 沙 reconstruction Zhengzhang Baxter Sagart sound relationship; 小 少 原始字 甲骨文 字形 分化 郭沫若 商承祚 于省吾\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshanggitsunitesh?\", \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found Baxter and Sagart’s version 1.1 table, but opening it returned an internal error; no table row was verified. Opened Nohara’s study and inspected its discussion of competing phonetic analyses and early orthographic usage for 少 and 小. The Oxford page verified the book’s publication metadata only and was not used for character claims.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live scan inspection of supplied 字源 PDF pages 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected both supplied original scan images directly. PDF p. 76 contains the 小 headword; p. 77 continues its adjacent 少 discussion. Scan readings were checked for 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大 and 《粹》1004. No OCR error was identified in the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat page could not be opened; bibliographic details are from the supplied source registration, not the catalogue.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character etymology academic paleography three dots sand grains 沙 小 少; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 少 甲骨文 沙 字源; 小 少 Old Chinese phonological relationship Nohara\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%91\", \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_article/-char/ja\", \"https://waseda.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/64927/files/ChugokuBungakuKenkyu_44_14.pdf\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found the CUHK character records, Academia Sinica evolution pages and Nohara’s study. Opened and inspected the CUHK 小 and 少 records, both Sinica pages, and the J-STAGE PDF. The J-STAGE article landing page failed to open, but the PDF opened and its discussion was inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 小 字形演變 少 字源 沙 商承祚 于省吾 馬敘倫\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.hiemalis.org/~acy/mnc/2679131gc1j.html\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/ancient.php?start=920\", \"https://www.ch.ntnu.edu.tw/SIS/show_paper_detail.php?issue_id=42&paper_id=378&periodicalsDir=DESC&periodicalsOrder=Sorter_issue_ch&periodicalsPage=7\", \"https://www.chnmuseum.cn/portals/0/web/zt/20191022jiagu/\", \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"A targeted follow-up surfaced the same CUHK and Sinica claims plus general bibliographic/context pages. The character-specific pages and J-STAGE PDF were inspected; the other results were not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live inspection of supplied 字源 scans, PDF pp. 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the supplied original-resolution scan images for PDF pages 76 and 77 and inspected targeted crops. Confirmed the 小 headword and passage on page 76 and the adjacent 少 continuation context on page 77. Checked the printed forms 小, 少, 沙, 大, 八, 丨 and citation 《粹》1004 against the pixels. No OCR correction was identified in the cited passage. The WorldCat URL was not opened; bibliographic metadata came from the supplied source registration.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\", \"字源’s claim that 小’s marks depict sand grains and that 小 was an early graph of 沙 is this author’s analysis; consulted references also support an abstract smallness-sign account. The original depicted referent remains unsettled. No inspected source establishes a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on the name or pronunciation of depicted sand grains.\", \"The 字源 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 and other early meanings, but the underlying inscriptions were not inspected here; no absolute artifact dates or demonstrated semantic bridge for the time and status uses were established.\", \"字源 PDF page 76 is printed p. 64; its continuation is PDF page 77, printed p. 65. Both scan images were directly inspected. No specific OCR correction was identified in the cited 小 passage.\", \"The local reading row for 小 is current kMandarin metadata, not evidence about ancient sound relationships. The official individual Unicode Unihan record endpoint was not accessed in this research invocation.\", \"The CUHK page attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but this invocation did not independently establish a historical sound relationship using inspected component and host readings in the same reconstruction system. Keep that role source-attributed and unresolved.\", \"The earliest marks’ specific referent remains disputed: inspected sources present both abstract marks for smallness and fine sand grains. No inspected evidence establishes the sand object’s name or shows that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using that name.\", \"The book’s cited oracle-bone 小雨 example was not checked against an inscription facsimile; no absolute artifact date was verified. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"Accounts support some early graphic and orthographic overlap between 小 and 少, but the specific development and lexical relationship remain contested. Nohara’s study reports competing phonological analyses; its ResearchGate page was inspected, but the underlying cited Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart pages were not independently opened here.\", \"The official individual Unihan endpoint and the Baxter–Sagart table could not be inspected in this invocation. No current or reconstructed reading rows are newly asserted; local rows in the input remain local data and are not treated as external verification.\", \"The 《字源》 scan backs the author’s analysis but does not settle it. No specific OCR correction was identified for the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat catalogue URL could not be opened; publication metadata were supplied in the input.\", \"The earliest marks’ referent remains disputed: consulted accounts describe abstract marks for smallness or fine sand grains. No inspected evidence independently establishes the depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on its name.\", \"The 《字源》 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 with 《粹》1004, but no inscription facsimile or absolute artifact date was checked. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"The shared-graph and later differentiation account for 小 and 少 is reported by several references, but it does not itself establish a historical sound relationship. Nohara’s inspected study finds the phonetic connection unsupported by the evidence he examines; the broader lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. This research did not establish that role through an independent historical sound comparison, so it remains source-attributed.\", \"The WorldCat catalogue URL was not inspected. Edition details are from the supplied source registration; the source scans themselves were directly inspected.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its present form has a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. Early marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\", \"ref033\", \"ref035\", \"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"小 has no settled account of what its early marks represent: analyses describe abstract signs of smallness, raindrops or scattered objects, or fine sand grains. The sand-grain account treats 小 as an early graph of 沙, but neither the depicted referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before they were differentiated; one account says 少’s lower dot became a diagonal stroke. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s analysis of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical interpretation, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\", \"ref033\", \"ref034\", \"ref035\", \"ref042\"]}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref018\", \"ref025\", \"ref026\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref004\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref018\", \"ref019\", \"ref035\", \"ref042\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\", \"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref019\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}, {\"id\": \"小~少:variant_of\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"variant_of\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before later differentiation; one account says 少’s lower mark became a diagonal stroke. This supports a qualified graphic relationship, not a direction in which one character derives from the other.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref035\", \"ref042\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small” and is written with a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. The early marks are interpreted either as signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\", \"ref033\", \"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; In the authored article, cite the retained book evidence ID on the specific supported claim; placing a new book record only in the dossier is not article enrichment. Use evidence_ids arrays, never inline labels. If the book corroborates existing prose, preserve that prose and add only the supported citation before fresh reviews. if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"additional_research_context\": {\"purpose\": \"Genuinely integrate consulted 字源 evidence into the explanation with fresh independent reviews. Previous research is verified but uncited.\", \"verified_uncited_book_records\": [{\"research_output\": \"initial-followup/research/result.json\", \"research_receipt_hash\": \"277f9ded3184345f610494a1b4e2fd365ebe24133b4b8fac1cbd612e9e03c4f4\", \"research_result_hash\": \"44a2e6be132e01ec5b0cd11730b3aedf1d129031e3b4fd7a7858bd3609dcacc0\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\"]}], \"instruction\": \"Read the page record and original scan; attach its exact retained evidence ID to the specific supported article claim in evidence_ids metadata. If existing prose already accurately states that claim, unchanged prose plus the supporting citation is sufficient. Do not put evidence IDs or source names into explanatory prose. Do not assert original meaning or earliest priority unless the source establishes it. Other authoritative sources remain welcome; book citation requires genuine relevant support.\"}, \"additional_context_policy\": \"These source leads and prior findings are research tasks, not preverified dossier evidence. Consult the referenced sources directly, check exact graph identity and scope, and add cited evidence for useful claims before authorship. Recheck reviewer hypotheses; modern structure does not establish historical roles. Record inaccessible sources as gaps.\", \"unfinished_draft_provenance\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/book-citation-integration/ziyuan-2012/5C0F\", \"article_hash\": \"3830174141a77649fcd89fcff8d1f9b9bf6541522ddf76f9ffab2dfb233834af\", \"dossier_hash\": \"ccd0119c02d34950e094226f73a4647c9e621f79aac9300c200daebaeffdae68\", \"previous_state_hash\": \"e722e9b4b37889a4fced3f184416a6ae45d26181dc78824c45ffa3389f7e3878\", \"canonical_article_hash\": \"ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749\", \"canonical_dossier_hash\": \"a69304c4a3535766b281e20fb125b3ecc8231c64ed859ca694e6c69f0d62c3be\", \"requires_fresh_research_and_reviews\": true, \"prior_review_proposals_hash\": \"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\"}, \"prior_review_proposals\": [], \"continuation_policy\": \"This is an unapproved draft, not reusable approval. Recheck source-dependent claims against current source scans/corpus. Prior revise findings are hypotheses to recheck, including possible source identity errors; do not obey them merely because a previous verifier repeated them. Preserve prior supported work and obtain fresh factual/readability reviews.\", \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"task\": \"Repair every listed validation finding in the supplied current candidate, retaining valid previous edits. If a learner paragraph exceeds its hard word limit, shorten that paragraph directly: keep the main meaning and essential caveat, move lower-priority detail to the expert account, and aim below 40 words for the overview, below 25 words for a component card, or below 35 words for a takeaway. For sound_limitation, use null or a complete cited object with text and evidence_ids, never a bare string. To remove an array item, replace the entire array with its remaining valid items; never set an item to null. Select distinct nonoverlapping paths with valid JSON replacement values; preserve unaffected fields.\", \"original_role\": \"editor\", \"article_contract\": {\"relationship_branches\": [{\"predicates\": [\"semantic_component_of\", \"phonetic_component_of\", \"pictorial_component_of\", \"indicator_component_of\", \"replacement_component_of\", \"empty_component_of\"], \"subject_kind\": \"component\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"variant_of\", \"simplified_from\", \"derived_from\", \"phonetic_element_in\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"has_sense\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"sense_developed_into\", \"phonetic_loan_for\"], \"subject_kind\": \"sense\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}], \"instruction\": \"These are the exact allowed relationship names and endpoint kinds. _component_of is a suffix, never a literal predicate. Unknown component roles create no invented unknown_component_of edge. Preserve scoped historical claims and supported graph links.\", \"citation_instruction\": \"Reader prose must contain no refNNN transport aliases or inline evidence ID lists. Store citations only in the containing record evidence_ids array. When editing only a text field, preserve its existing evidence_ids; if support changes, edit that array as a separate nonoverlapping path. Never append [ref001, ref002] to prose.\"}, \"previous_patch\": {\"edits\": [{\"path\": \"summary\", \"value_json\": \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"小 means “small.” Its present form has a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. Early marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\\\",\\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"ref013\\\",\\\"ref014\\\",\\\"ref018\\\",\\\"ref026\\\",\\\"ref033\\\",\\\"ref035\\\",\\\"ref006\\\",\\\"ref007\\\"]}\"}, {\"path\": \"formation\", \"value_json\": \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"小 has no settled account of what its early marks represent: analyses describe abstract signs of smallness, raindrops or scattered objects, or fine sand grains. The sand-grain account treats 小 as an early graph of 沙, but neither the depicted referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before they were differentiated; one account says 少’s lower dot became a diagonal stroke. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s analysis of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical interpretation, not evidence of the original construction.\\\",\\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"ref003\\\",\\\"ref013\\\",\\\"ref014\\\",\\\"ref016\\\",\\\"ref017\\\",\\\"ref018\\\",\\\"ref020\\\",\\\"ref024\\\",\\\"ref025\\\",\\\"ref032\\\",\\\"ref033\\\",\\\"ref034\\\",\\\"ref035\\\",\\\"ref042\\\"]}\"}, {\"path\": \"history/1\", \"value_json\": \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\\\",\\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"ref003\\\",\\\"ref004\\\",\\\"ref016\\\",\\\"ref017\\\",\\\"ref018\\\",\\\"ref019\\\",\\\"ref035\\\",\\\"ref042\\\"]}\"}, {\"path\": \"meaning_history/senses/0/status\", \"value_json\": \"\\\"earliest_attested\\\"\"}, {\"path\": \"meaning_history/senses/0/period\", \"value_json\": \"\\\"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\\\"\"}, {\"path\": \"meaning_history/senses/0/evidence_ids\", \"value_json\": \"[\\\"ref015\\\",\\\"ref018\\\",\\\"ref024\\\",\\\"ref035\\\"]\"}, {\"path\": \"relationships/1\", \"value_json\": \"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"小~少:variant_of\\\",\\\"subject\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"character\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"小\\\"},\\\"object\\\":{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"character\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"少\\\"},\\\"predicate\\\":\\\"variant_of\\\",\\\"context_character\\\":\\\"小\\\",\\\"certainty\\\":\\\"probable\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before later differentiation; one account says 少’s lower mark became a diagonal stroke. This supports a qualified graphic relationship, not a direction in which one character derives from the other.\\\",\\\"evidence_ids\\\":[\\\"ref014\\\",\\\"ref016\\\",\\\"ref017\\\",\\\"ref035\\\",\\\"ref042\\\"]}\"}]}, \"validation_findings\": [\"$.formation: 'type' is a required property\"], \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/5c727c033b6385ffc7d9e54131b8d8a906e83d2f64155b55a91de3865c66e513/round-0/editor/prompt.txt:298:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; scanned headword and continuation checked on PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The scanned entry identifies 小 as pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny grains of dust or sand, calling it an early graph of 沙; it says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and gives that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions. It cites 《粹》1004 for 小雨, then describes extensions to low status, contempt, and youth. It says the current form was corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, with similar use in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its explanation continues onto printed p. 65. Direct scan inspection confirms the cited headword and passage. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and the text’s citation 《粹》1004. The scan supports what this author asserts; it does not settle the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》 (2012), PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early use and adjacent 少 entry\", \"text\": \"The database reports two analyses of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敘倫. It also describes 小 and 少 as an originally shared graph. Its examples include oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣. The page provides no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or usages, so it cannot alone establish a chronology among the senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"金真姬,《常用漢字二百字探源》\", \"field\": \"小, 字形探源／字義探源, PDF pp. 20–21; printed pp. 26–27\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry reports disagreement about what the three dots represent, including raindrops, scattered objects or fine smallness. It favors an abstract indication of smallness and says later lengthened dots prompted Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 analysis, which it rejects as based on the altered form. It cites oracle-bone 小雨, 小風 and 小牢 as uses for ‘small.’ This supports a competing scholarly account, not a settled referent for the dots.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》, PDF pp. 20–21\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; it says the lower dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions, distinguishing it from 小. This is an explicit shared-origin analysis, while other consulted sources retain competing analyses of 小’s dots.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小 formation, early uses and adjacent 少, 沙 and 雀 records\", \"text\": \"The database reports competing interpretations of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation, and that oracle-bone forms could use four dots for 小/少. It reports 小雨, 小食 (afternoon), 小采 (dusk), and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments. Its 沙 entry discusses several analyses of 沙 forms; thus the page does not establish that 沙 was borrowed phonetically from 小. Its 雀 entry says 小 contributes both ‘small bird’ and sound, while separately reporting varied early uses and graphic forms.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as tiny sand grains scattered around, expressing smallness, and says the middle dot in small-seal script became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks. This is one source’s interpretation, not independent proof of the depicted referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref038\", \"source\": \"教育部《異體字字典》, Taiwan Ministry of Education\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary preserves Xu Shen’s historical account: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It lists modern senses including small, make smaller, brief, young, lower-ranked, humble self-reference, slight degree and despise, with citations, but does not establish a chronological order among them.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref039\", \"source\": \"Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology”\", \"field\": \"少 and 小 sound relationship; discussion and reconstruction comparison, pp. 2–6, 16\", \"text\": \"The inspected study summarizes two positions on the proposed sound relationship around 少: Zhengzhang and Pan allow relationships among 少, 小 and related forms through reconstructed nasal initials; Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relationship, with Schuessler treating 小 as semantic rather than phonetic in the cited discussion. The study also documents that early spellings 少 and 小 can both represent the words ‘small’ and ‘few’ in some oracle-bone, bronze and Warring States materials, with orthographic practice differing across regions and periods. This supports caution about inferring a phonetic relation from shared early spelling or present form alone.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshangguyinnitsuite\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref040\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1\", \"field\": \"Search result for author-hosted reconstruction table; PDF could not be opened in this invocation\", \"text\": \"Search surfaced the authors’ version 1.1 table PDF, but the browser failed to open it. No specific 小, 少, 沙 or 雀 row was independently inspected in this invocation, so no table readings are asserted here.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙; 雀\", \"kind\": \"access_failure\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref041\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64\", \"text\": \"The entry calls 小 pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny dust or sand particles, an early graph of 沙. It says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and cites oracle-bone and bronze uses, including 《粹》1004 小雨. It also describes extensions to low status, contempt and youth, and says the bronze form had become corrupted, prompting Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 explanation. Both attached page images were inspected; the printed headword, cited forms 小, 沙, 大, 八, 丨 and 《粹》1004 are legible. This verifies the author’s account, not the disputed sand interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 2012, PDF p. 76; printed p. 64\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref042\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"field\": \"Adjacent 少 entry; PDF p. 76 and continuation p. 77, printed pp. 64–65\", \"text\": \"The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and were used alike in oracle-bone texts, then differentiated; it describes 少’s final diagonal stroke as a distinguishing mark. The entry continues onto printed p. 65. Both source scans were inspected; the relevant 少 and 小 forms are legible. This is the book author’s graphic account, not proof of a historical sound relationship.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》, 李學勤主編, 2012, PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref043\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小, 少 and 沙 records: formation, cited uses and graph components\", \"text\": \"The 小 record presents two analyses of its early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand grains, attributed to 馬敍倫. It reports 小雨, 小食 as an afternoon expression, 小采 as a dusk expression, and bronze uses including 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣, without absolute artifact dates. Its 少 record says early forms could overlap and that three-dot and four-dot spellings later distinguished 小 and 少. The 沙 account reports competing analyses of bronze uses, so the cited pages do not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 沙\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref044\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database asserts that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning, and says the lowest dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions. This supports that interpretation as a source-attributed account; CUHK presents competing analyses of 小’s marks.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref045\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen forms. It interprets the earlier marks as scattered fine sand grains expressing smallness and says the middle dot became a vertical stroke separating the two side marks in small-seal script. This is the database’s interpretation, not independent proof of the marks’ referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref046\", \"source\": \"Masaki Nohara, “The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology,” 中国語学 262 (2015), pp. 57–75\", \"field\": \"少–小 phonetic relationship and early orthographic usage, especially PDF pp. 59–65\", \"text\": \"Nohara reviews competing Old Chinese analyses. Zhengzhang and Pan allow links among 少, 小 and related forms via proposed nasal initials, while Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart reject a 少–小 phonetic-series relation in the discussion Nohara cites. Nohara argues that excavated-text spelling and sound evidence do not warrant treating the early graphic overlap as proof of a phonetic relationship. The paper also documents that 少 and 小 spellings could represent both ‘few’ and ‘small’ in some early materials, with regional and period differences.\", \"record_character\": \"少; 小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_study\", \"url\": \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\", \"title\": \"「少」の上古音について／The reconstruction of the word shao (少) in Old Chinese Phonology\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 