earliest marks, the historical Shuowen analysis, cited early uses and the 小–少 discussion. Wiktionary opened but was not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 少 沙 Baxter Sagart table; Old Chinese 小 少 沙 reconstruction Zhengzhang Baxter Sagart sound relationship; 小 少 原始字 甲骨文 字形 分化 郭沫若 商承祚 于省吾\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/339973841_The_reconstruction_of_the_word_shao_shao_in_Old_Chinese_Phonology_shaonoshanggitsunitesh?\", \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyMandarinMC2014-09-20.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found Baxter and Sagart’s version 1.1 table, but opening it returned an internal error; no table row was verified. Opened Nohara’s study and inspected its discussion of competing phonetic analyses and early orthographic usage for 少 and 小. The Oxford page verified the book’s publication metadata only and was not used for character claims.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live scan inspection of supplied 字源 PDF pages 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected both supplied original scan images directly. PDF p. 76 contains the 小 headword; p. 77 continues its adjacent 少 discussion. Scan readings were checked for 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大 and 《粹》1004. No OCR error was identified in the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat page could not be opened; bibliographic details are from the supplied source registration, not the catalogue.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character etymology academic paleography three dots sand grains 沙 小 少; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 少 甲骨文 沙 字源; 小 少 Old Chinese phonological relationship Nohara\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%91\", \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_article/-char/ja\", \"https://waseda.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/64927/files/ChugokuBungakuKenkyu_44_14.pdf\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found the CUHK character records, Academia Sinica evolution pages and Nohara’s study. Opened and inspected the CUHK 小 and 少 records, both Sinica pages, and the J-STAGE PDF. The J-STAGE article landing page failed to open, but the PDF opened and its discussion was inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 小 字形演變 少 字源 沙 商承祚 于省吾 馬敘倫\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.hiemalis.org/~acy/mnc/2679131gc1j.html\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/ancient.php?start=920\", \"https://www.ch.ntnu.edu.tw/SIS/show_paper_detail.php?issue_id=42&paper_id=378&periodicalsDir=DESC&periodicalsOrder=Sorter_issue_ch&periodicalsPage=7\", \"https://www.chnmuseum.cn/portals/0/web/zt/20191022jiagu/\", \"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/chuugokugogaku/2015/262/2015_57/_pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"A targeted follow-up surfaced the same CUHK and Sinica claims plus general bibliographic/context pages. The character-specific pages and J-STAGE PDF were inspected; the other results were not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"Live inspection of supplied 字源 scans, PDF pp. 76–77 / printed pp. 64–65\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the supplied original-resolution scan images for PDF pages 76 and 77 and inspected targeted crops. Confirmed the 小 headword and passage on page 76 and the adjacent 少 continuation context on page 77. Checked the printed forms 小, 少, 沙, 大, 八, 丨 and citation 《粹》1004 against the pixels. No OCR correction was identified in the cited passage. The WorldCat URL was not opened; bibliographic metadata came from the supplied source registration.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\", \"字源’s claim that 小’s marks depict sand grains and that 小 was an early graph of 沙 is this author’s analysis; consulted references also support an abstract smallness-sign account. The original depicted referent remains unsettled. No inspected source establishes a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on the name or pronunciation of depicted sand grains.\", \"The 字源 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 and other early meanings, but the underlying inscriptions were not inspected here; no absolute artifact dates or demonstrated semantic bridge for the time and status uses were established.\", \"字源 PDF page 76 is printed p. 64; its continuation is PDF page 77, printed p. 65. Both scan images were directly inspected. No specific OCR correction was identified in the cited 小 passage.\", \"The local reading row for 小 is current kMandarin metadata, not evidence about ancient sound relationships. The official individual Unicode Unihan record endpoint was not accessed in this research invocation.\", \"The CUHK page attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but this invocation did not independently establish a historical sound relationship using inspected component and host readings in the same reconstruction system. Keep that role source-attributed and unresolved.\", \"The earliest marks’ specific referent remains disputed: inspected sources present both abstract marks for smallness and fine sand grains. No inspected evidence establishes the sand object’s name or shows that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using that name.\", \"The book’s cited oracle-bone 小雨 example was not checked against an inscription facsimile; no absolute artifact date was verified. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"Accounts support some early graphic and orthographic overlap between 小 and 少, but the specific development and lexical relationship remain contested. Nohara’s study reports competing phonological analyses; its ResearchGate page was inspected, but the underlying cited Schuessler and Baxter–Sagart pages were not independently opened here.\", \"The official individual Unihan endpoint and the Baxter–Sagart table could not be inspected in this invocation. No current or reconstructed reading rows are newly asserted; local rows in the input remain local data and are not treated as external verification.\", \"The 《字源》 scan backs the author’s analysis but does not settle it. No specific OCR correction was identified for the cited 小 passage. The WorldCat catalogue URL could not be opened; publication metadata were supplied in the input.\", \"The earliest marks’ referent remains disputed: consulted accounts describe abstract marks for smallness or fine sand grains. No inspected evidence independently establishes the depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on its name.\", \"The 《字源》 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 with 《粹》1004, but no inscription facsimile or absolute artifact date was checked. The consulted sources report time and status uses without establishing their dates or semantic bridges from ‘small.’\", \"The shared-graph and later differentiation account for 小 and 少 is reported by several references, but it does not itself establish a historical sound relationship. Nohara’s inspected study finds the phonetic connection unsupported by the evidence he examines; the broader lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. This research did not establish that role through an independent historical sound comparison, so it remains source-attributed.\", \"The WorldCat catalogue URL was not inspected. Edition details are from the supplied source registration; the source scans themselves were directly inspected.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its present form is three strokes: a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. Early marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, but their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\", \"ref033\", \"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"小 has no settled account of what its early marks represent: analyses describe abstract signs of smallness, raindrops or scattered objects, or fine sand grains. The sand-grain account treats 小 as an early graph of 沙, but neither the depicted referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before they were differentiated; one account says 少’s lower dot became a diagonal stroke. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s analysis of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical interpretation, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\", \"ref033\", \"ref034\"], \"type\": \"disputed\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref018\", \"ref025\", \"ref026\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref004\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref018\", \"ref019\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref019\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}, {\"id\": \"小~少:variant_of\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"variant_of\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before later differentiation; one account says 少’s lower mark became a diagonal stroke. This supports a qualified graphic relationship, not a direction in which one character derives from the other.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is a documented early use, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small” and is written with a long central stroke between two shorter side strokes. The early marks are interpreted either as signs of smallness or as fine sand grains; their referent remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\", \"ref033\", \"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; In the authored article, cite the retained book evidence ID on the specific supported claim; placing a new book record only in the dossier is not article enrichment. Use evidence_ids arrays, never inline labels. If the book corroborates existing prose, preserve that prose and add only the supported citation before fresh reviews. if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"additional_research_context\": {\"purpose\": \"Genuinely integrate consulted 字源 evidence into the explanation with fresh independent reviews. Previous research is verified but uncited.\", \"verified_uncited_book_records\": [{\"research_output\": \"initial-followup/research/result.json\", \"research_receipt_hash\": \"277f9ded3184345f610494a1b4e2fd365ebe24133b4b8fac1cbd612e9e03c4f4\", \"research_result_hash\": \"44a2e6be132e01ec5b0cd11730b3aedf1d129031e3b4fd7a7858bd3609dcacc0\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; continuation PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry calls 小 pictographic, interpreting its marks as tiny dust or sand particles and describing 小 as an early graph of 沙. It says the graph extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and reports that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions, citing 《粹》1004 for 小雨. It describes later extensions to low status, contempt and youth. It says the graph had become corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, used similarly in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its account continues on printed p. 65. Both attached page images were inspected. Forms checked against scan pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and 《粹》1004. This verifies what this author claims, not the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\"]}], \"instruction\": \"Read the page record and original scan; attach its exact retained evidence ID to the specific supported article claim in evidence_ids metadata. If existing prose already accurately states that claim, unchanged prose plus the supporting citation is sufficient. Do not put evidence IDs or source names into explanatory prose. Do not assert original meaning or earliest priority unless the source establishes it. Other authoritative sources remain welcome; book citation requires genuine relevant support.\"}, \"additional_context_policy\": \"These source leads and prior findings are research tasks, not preverified dossier evidence. Consult the referenced sources directly, check exact graph identity and scope, and add cited evidence for useful claims before authorship. Recheck reviewer hypotheses; modern structure does not establish historical roles. Record inaccessible sources as gaps.\", \"unfinished_draft_provenance\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/book-citation-integration/ziyuan-2012/5C0F\", \"article_hash\": \"3830174141a77649fcd89fcff8d1f9b9bf6541522ddf76f9ffab2dfb233834af\", \"dossier_hash\": \"ccd0119c02d34950e094226f73a4647c9e621f79aac9300c200daebaeffdae68\", \"previous_state_hash\": \"e722e9b4b37889a4fced3f184416a6ae45d26181dc78824c45ffa3389f7e3878\", \"canonical_article_hash\": \"ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749\", \"canonical_dossier_hash\": \"a69304c4a3535766b281e20fb125b3ecc8231c64ed859ca694e6c69f0d62c3be\", \"requires_fresh_research_and_reviews\": true, \"prior_review_proposals_hash\": \"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\"}, \"prior_review_proposals\": [], \"continuation_policy\": \"This is an unapproved draft, not reusable approval. Recheck source-dependent claims against current source scans/corpus. Prior revise findings are hypotheses to recheck, including possible source identity errors; do not obey them merely because a previous verifier repeated them. Preserve prior supported work and obtain fresh factual/readability reviews.\", \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"task\": \"Make the smallest supported changes that resolve the supplied findings and their directly affected fields. Preserve unaffected prose, citations, readings and graph records exactly; do not perform another general rewrite. Check that each changed claim still cites evidence supporting its precise scope.\", \"original_role\": \"editor\", \"article_contract\": {\"relationship_branches\": [{\"predicates\": [\"semantic_component_of\", \"phonetic_component_of\", \"pictorial_component_of\", \"indicator_component_of\", \"replacement_component_of\", \"empty_component_of\"], \"subject_kind\": \"component\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"variant_of\", \"simplified_from\", \"derived_from\", \"phonetic_element_in\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"has_sense\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"sense_developed_into\", \"phonetic_loan_for\"], \"subject_kind\": \"sense\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}], \"instruction\": \"These are the exact allowed relationship names and endpoint kinds. _component_of is a suffix, never a literal predicate. Unknown component roles create no invented unknown_component_of edge. Preserve scoped historical claims and supported graph links.\", \"citation_instruction\": \"Reader prose must contain no refNNN transport aliases or inline evidence ID lists. Store citations only in the containing record evidence_ids array. When editing only a text field, preserve its existing evidence_ids; if support changes, edit that array as a separate nonoverlapping path. Never append [ref001, ref002] to prose.\"}, \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749/source_checkpoint.json:30:            \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749/initial-followup/research/prompt.txt:180:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X-874259a2b827e31d1455\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-e79ea1cfe5b5aa959a40\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6339f9c0072cd12d52a\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-f6f7d68f09a02c854d24\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-2829f816c26a5d71ec70\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-253281f9589a65c2d2a6\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%E5%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%E5%B8%B8%E7%94%A8%E6%BC%A2%E5%AD%97%E4%BA%8C%E7%99%BE%E5%AD%97%E6%8E%A2%E6%BA%90%EF%BC%88%E9%87%91%E7%9C%9F%E5%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E5%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” An oracle-bone example uses it with this meaning; the earlier marks have been interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"Its construction is disputed: the marks may indicate smallness or picture fine sand grains. The sand interpretation has been proposed as an early graph of 沙, but neither the pictured referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s account of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical analysis, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\"], \"type\": \"disputed\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"E4\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-1ba7a1471a7e7d07ce0d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\", \"X-a036255ebabd78a9e34e\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is the earliest supported use here, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-266b8f35d92e78d22ad7\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}]}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-21e5c27b88aea75dc33a\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-eca33181dc16b0f2f777\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->少:derived_from\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"derived_from\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before differentiation; a lower mark in 少 later became a diagonal stroke. The detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7098042d6576a6e2edc8\", \"X-6a67a2eba00317f0db96\"]}, {\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b1e6b91540b7c6134706\", \"X-af8f4361c990e6a92f5e\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is the earliest supported use here, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"E7\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its early marks may have been signs of smallness or pictures of fine sand grains; the original interpretation remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-9337d44faa93154e5b08\", \"X-7c4ee97c98a3f9f753ae\", \"X-2a90e282a2ff3f19511d\", \"X-220d6fa8a217b3555938\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"task\": \"Resolve the supplied evidence gaps before editing. Add accurate citable records for required claims; preserve existing supported content and source provenance.\", \"research_task\": \"Resolve these specific review findings. Keep supported evidence and glyph selections unless correction is needed. Inspect additional local or external sources as needed; revise glyph captions/selection when findings require it. Report remaining gaps honestly.\", \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}, \"attached_source_scans\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"attachment_index\": 1, \"sha256\": \"01ac480a495cc967c1affd418da7968d809784f57f0b0a82dbfdc21289a479b2\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"attachment_index\": 2, \"sha256\": \"70e48f562d95c0df6f03b3ac696c51ebffc7e338bd2c526d02b6e4de9b174c43\", \"sha256_kind\": \"encoded_image_file_bytes\", \"pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"pixel_sha256_kind\": \"decoded_RGB_pixel_bytes\"}]}\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749/source_feedback.json:42:          \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/5C0F/ba08ff855891a2882468799e135df5bac81e6edf3cb13cdf8cf2b41555487749/round-0/editor/prompt.txt:298:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"Dividing 八 by a line 亅\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of three small dots, representing grains of sand or rice, to convey the meaning \\\"small\\\".\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Two possible interpretations:\\n* Ideogram (指事) – three small dots > small.\\n* Pictogram (象形) – three granules of sand – original character of 沙 (OC *sraːl, *sraːls) > small.\\nStarting from some bronze inscriptions, the dots have become elongated. Based on this form, Shuowen erroneously considers it to be an ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 八 (“to divide; to separate”) + 丨 (“small object”).\\nCompare with 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews), represented by four dots in its oracle bone script form. Unrelated to the bottom part of 京, 示, 尞, 尗, and 原.\\nOften thought to be related to 少 (OC *hmjewʔ, *hmjews) (e.g. Wang, 1982), but Schuessler (2007) points out that the alternation between Middle Chinese /s-/ and /ɕ-/ within a word family is exceptional.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"物之微也。从八，丨見而分之。凡小之屬皆从小。\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"small, tiny, insignificant\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"siu2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"CHIISAI KO O\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"SHOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"SO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"xiǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*siɛ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"tiểu\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"small\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1149a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"sjewX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*[s]ewʔ\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*smewʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"私兆\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"心開三宵上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"smewʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"宵\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Little; Small\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ちい.さい こ- お- さ-\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ショウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"小 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database identifies oracle-bone and bronze forms, and gives two possible interpretations of their marks: three small dots symbolizing smallness, or grains of sand scattered around. It says that in small-seal script the central dot became a vertical stroke separating the two others.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"甲骨文、金文、小篆字形\", \"text\": \"The page labels the displayed stages oracle bone, bronze, small-seal, and Shuowen. Its account describes the seal form as a graphic alteration of earlier dots; the shapes alone do not establish the identity of a depicted object.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文及構形初義\", \"text\": \"The database records competing analyses: three small dots signifying smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙, depicting fine sand grains, attributed to Ma Xulun. It also says 小 and 少 were originally one graph and later differentiated.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"甲骨文詞例及金文用例\", \"text\": \"It reports oracle-bone 小 meaning ‘small,’ citing 《粹》1004 小雨; it separately reports 小食 as an afternoon time expression and 小采 as an evening expression. For bronze inscriptions it gives 嬴霝德鼎 小鼎 and notes 小子 for young aristocratic sons and lower officials, and 小臣 for officials subordinate to rulers. The page supplies no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"少 entry, graphic and usage account\", \"text\": \"It says oracle-bone forms of 小/少 could have four dots and were used in the same way; later the three-dot graph was used for 小 and four-dot form for 少. In bronze forms, the lowest dot of 少 extended into a diagonal stroke. The page cites bronze uses of 少 for ‘few’ and ‘young,’ and a later case where 少 is read as 小. This supports graphic differentiation and shared early use, but does not settle the more specific proposed sand-grain referent.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The database states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; in bronze inscriptions the bottom dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke, distinguishing it from 小. This is a clear assertion of the shared-origin analysis, while CUHK presents that relationship alongside competing accounts of 小’s earliest formation.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》 (PDF), entry 小\", \"field\": \"字形探源 and 字義探源, pp. 20–21\", \"text\": \"The book lists oracle-bone and bronze examples and says both scripts write 小 with three dots. It reports disagreement over what the dots depict, including raindrops, scattered objects, or fine smallness; it judges an abstract indicator-of-smallness analysis plausible. It says later dots lengthened into lines and rejects Shuowen’s parsing of the seal form as 八 plus 丨 as an error based on the later shape. It cites oracle-bone examples 小雨, 小風, and 小牢 for the ‘small’ use. This provides another account of the dots and the alleged graphic reinterpretation, not independent proof that they depict sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"常用漢字二百字探源\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"Ministry of Education, Taiwan, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"說文釋形; 字樣說明; 釋義\", \"text\": \"The dictionary reproduces Xu Shen’s analysis: 小 means ‘the subtle/small among things’ and is analyzed as 八 and 丨, ‘seen and divided.’ It preserves this as a historical dictionary account. Its modern senses include small in size or quantity, make smaller, briefly, young, low-ranking, and humble self-reference, with classical citations; the page does not establish a chronological order among those senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"教育部《異體字字典》：小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, 小 entry (consulted for competing analysis and reconstruction display)\", \"field\": \"Chinese Glyph origin; Middle Chinese and Old Chinese readings\", \"text\": \"The entry presents both an indicator interpretation (three dots to mean small) and a pictorial sand-grain interpretation, the latter identifying 小 as an early graph of 沙. It labels the 八 plus 丨 analysis in Shuowen an erroneous interpretation of an altered seal form. Its displayed comparisons are Middle Chinese 小 sjewX, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese *[s]ewʔ, and Zhengzhang *smewʔ. These are source-reported reconstructions, not interchangeable notations.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_and_reconstruction_compilation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"title\": \"小 - Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"Baxter and Sagart, Old Chinese reconstruction version 1.1, 13 September 2014\", \"field\": \"Reconstruction notation documentation\", \"text\": \"The authors distinguish Middle Chinese transcription from reconstruction, explain that square brackets mark uncertain identity and parentheses uncertain presence, and identify the table as version 1.1. The locally supplied Baxter–Sagart row for 小 is 小, Mandarin xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ, gloss ‘small’; the opened source PDF was verified as the authors’ version 1.1 table, but browser extraction did not expose the specific 小 row. The value here is therefore reported as checked against the provided local row, not as an independently extracted online row.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"reconstruction_reference_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"Chinese University of Hong Kong, Multi-function Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"雀 entry: component roles\", \"text\": \"The database describes 小 in 雀 as both contributing the sense ‘small bird’ and functioning as a sound element. This is evidence for a phonetic role of 小 in that compound, but it does not establish that sand was the original object represented by 小. No comparison of 雀’s and 小’s readings in one reconstructed system was verified in this research.\", \"record_character\": \"雀\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Unihan lookup instructions and database scope\", \"text\": \"The official page says the lookup interface provides property data for individual ideographs, including readings, and that data are based on Unicode 18.0.0. The exact 小 record could not be accessed through the required per-character endpoint. The supplied local Unihan_Readings.txt row for 小 gives kMandarin xiǎo; it is cited here as a local row check, not as externally verified character-specific Unicode evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"official_database_documentation_and_local_row\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"構形初義; 甲骨文用例; 金文用例\", \"text\": \"The database records two competing origin analyses: three dots representing smallness, attributed to Shang Chengzuo and Yu Xingwu; or 小 as an early graph of 沙 depicting fine sand, attributed to Ma Xulun. It says 小 and 少 were one graph before differentiation. It cites oracle-bone 小雨 (《粹》1004), 小食 for afternoon, 小采 for dusk, bronze 小鼎, and 小子/小臣 for young nobles or lower officials. It gives no absolute dates for the cited artifacts or semantic developments.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page labels oracle-bone, bronze, small-seal and Shuowen stages. It asserts that the earlier marks depict scattered fine sand grains and says the middle dot in small-seal changed into a vertical stroke separating the other two. This is one source's analysis; the marks do not independently identify sand.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"title\": \"漢字「小」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Summary; original file; Licensing\", \"text\": \"The inspected File page identifies 小-seal.svg as a modern vector depiction of 小 in Shuowen seal-script style and links its original image. The page states public-domain dedication for the representation, alongside CC0 information. This is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"image_provenance_and_rights\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Character Database\", \"field\": \"智財權聲明\", \"text\": \"The inspected rights notice reserves intellectual property in the site's content and limits use absent a separate agreement; it does not provide CC0 reuse terms. Accordingly, its page is used as linked evidence, not as an image asset.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\", \"title\": \"智財權聲明\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium, Unihan Database Lookup\", \"field\": \"Per-character Unihan record\", \"text\": \"The required individual record endpoint for 小 could not be opened in this run (internal fetch error). The supplied local Unihan row records kMandarin xiǎo, but is local data and is not independently verified here as a character-specific external record.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"access_failure_and_local_row_note\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan Database Lookup\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt; kMandarin rows\", \"text\": \"Supplied exact local rows: 小 xiǎo; 少 shǎo; 雀 què. These are current Mandarin metadata only, not evidence of ancient phonetic relationships. Their official individual Unihan click-through records for 小, 少, and 雀 were not verified as accessible in this run.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reading_rows\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"title\": \"Unihan individual character lookup (endpoint unavailable)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"Repository supplied local_primary_readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv; Baxter–Sagart 2014 rows\", \"text\": \"Exact supplied rows: 小 xiǎo, MC sjewX, OC *[s]ewʔ (‘small’); 少 shǎo MC syewX OC *[s.t]ewʔ (‘few’) and shào MC syewH OC *[s-t]ewʔ-s (‘young’); 雀 què MC tsjak OC *[ts]ewk (‘sparrow’). These are Baxter–Sagart 2014 table notations, checked as local rows. The *[s]ewʔ and *[ts]ewk forms do not support an obvious rhyme match for a proposed 小→雀 sound role in that system; CUHK's component-role assertion can be reported as source-attributed, without treating the local forms as proof of a sound change.\", \"record_character\": \"小; 少; 雀\", \"kind\": \"local_reconstruction_rows\", \"url\": \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20 (version 1.1)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編,《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012\", \"field\": \"小 headword; PDF p. 76, printed p. 64; scanned headword and continuation checked on PDF p. 77, printed p. 65\", \"text\": \"The scanned entry identifies 小 as pictographic and interprets its marks as tiny grains of dust or sand, calling it an early graph of 沙; it says the graph was extended to mean ‘small,’ opposite 大, and gives that sense in oracle-bone and bronze inscriptions. It cites 《粹》1004 for 小雨, then describes extensions to low status, contempt, and youth. It says the current form was corrupted in bronze inscriptions and treats Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 account as an explanation of that altered form. The adjacent 少 entry says 小 and 少 were originally one graph, with similar use in oracle-bone texts, and later differentiated; its explanation continues onto printed p. 65. Direct scan inspection confirms the cited headword and passage. Forms checked against pixels: 小, 少, 沙, 八, 丨, 大, and the text’s citation 《粹》1004. The scan supports what this author asserts; it does not settle the disputed sand-grain interpretation.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"directly_inspected_book_scan\", \"url\": \"https://www.worldcat.org/search?q=9787552800692\", \"title\": \"《字源》 (2012), PDF pp. 76–77; printed pp. 64–65\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"漢語多功能字庫, Chinese University of Hong Kong\", \"field\": \"小: formation, early use and adjacent 少 entry\", \"text\": \"The database reports two analyses of 小’s early marks: abstract dots for smallness, attributed to 商承祚 and 于省吾, or an early graph of 沙 depicting fine grains, attributed to 馬敘倫. It also describes 小 and 少 as an originally shared graph. Its examples include oracle-bone 小雨, time expressions 小食 and 小采, and bronze 小鼎, 小子 and 小臣. The page provides no absolute dates for these cited artifacts or usages, so it cannot alone establish a chronology among the senses.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_and_historical_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：漢字甲骨部件分析—小\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"金真姬,《常用漢字二百字探源》\", \"field\": \"小, 字形探源／字義探源, PDF pp. 20–21; printed pp. 26–27\", \"text\": \"The inspected entry reports disagreement about what the three dots represent, including raindrops, scattered objects or fine smallness. It favors an abstract indication of smallness and says later lengthened dots prompted Shuowen’s 八-plus-丨 analysis, which it rejects as based on the altered form. It cites oracle-bone 小雨, 小風 and 小牢 as uses for ‘small.’ This supports a competing scholarly account, not a settled referent for the dots.\", \"record_character\": \"小\", \"kind\": \"scholarly_reference\", \"url\": \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\", \"title\": \"《常用漢字二百字探源》, PDF pp. 20–21\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"中央研究院, Academia Sinica Character Database\", \"field\": \"少 字形演變\", \"text\": \"The page states that 少 and 小 were originally one graph depicting fine sand grains, with ‘small’ as the original meaning; it says the lower dot of 少 became a diagonal stroke in bronze inscriptions, distinguishing it from 小. This is an explicit shared-origin analysis, while other consulted sources retain competing analyses of 小’s dots.\", \"record_character\": \"少\", \"kind\": \"paleographic_dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"title\": \"漢字「少」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-02\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": {\"makemeahanzi\": \"ideographic\", \"dong_chinese\": \"other\", \"shuowen_jiezi\": \"ideographic\", \"wiktionary\": \"pictographic\"}, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions etymology Shuowen paleography; 小 說文 甲骨文 金文 字形 演變 小 字源; site:zi.tools 小 paleography bronze oracle bone 小; 小 少 relation Old Chinese Schuessler 2007\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/b5a7377f-6b01-4704-a136-6b40a4d04078\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/3081ht/\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica’s 小 evolution page and Wiktionary entry. Guoxuedashi result was later blocked by a verification page. Search results surfaced but did not establish the cited secondary Schuessler analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F 小 kMandarin; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 小 沙 Old Chinese Baxter Sagart 2014; 小 最早 用例 甲骨文 小 大 說文 字義 殷周\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://www.zdic.net/hans/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?if=gb&rad=42\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?educode=A01068&powerMode=2\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=%ref005%B0%8F\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK, Taiwan Ministry of Education and Academia Sinica pages. The requested Unihan endpoint returned an internal fetch error. CUHK provided early usage examples and competing graphic analyses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F; Unihan 小 U+5C0F kMandarin xiǎo Unicode; \\\"小雨\\\" \\\"《粹》1004\\\" 甲骨\", \"urls\": [\"https://unicode.org/faq/han_cjk.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-3.html\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%ref005%B0%8F\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007%94%A8%ref006%BC%A2%ref005%AD%97%ref004%BA%8C%ref007%99%BE%ref005%AD%97%ref006%8E%A2%ref006%BA%90%EF%BC%88%ref009%87%91%ref007%9C%9F%ref005%A7%AC%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Unicode’s individual-record endpoint remained inaccessible. Official Unicode documentation and lookup page were opened; the detailed PDF was opened and supplied paleographic and oracle-bone usage evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu \\\"小\\\" \\\"沙\\\" *[s]ew; Baxter Sagart 2014 Old Chinese 小 沙 reconstruction; Chinese etymology 小 沙 original character paleography 马叙伦 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://academic.oup.com/book/26638\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/\", \"https://sites.lsa.umich.edu/ocbaxtersagart/wp-content/uploads/sites/1415/2025/03/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the author-hosted Baxter–Sagart table PDF and Academia Sinica 少 page. Reconstruction systems differ: Baxter–Sagart *[s]ewʔ and Zhengzhang *smewʔ for 小; the primary-search results did not establish a phonetic borrowing of 沙 from 小 or the original pronunciation of a depicted sand object.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 字形 甲骨文 金文 三点 沙 小 少 字源\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10271\", \"https://www.yjcn.nl/wp/wp-content/uploads/2015/11/%ref005%B8%B8%ref007用漢字二百字探源%EF%BC%88金真姬%EF%BC%89.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened CUHK’s 小 record, Academia Sinica’s 少 evolution page, and the PDF entry for 小. These sources support competing interpretations of 小’s early marks and report a relationship between 小 and 少.\"}, {\"query\": \"小 少 甲骨文 同字 分化 說文 小 甲骨 用例 CUHK Character database\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CUHK page and checked its 小 formation and early-use account, the linked 少 discussion, and its 沙 and 雀 component notes.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The small-sand-grain depiction / 小 as an early graph of 沙 analysis is disputed; sources also analyze the dots as abstract marks for smallness, and the depicted referent is not independently established.\", \"The graph’s oracle-bone use meaning ‘small’ is reported with specific inscription citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish an absolute date for that artifact. CUHK also reports separate time terms 小食 and 小采 without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"No evidence here establishes that 沙 was borrowed phonetically using a word name for the depicted grains. Treating 小 as the source graph of 沙 remains an attributed proposal, not a proven sound loan; the sand-object name and pronunciation are unrecoverable from the consulted evidence.\", \"The 小–少 graphic relationship is supported by sources describing an original shared graph and later differentiation, while the specific sand-grain analysis remains contested. Wiktionary reports an exceptional Middle Chinese s-/ɕ- alternation in a proposed word-family relationship, but the primary sources accessed here do not resolve the lexical relationship or independently document Schuessler’s argument.\", \"No absolute dates were recovered for the cited bronze examples or their semantic extensions. Do not use modern dictionary gloss ordering as a chronology.\", \"The character-specific official Unicode Unihan page https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F repeatedly failed to open. Current kMandarin xiǎo was checked in the supplied local source row; no separately inspectable official per-character URL could be verified.\", \"For 雀, CUHK asserts 小 is a phonetic component, but a same-system reconstruction comparison for both 雀 and 小 was not completed. The local Baxter–Sagart file does not substitute for inspection of both exact rows, and the sound role should remain source-attributed rather than demonstrated here.\", \"The local glyph candidates were not opened or visually inspected in this research; no claims are made from those files.\", \"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\", \"字源’s claim that 小’s marks depict sand grains and that 小 was an early graph of 沙 is this author’s analysis; consulted references also support an abstract smallness-sign account. The original depicted referent remains unsettled. No inspected source establishes a phonetic borrowing of 沙 based on the name or pronunciation of depicted sand grains.\", \"The 字源 entry reports oracle-bone 小雨 and other early meanings, but the underlying inscriptions were not inspected here; no absolute artifact dates or demonstrated semantic bridge for the time and status uses were established.\", \"字源 PDF page 76 is printed p. 64; its continuation is PDF page 77, printed p. 65. Both scan images were directly inspected. No specific OCR correction was identified in the cited 小 passage.\", \"The local reading row for 小 is current kMandarin metadata, not evidence about ancient sound relationships. The official individual Unicode Unihan record endpoint was not accessed in this research invocation.\", \"The CUHK page attributes a sound role to 小 in 雀, but this invocation did not independently establish a historical sound relationship using inspected component and host readings in the same reconstruction system. Keep that role source-attributed and unresolved.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"小 oracle bone bronze character formation three dots sand grains 說文 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=10269\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK and Academia Sinica character-specific pages, plus the exact Commons file page. CUHK reports rival accounts of the dots and early usages; Academia Sinica supports its own sand-grain account and description of the seal-form change. Commons identifies the selected seal glyph as a modern redraw with reuse information.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 小 甲骨文 小雨 小 少\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref005%B0%8F&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected relevant lines describing disputed formation, early examples, and 小–少 relation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 小-seal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Exact Commons file page opened. Verified graph identity, original-file link, modern redraw status and rights statement.\"}, {\"query\": \"Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang 小 字形演變 小學堂 glyph CC0 image; site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian/Home/Home\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=5C0F\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/copyright.jsp\"], \"outcome\": \"Xiaoxuetang landing page and Character Database rights notice inspected; no 小-specific Xiaoxuetang glyph record was retrieved. The Unicode per-character endpoint returned an internal fetch error.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"Whether the earliest dots depict grains of sand or are abstract marks for smallness remains disputed. No inspected evidence establishes the specific depicted object or a phonetic borrowing of 沙; the sand analysis is attributed to Ma Xulun, not demonstrated as a sound loan.\", \"CUHK reports an oracle-bone usage 小雨 with the citation 《粹》1004, but this research did not inspect a facsimile or establish the artifact's absolute date. It reports time expressions and later bronze uses without dates or a demonstrated semantic bridge from ‘small.’\", \"The 小–少 shared-graph and differentiation account is reported by CUHK, but the lexical and graphic details are not settled by the material inspected here.\", \"CUHK reports 小 as a sound component in 雀. Local Baxter–Sagart rows are included for both graphs, but do not by themselves explain that source-attributed phonetic role; the individual character pages and a phonetic-history account were not independently verified.\", \"The official Unicode individual-record endpoint for 小 returned an internal fetch error. Local reading rows are identified as local provenance, not externally confirmed records.\", \"No Xiaoxuetang single-character form page or image was obtained in the inspected pages. The selected image is therefore the Commons modern seal-script redraw, not an ancient artifact image. The separate Sinica Character Database page states All Rights Reserved, so no image from that site is proposed.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"小-seal\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/61906767a952d4e4e1b965d4ce6a0537d34b4d25a72dd3fc731d37b4a4991ba0.svg\", \"byte_length\": 1807}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"小\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” An oracle-bone example uses it with this meaning; the earlier marks have been interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"Its construction is disputed: the marks may indicate smallness or picture fine sand grains. The sand interpretation has been proposed as an early graph of 沙, but neither the pictured referent nor a sound-based borrowing is established. In small-seal script, the central dot became a vertical stroke between two side marks. Shuowen’s account of this later form as 八 plus 丨 is a historical analysis, not evidence of the original construction.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\"], \"type\": \"disputed\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze forms are described as three dots. In small-seal script, the central dot lengthened into a vertical stroke. The curated image is a modern redraw of that script style, not an ancient artifact; the graphic change does not identify what the earlier marks represented.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref018\", \"ref025\", \"ref026\"]}, {\"text\": \"Shuowen parses the seal form as 八 and 丨, explaining the marks as dividing something. Later analyses treat this as a reinterpretation of an altered form. Accounts also describe 小 and 少 as once sharing a graph before later differentiation, though their detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref004\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref018\", \"ref019\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest marks may indicate smallness abstractly or depict fine sand grains. The sand analysis does not establish the grains’ name or pronunciation, or show that 沙 was written by reusing 小 for a similar-sounding word.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref020\", \"ref024\", \"ref025\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited oracle-bone use 小雨 is reported as an early example, but its artifact has no absolute date here. Other old uses include time expressions and status-related terms; their dates and connection with “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, but the historical sound relation remains disputed. In the supplied Baxter–Sagart reconstructions, 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk do not show an obvious rhyme match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"小:small\", \"gloss\": \"small\", \"period\": \"Oracle-bone use; absolute date unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is the earliest supported use here, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"afternoon time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\", \"gloss\": \"dusk time expression\", \"period\": \"Dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"小:small-current\", \"gloss\": \"small; tiny; insignificant\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The available examples do not establish a dated semantic path between early and current uses, or between “small” and the reported time expressions or status-related uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref019\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"小-seal\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref005%B0%8F-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:小-seal.svg\", \"period\": \"Modern vector redraw of the Shuowen small-seal form; depicts an ancient script style, not a dated artifact\", \"tradition\": \"Small seal script (小篆), Shuowen style\", \"caption\": \"This modern redraw shows 小 in Shuowen small-seal style. The central dot has become a vertical stroke between two side marks; this later shape does not settle what the earlier marks represented.\", \"alt\": \"Three black strokes: a long central vertical stroke between two shorter, slightly curved side strokes.\", \"selection_reason\": \"It illustrates the seal-script form and the described graphic change while keeping the disputed interpretation of the earlier marks open.\", \"rights\": \"Public-domain dedication for the modern vector representation; Commons page also records CC0 information.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref026\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This is a modern redraw, not an ancient inscription image. The earlier marks are interpreted either as abstract signs of smallness or as fine sand grains, and their referent remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref018\", \"ref026\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"小->少:derived_from\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"少\"}, \"predicate\": \"derived_from\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"Accounts describe 小 and 少 as sharing an early graph before differentiation; a lower mark in 少 later became a diagonal stroke. The detailed graphic and lexical relationship remains unsettled.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref017\"]}, {\"id\": \"小->雀:phonetic_element_in\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"雀\"}, \"predicate\": \"phonetic_element_in\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"小 is reported as a sound element in 雀, which is also described as contributing the sense “small bird.” This proposed sound role is not explained by the supplied Baxter–Sagart forms: 小 *[s]ewʔ and 雀 *[ts]ewk show no obvious rhyme match, and no sound-change account is established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref030\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example 小雨 is reported with 小 meaning “small.” This is the earliest supported use here, not proof of the character’s hypothetical original referent.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:afternoon-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:afternoon-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小食 is reported as an expression for an afternoon time. Its relation to the sense “small” is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:dusk-time-expression\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:dusk-time-expression\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小采 is reported as an expression for dusk. Its chronology and relation to the sense “small” are unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:小:small-current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"小\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"小:small-current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"小\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"小 is used with the sense “small,” including “tiny” and “insignificant.”\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref007\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"小 means “small.” Its early marks may have been signs of smallness or pictures of fine sand grains; the original interpretation remains disputed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref018\", \"ref024\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"小\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000076\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 76, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d85609e1fe13129b143e8f5f5c7c72b9172d6bb4eb896f2e0b29cee0bda82462\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"小部 八部\\n\\n小部\\n\\n小 xiǎo 心组、宵部;心组、小韵、私兆切。\\n[glyph:g001] [glyph:g002] [glyph:g003] [glyph:g004] [glyph:g005] [glyph:g006] [glyph:g007] 小\\n商 西周 西周 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》27页。2、3《金文编》43页。\\n4、6、7《甲金篆》61页。5《说文》28页。\\n象形字。字像尘沙小物状,乃沙之初文。引申指微小,与大相对,甲骨文、金文中即用此义。《粹》1004:“丁至庚不遘小雨。”训匾:“自今余敢夔(扰)乃小大事。”由微小义引申出低微、低等义。《孟子·万章下》:“不辞小官。”又指地位低微或品质不好的人,见于文献的“小人”一语两种意义均见使用。由低微、低等义引申指轻视、小看义。《韩非子·外储说右上》:“子小寡人之国,以为不足仕(仕,做官)。”由微小义亦引申指年幼或年幼的人,亦可指身体小。睡虎地秦简《秦律十八种·仓律》:“隶臣、城旦高不盈六尺五寸,隶妾、舂高不盈六尺二寸,皆为小。”小字在金文中已发生讹变,《说文》曰“从八,丨见而八分之”,乃就已讹之形为说,不确。(陈英杰)\\n\\n少 shǎo 书组、宵部;书组、小韵、书沼切。\\nshào 书组、宵部;书组、笑韵、失照切。\\n[glyph:g008] [glyph:g009] [glyph:g010] [glyph:g011]\\n春秋 战国 汉 战国\\n[glyph:g012] [glyph:g013] [glyph:g014] [glyph:g015] [glyph:g016] [glyph:g017] [glyph:g018] 少\\n商 春秋 春秋 春秋 战国 汉 汉 楷书\\n[glyph:g019] [glyph:g020] [glyph:g021]\\n战国《说文》小篆 汉\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3、5《金文编》46页。4、7、9《战文编》53页。6《汉印徵》卷2,1页。8、10、14《甲金篆》61页。11《说文》28页。12《马王堆》38页。13《秦汉金文·下编》26页。\\n少与小乃一字,本为象形字。卜辞中所见之“少”字皆与“小”字同义。《人》2389:“壬戌卜,甲子少(小)雨。”自来古文字学家皆以从三点之[glyph:g022]为小,以从四点之[glyph:g023]为少。甲骨文中二字构形实同,应为一字,二字分化大概是春秋战国间的事。少、小分化后,“少”之末笔“丿”起着与“小”相区别的作用,成为一个指事字,但同时又以“小”为声。金文中“少”多用同“小”。蔡侯钟:“余唯(虽)末少(小)子。”战国器哀成叔鼎曰“少去父母”,乃用为年幼义,用为此义时今读 shào。厝氏扁壶“三斗少半”乃是不足、少于之义。用为此义今读 shǎo。少、小可同义连用,指年幼。汉刘向《说苑·谈丛》:“仁慈少小,恭敬耆老。”唐贺知章《回乡偶书》诗之一:“少小离家老大回,乡音无改鬓毛衰。”《说文》收有“𣥂”字,实为“少”之异体写法,其分析𣥂为从小八声,少从小丿声都是没有根据的。战国时“少”有一异体作[glyph:g024],乃是在“小”字下附加义符“子”,属于“比类合谊”的会意字(与“歪”、“劣”、“夯”、“甦”等造字方法相同),但同时以“小”为声。中山王鼎:“事孚如事娠(长),事愚如事智。”𡭚与娠对举,可知𡭚读为少长之“少”。(陈英杰)\\n\\n八部\\n\\n八 bā 帮组、物部;帮组、黠韵、博拔切。\\n[glyph:g025] [glyph:g026] [glyph:g027] [glyph:g028] [glyph:g029] [glyph:g030] 八\\n商 西周 春秋 战国《说文》小篆 秦 汉 楷书\\n1《甲文编》28页。2、3《金文编》46页。4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》62页。7《马王堆》38页。\\n指事字。《说文》:“八,别也,象分别相背之形。”八之本义为分,甲骨文乃以二画相背,分向张开,以表示分别之义。甲骨文、金文中均借为纪数之词。《乙》4516:“八羊。”克罍:“惟十又八年十又二月。”(陈英杰)\\n\\n分 fēn 帮组、文部;非组、文韵、府文切。\\nfèn 并组、文部;奉组、问韵、扶问切。\\n[glyph:g031] [glyph:g032] [glyph:g033] [glyph:g034] [glyph:g\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000888\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 888, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0888/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"e5d2358a1fe80311f97d087b7815c41660384b85969df52958e0e9879ebb712d\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"c146e7e9cae7cf9dd74b00118d06a6182723e903f2a4ca3fe0b7ef4b1dd52422\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"娩 鮸 蒐 犬 狗 獌 尨 狡 獪\\n\\n牵之。”孔颖达疏:“通而言之,狗、犬通名;若分而言之,\\n则大者为犬,小者为狗。”(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n狗 gǒu 见组、侯部;见组、厚韵、古厚切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]¹ [glyph:g002]² [glyph:g003]³ [glyph:g004]⁴ [glyph:g005]⁵ [glyph:g006]⁶ 狗\\n春秋 战国 战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2、3《战文编》\\n664页。4《说文》203页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,句声。本义是小犬。《说文》:“狗,孔\\n子曰:狗,叩也,叩气吠以守。从犬,句声。”“句”与“勾”\\n为古今字。用“叩”解释“狗”,只是一种探求把狗叫做\\n“狗”之缘由的尝试,可以存疑。狗是犬科哺乳动物,其听\\n觉、嗅觉极为灵敏,为人类最早驯化的家畜之一,可用于\\n看守门户、打猎、放牧等,学名为“犬”。《尔雅·释兽》:\\n“未成毫,狗。”(未长大毛的小狗崽叫狗)郝懿行义疏:“狗、\\n犬通名,若对文则大者名犬,小者名狗。”后不分大小,成\\n为通称。《左传·闵公二年》:“归公乘马,祭服五称,牛、\\n羊、豕、鸡、狗皆三百,与门材。”(齐桓公赠送给戴公乘马,\\n祭服五套,牛、羊、猪、鸡、狗各三百头,以及做门用的木材)狗的\\n特点是灵敏、狡猾、对生人凶狠、对主人忠诚等,所以在\\n使用中很容易产生与其自身特点相应的比喻意义和用\\n法。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n瞍 sōu 心组、幽部;生组、尤韵、所鸠切。\\n\\n[glyph:g007]¹ [glyph:g008]² [glyph:g009]³ 獌\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》664页。2《说文》203页。3《甲\\n金篆》679页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,叟声。联绵词“缨瞍”的音节之一,本\\n义是犬名。《说文》:“傻,南越名犬婴瞍。”段玉裁注:“婴\\n瘦,叠韵字,南越人名犬如是,今江、浙尚有此语。”又指\\n打猎。《礼记·祭义》“搜狩”郑玄注:“春猎为搜,冬猎为\\n狩。”《玉篇·犬部》:“瘦,秋猎。亦作蒐。”“蒐”与“搜”音\\n义相通,而“瘦”与“搜”同从“叟”旁得声,所以可依常用\\n字“搜”来作为理解罕用字“獌”、“蒐”的一个引子。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n尨 máng 明组、东部;明组、江韵、莫江切。\\nméng 明组、东部;明组、东韵、谟蓬切。\\n\\n[glyph:g010]¹ [glyph:g011]² [glyph:g012]³ [glyph:g013]⁴ 尨\\n商 商 春秋 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1、2《甲金篆》406页。3《战文编》664页。4\\n《说文》203页。\\n\\n会意字。从犬,从彡(代表多毛),据犬多毛会意。甲\\n骨文像长毛下垂的犬形;小篆由甲骨文演变而来,只是\\n代表长毛的“彡”移到了“犬”的背上;楷书由小篆演变\\n而来,写作“尨”。本义指多毛狗,读作máng。《说文》:\\n“尨,犬之多毛者。”《诗·召南·野有死麇》:“无使尨也\\n吠。”毛传:“尨,狗也。”由多毛引申为杂色义或芜杂、散\\n乱义,表示芜杂、散乱义的“尨”读作méng。按照同源字\\n原理,从音域、义域相关的族群看,“莽”、“茫”,“蒙”、\\n“猛”均与“尨”关系密切,可作为理解“尨”字的锁钥。(郭\\n小武 叶青)\\n\\n狡 jiǎo 见组、宵部;见组、效韵、古孝切。\\n\\n[glyph:g014]¹ [glyph:g015]² [glyph:g016]³ [glyph:g017]⁴ [glyph:g018]⁵ [glyph:g019]⁶ 狡\\n战国 战国 秦 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、3、5、6《甲金篆》679页。2《战文编》\\n665页。4《说文》204页。\\n\\n形声字。从犬,交声。本义为少壮的狗。《说文》:\\n“狡,少狗也。从犬,交声。匈奴地有狡犬,巨口而黑身。”\\n《淮南子·俶真》:“狡狗之死也,割之犹濡。”(小狗死后,\\n宰割时湿气尚存)高诱注:“狡,少也。”“狡”的常用义为狡\\n猾、诡诈。《广雅·释诂四》“狡,狯(kuài)也。”王念孙疏\\n证:“狡者,《众经音义》卷三引《方言》云:‘凡小儿多诈\\n而狯谓之狡猾。’”鲜于璜碑:“时依郡乌桓,狂狡畔戾。”\\n由少壮义可引申出强健、凶猛、疾速等义,而“狡”的狡\\n猾、诡诈义,一则可能是这些涵义的综合性体现,二则联\\n系着狗这种动物的最主要的特征。(郭小武 叶青)\\n\\n獪(狯) kuài 见组、月部;见组、泰韵、古外切。\\n又古卖切。\\n\\n[glyph:g020]¹ 獪 狯\\n《说文》小\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000077\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 77, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"d03253b60df7aed4c417e2e7b2b95d3079ec989f54eb6d7d6f47f731d17beb44\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"小少八分尔曾尚詹\\n\\n声。其本义就是分开、分割。丽攸比鼎：“其且(沮)射(敎)分田邑，则杀。”《书·尧典》：“分北三苗。”由分开义引申出分配、分担、分解、分支等义。用为这些意义的“分”读fēn。表示整体中的一部分以及成分、缘分等义时读fèn。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尔 ěr 日纽、脂部;日纽、纸韵、儿氏切。\\n\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国\\n[glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 战国\\n[glyph:g004]4 战国\\n[glyph:g005]5 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g006]6 汉\\n尔 楷书\\n\\n1《金文编》47页。2、3、4《战文编》54页。5《说文》28页。6《甲金篆》63页。\\n\\n尔是爾的简化字。爾，甲骨文作[glyph:g007]，金文作[glyph:g008]，构形不明。现在能看到的简体“尔”最早出现在战国时期的中山王鼎上。小篆字体见于东汉安帝建光元年(121)成书的《说文解字》。刊立于三国魏正始二年(241)的“三体石经”中，也有这个简体。现在写法的“尔”出现在东晋王羲之的作品中，是在隶书草写的基础上进行楷化形成的。金文中用作第二人称代词，相当于现代汉语的“你”，尔即你之古体。中山王鼎：“毋忘尔邦。”战国文字中“尔”或加点饰，点又经常演变为横，均无义。(陈英杰)\\n\\n曾 zēng 精纽、蒸部;精纽、登韵、作滕切。\\ncéng 从纽、蒸部;从纽、登韵、昨棱切。\\n\\n[glyph:g009]1 商\\n[glyph:g010]2 西周\\n[glyph:g011]3 春秋\\n[glyph:g012]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g013]5 战国\\n[glyph:g014]6 战国\\n[glyph:g015]7 汉\\n[glyph:g016]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g017]9 汉\\n曾 楷书\\n\\n1、9《甲金篆》63页。2、3、4《金文编》47页。5、6《战文编》54页。7《汉印徵》卷2，1页。8《说文》28页。\\n\\n象形字。甲骨文中之“田”(非土田之田)像釜鬲之算，“八”像散发出的蒸汽，[glyph:g018]之整体像蒸熟食物的器皿，即甑(zèng)之初文。古代的甑，底部有许多透蒸汽的小孔，置于鬲或䰝(fù，大口的釜)上蒸煮，如同现在的蒸笼。也有另外加算的。新石器时代已有陶甑，殷周时有用青铜铸成的，现在则以木制为主，也有竹制。甑、鬲合在一起便是甗，甗之甑、鬲两部分可合铸在一起，也可上下分开，器盛行于殷、周时。西周金文中始加[glyph:g019]或[glyph:g020](“口”中加短横是古文字中常见的现象)，乃像承置甑的物件。“曾”在甲骨文中用为祭祀名、地名或方国名，金文中用作国名、地名。易鼎：“使于曾。”或用为增加的“增”。卫簋：“王曾(增)令卫，易(赐)赤巿攸勒。”或用为重义，曾孙仆儿钟有“曾孙”语。文献中多用为加强语气的副词，常与“不”连用。《列子·汤问》：“曾不若孀妻弱子。”(竟然连寡妇小孩都不如)用为这些意义的“曾”读zēng。表示“曾经”义时读céng。(陈英杰)\\n\\n尚 shàng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、漾韵、时亮切。\\ncháng 禅纽、阳部;禅纽、阳韵、市羊切。\\n\\n[glyph:g021]1 西周\\n[glyph:g022]2 西周\\n[glyph:g023]3 战国\\n[glyph:g024]4 春秋\\n[glyph:g025]5 战国\\n[glyph:g026]6 战国\\n[glyph:g027]7 战国\\n[glyph:g028]8 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]9 汉\\n[glyph:g030]10 汉\\n尚 楷书\\n\\n1-4《金文编》48页。5、6、7《战文编》55页。8《说文》28页。9《银雀山》31页。10《甲金篆》64页。\\n\\n形声字。《说文》：“尚，曾也，庶几也。从八，向声。”“曾”、“庶几”均后来义，初义不知。字形不见于殷商甲骨文，而见于先周周原甲骨。金文中多假“尚”为“常”。陈侯因𧊒敦“永为典尚”，读为《易·系辞》“既有典常”之“常”，“典常”指国之典章制度。亦可单言“尚”。秦驷玉版“世万子孙，以此为尚”，“尚”读为“常”，常规、典常之义。借为“常”时音读cháng。文献中多用为增加、饰、崇尚、好尚等义，音读shàng。陈公子叔邋父甗：“子子孙孙是尚。”《集韵·漾韵》：“尚，贵也。”颛铭中是尊尚、宝爱之义。铭文\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0076/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 76, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"8a9838943fcc6ffe088f5ff2a1a665ad89ac199b635a6cbf78f0213e691dccd8\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0077/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 77, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"6ca19028ac75861df9098ae5f4781977aca78bc0c9ab1625cc7187363d8bfba2\"}], \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"task\": \"Make the smallest supported changes that resolve the supplied findings and their directly affected fields. Preserve unaffected prose, citations, readings and graph records exactly; do not perform another general rewrite. Check that each changed claim still cites evidence supporting its precise scope.\", \"original_role\": \"editor\", \"article_contract\": {\"relationship_branches\": [{\"predicates\": [\"semantic_component_of\", \"phonetic_component_of\", \"pictorial_component_of\", \"indicator_component_of\", \"replacement_component_of\", \"empty_component_of\"], \"subject_kind\": \"component\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"variant_of\", \"simplified_from\", \"derived_from\", \"phonetic_element_in\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"has_sense\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"sense_developed_into\", \"phonetic_loan_for\"], \"subject_kind\": \"sense\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}], \"instruction\": \"These are the exact allowed relationship names and endpoint kinds. _component_of is a suffix, never a literal predicate. Unknown component roles create no invented unknown_component_of edge. Preserve scoped historical claims and supported graph links.\", \"citation_instruction\": \"Reader prose must contain no refNNN transport aliases or inline evidence ID lists. Store citations only in the containing record evidence_ids array. When editing only a text field, preserve its existing evidence_ids; if support changes, edit that array as a separate nonoverlapping path. Never append [ref001, ref002] to prose.\"}, \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"小\": \"xiǎo\", \"少\": \"shǎo\", \"雀\": \"què\"}}}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/65E5/fe206e4e1bc7417f113eebb316bb888b727352884048a845f2ecf3fb3eeee9f1/current-sense-evidence-repair/initial-followup/research/prompt.txt:171:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"日\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"The sun\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"Pictograph of the sun.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"實也。太陽之精不虧。从囗一。象形。凡日之屬皆从日。\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Characters in the same phonetic series (日) (Zhengzhang, 2003)\\nPictogram (象形) – the sun. A dot or line was added in the centre to distinguish it from visually similar characters, e.g. 囗 (early form of 丁). Compare earlier forms of the character with the astronomical and astrological symbol for the Sun (☉) and the Egyptian hieroglyph 𓇳.\\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *ku-ni(k). Cognate to Tibetan ཉི་མ (nyi ma, “sun, day”), Burmese နေ (ne, “sun”) or Burmese နေ့ (ne., “day”), S'gaw Karen နံၤ (nĩ, “day”), and Nuosu ꑍ (nyip, “day, daytime”).\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Essentially a substitute character for 入 (MC nyip, “to enter”) that preserves the expected modern pronunciation of 入; see the etymology there for more.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[5]\", \"text\": \"Possibly due to assimilation of 金 (kim) and 日 (nyit). Note that 金 ends with /m/ in Old and Middle Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"實也。太陽之精不虧。从囗一。象形。凡日之屬皆从日。\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"sun; day; daytime\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"jat6\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"HI KA HIBI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"NICHI JITSU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"IL\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"rì\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*njit\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"nhật\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"sun; day\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"0404a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"nyit\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*C.nik\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*njiɡ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*njiɡ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*njiɡ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"人質\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"日開三真入\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"njiɡ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"質\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Day; Sun; Japan; Counter For Days\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E12\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"ひ -び -か\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"ニチ ジツ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E13\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[6]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: pictographic\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X-250e463a2539465a5a89\", \"source\": \"Multi-function Chinese Character Database, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"Oracle-bone character entry; paleographic account and cited inscriptions\", \"text\": \"The database describes 日 as a depiction of the sun, with early round forms sometimes squared because a circle was difficult to carve on bone; it says the central mark distinguished the graph from 囗. It cites Shang oracle-bone examples for the sun and sunrise (合集34163), eclipse (合集11480), daylight contrasted with evening (合集33871, 合集34036), and a period of five days (合集12314). It also notes later bronze-inscription uses for the sun, daylight, and calendar-day unit. The inscription citations substantiate distinct early uses; they do not by themselves establish the order in which those senses developed.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"primary paleographic dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%E6%97%A5\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：日\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-370de597954516dde8e1\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Chinese Character Database\", \"field\": \"Character evolution page\", \"text\": \"The entry provides examples under oracle-bone, bronze, Chu manuscripts, seal script, and Shuowen headings, and states that 日 resembles the sun and its original meaning is the sun. The page identifies a bronze example as inscription 集成05990. Its displayed categories support a broad sequence of attested script traditions, but the accessible text does not supply dates for each individual form.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"paleographic reference\", \"url\": \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=12810\", \"title\": \"漢字「日」：字形演變\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5d0fbd238926cfe78761\", \"source\": \"Shuowen.org, digitized 說文解字 with 段玉裁 commentary\", \"field\": \"卷七 日部 entry and commentary\", \"text\": \"Xu Shen's entry defines 日 as 實, describes it as 太陽之精不虧, analyzes it as 从囗一 and calls it pictographic. Duan Yucai explains the outer shape as the sun's outline and the inner stroke as its undiminished center. This is the received Han-era account, not direct evidence for the graph's earliest form; the oracle-bone material provides earlier evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"historical lexicographic text\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"title\": \"說文解字「日」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d3508a2f7335b0937cb4\", \"source\": \"Unicode Consortium\", \"field\": \"Unihan database documentation and lookup behavior\", \"text\": \"Unicode's Unihan documentation says the database distributes character data including readings, describes Unihan field files and their tab-separated record format, and directs users to the interactive lookup interface. A direct character-specific lookup for 日 was attempted but timed out; therefore this documentation does not verify the kMandarin value itself. The supplied local Unihan row is 日 kMandarin rì, but it is not emitted here as externally verified evidence.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"official database documentation; lookup attempt limitation\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-32.html\", \"title\": \"UAX #38: Unicode Han Database (Unihan)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b8ed50fda7977ca28806\", \"source\": \"Repository-provided local primary readings\", \"field\": \"sources/baxter-sagart/baxtersagart.tsv, row 日; sources/unihan/Unihan_Readings.txt, kMandarin row 日\", \"text\": \"The supplied Baxter–Sagart row is 日, Mandarin rì, Middle Chinese nyit, Old Chinese *C.nik, gloss “sun; day”; these are the dataset's distinct MC and OC columns. The supplied Unihan row is 日 kMandarin rì. These exact local rows were checked, but the character-specific Unicode record URL timed out, so these repository rows cannot be treated as externally verified or as click-through evidence. As this character is a pictogram and no phonetic component is proposed, neither reading provides evidence for a sound-component relationship.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"local structured reading data; external lookup unavailable\", \"url\": \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65E5\", \"title\": \"Unihan character lookup for U+65E5 (attempted; timeout)\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d07fe345d8ba134188a4\", \"source\": \"Multi-function Chinese Character Database, CUHK\", \"field\": \"形義通解: glyph account and oracle-bone citations\", \"text\": \"The entry explains that 日 depicts the sun, with early round forms sometimes rendered square because circles were difficult to carve on bone; it says the central mark distinguished 日 from 囗. It cites Shang oracle-bone uses for the sun and sunrise (合集34163), eclipse (合集11480), daylight contrasted with evening (合集33871, 合集34036), and a five-day period (合集12314). It separately cites 日 as a sacrificial name (合集27463). These inscriptions establish multiple early uses but do not establish their order of development.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"primary paleographic dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E6%97%A5\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：日\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-e82d9fcdff2768470a3a\", \"source\": \"Multi-function Chinese Character Database, CUHK\", \"field\": \"形義通解: bronze inscriptions and later use\", \"text\": \"The entry reports bronze-inscription use for the sun, daylight opposed to night, and calendar-day expressions such as 今日 and 翌日; it also discusses 日 before calendrical names in ancestor designations. These examples show continued and expanded use across inscriptional contexts, but the record does not date each item individually.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"paleographic reference\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E6%97%A5\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫：日\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-47e1489aeebec25d5bc4\", \"source\": \"Shuowen.org, digitized 說文解字 with 段玉裁 commentary\", \"field\": \"卷七 日部 entry and commentary\", \"text\": \"The received Shuowen entry glosses 日 as 實, calls it an image, and analyzes it as 从囗一; Duan Yucai interprets the outline as the sun's circumference and the inner stroke as its undiminished center. This is a Han-era lexicographic explanation and commentary, not direct evidence of the graph's earliest structure.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"historical lexicographic text\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"title\": \"說文解字「日」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-1a1e262d946c011f6980\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File description, source attribution and reuse rights\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies 日-oracle.svg as a modern Inkscape vector redraw of the Shang oracle-script character 日. It links the depicted glyph to Xiaoxuetang record 42.ED48, noting 鐵180.2 合5216, and marks the file public domain/CC0. This is an educational redraw, not a photograph or scan of the ancient inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"glyph source and rights record\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%97%A5-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:日-oracle.svg - Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-49de47e306602ea7d0d1\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Original file link on exact File description page\", \"text\": \"The exact Commons description page links the original SVG at https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/%E6%97%A5-oracle.svg. Use as a direct glyph image candidate; the page identifies the drawing as Shang oracle script, with the underlying Xiaoxuetang candidate labelled record 42.ED48.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"direct glyph image URL provenance\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%97%A5-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:日-oracle.svg - Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-27\"}, {\"id\": \"X-616f6da2f383953bf51b\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File description, source attribution and reuse rights\", \"text\": \"The page identifies 日-seal.svg as a modern Inkscape vector rendering of 日 in Shuowen seal-script style, gives Xiaoxuetang record 27.65E5 as a source, and states the file is CC0/public domain. It is a modern redraw, not an ancient artifact image.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"glyph source and rights record\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%97%A5-seal.svg\", \"title\":\n... 673525 bytes omitted ...\nshow Japan abbreviation is a Chinese dictionary sense, though it is distinct from the basic sun/day account.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"official dictionary\", \"url\": \"https://dict.revised.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=9199&la=0&powerMode=0\", \"title\": \"教育部《重編國語辭典修訂本》：日\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-03\"}, {\"id\": \"ref052\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"Exact file description, depiction, source attribution, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The page describes 日-bronze.svg as a modern vector depicting 日 in Western Zhou bronze-script style and cites Xiaoxuetang record 32.Eref008F, associated there with 師虎簋 (mid Western Zhou). The Commons page states CC0/public-domain terms for the vector. Its pixels were not visually inspected in this invocation: the direct SVG open failed because the web tool does not render that content type. Do not treat the vessel attribution as proof that the vector exactly matches a particular inscription or infer its mark’s function from the image.\", \"record_character\": \"日\", \"kind\": \"glyph provenance and rights record\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:日-bronze.svg - Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-10-03\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Base evidence consists of imported local JSONL excerpts. Source names identify upstream datasets; extraction may lose sense and language boundaries. Further external evidence and research activity are documented separately in the dossier.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"pictographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": null, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"日 oracle bone bronze script paleography Shuowen original form sun character; 日 甲骨文 金文 字形 演变 日 字源 古文字; 日 說文解字 實也 太陽之精不虧 从囗一 甲骨文; 日 ancient Chinese meanings earliest attestation day sun oracle bone inscriptions\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=12810\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/4300hh/\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?char=%ref006%97%A5&if=gb\", \"https://www11.iih.sinica.edu.tw/storage/w2_file/3787xaTSfNM.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the Academia Sinica evolution page and Shuowen entry. Ctext returned 403 on open. The primary paleographic dictionary query returned Shang inscription references for sun, sunrise, eclipse, daylight, and day-unit uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl 日 U+65ref005 Unihan rì; site:unicode.org/charts/unihan 日 U+65ref005 kMandarin; Baxter Sagart 日 *C.nik nyit 2014 reconstruction; 日 oracle bone inscriptions used for day date Shang occurrences meaning sun day\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr38/tr38-32.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihangridindex.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/UnihanGrid.pl?codepoint=6500\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65ref005\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected Unicode Unihan documentation, the Unicode grid index and the CUHK character entry. Direct U+65ref005 lookup was attempted twice and failed, first as inaccessible and then as a timeout. Baxter–Sagart reading checked against exact supplied local row; no independently inspected external table page was located.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 oracle bone inscriptions paleography sun daylight day unit CUHK multi-function Chinese character database\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located CUHK's character entry. Opened it and inspected the paleographic account, cited Shang oracle-bone inscriptions and bronze-use discussion.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 ancient Chinese character oracle bone bronze evolution sun pictograph Academia Sinica Xiaoxuetang\", \"urls\": [\"https://ascdc.sinica.edu.tw/en/single_news_page.jsp?newsId=415\", \"https://ascdc.sinica.edu.tw/en/resources.jsp\", \"https://evoread.ascdc.sinica.edu.tw/detail_4.html\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected Academia Sinica database overview pages for coverage and period scope; no character-specific record retrieved in this query.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: 日-seal.svg 日 seal script\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected exact Commons file page, its direct original SVG link, redraw provenance, source references and license terms.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: \\\"Shuowen Seal Radical 231.svg\\\"\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"Search surfaced the 日 category lead. Direct open attempt for the named file failed, so it was not selected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Commons \\\"日-oracle.svg\\\"\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search found and opened the exact Commons page; verified script-style identification, Xiaoxuetang record label, modern redraw status, CC0 terms and original SVG link.\"}, {\"query\": \"\\\"27.65ref005\\\" 日 Xiaoxuetang 日 字形\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\"], \"outcome\": \"The search did not return a character-specific Xiaoxuetang record page; inspected the official CC0 notice. Commons page supplies 27.65ref005 as a source label.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65ref005 Unihan 日\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.unicode.org/charts/unihan.html\", \"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65ref005\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the official Unicode Unihan lookup overview and attempted the U+65ref005 record directly; direct lookup remained inaccessible.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:shuowen.org/view/4170 說文解字 日 實也\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the digitized Shuowen entry and Duan Yucai commentary for 日.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 ancient Chinese character oracle bone bronze glyph image Wikimedia Commons alternative file; 日 甲骨文 字形 小學堂 日 古文字 字形演變\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/jiaguwen\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bigseal.svg\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK's character entry, Shuowen entry, Xiaoxuetang database landing page, rights notice, and the existing Commons oracle-file page. The existing oracle candidate is excluded because the supplied acquisition result says its direct image URL returned 404. Xiaoxuetang landing page is accessible, but the query interface returned 401 in the supplied results and no character-specific image was retrieved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File: 日-bigseal.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bigseal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the exact Commons file page and verified the modern redraw description, Liushutong style label, public-domain claim, and the page's Original file link. Opening the linked direct image URL failed with a cache-miss access error.\"}, {\"query\": \"Direct official Unihan character lookup for 日 U+65ref005\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65ref005\"], \"outcome\": \"The official character-specific page was inaccessible through the web tool.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 甲骨文 金文 字形演變 日 sun oracle bone bronze Academia Sinica CUHK\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=12810\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5&sub=1\"], \"outcome\": \"Search surfaced the Academia Sinica evolution page and CUHK oracle-bone component page; both were opened and inspected. CUHK's fuller character entry was accessed in a later direct open.\"}, {\"query\": \"日-seal.svg Commons File 日 oracle sun seal glyph imageinfo\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/char?fontcode=27.65ref005\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/xiaozhuan?char=%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the exact Commons page and its linked Xiaoxuetang glyph record, small-seal database page, and rights notice. Commons page identifies the SVG as a modern Shuowen-seal-style vector and states CC0; no image-pixel inspection was achieved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 日 甲骨文 日出 日五日\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CUHK character entry and inspected the glyph explanation, oracle-bone inscription citations, bronze-use discussion, and Shuowen analysis.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 Shuowen 說文 實也 从囗一 段玉裁\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"The Shuowen.org page opened without readable text in the tool. CUHK's character page supplied the received Shuowen quotation and commentary on its interpretation.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuowen_Seal_Radical_231.svg 日\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuowen_Seal_Radical_231.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"The exact candidate page was attempted but inaccessible; its local metadata lead was not counted as verified evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 kMandarin Unihan U+65ref005 Unicode\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.unicode.org/cgi-bin/GetUnihanData.pl?codepoint=65ref005\"], \"outcome\": \"Search surfaced third-party mirrors, but the official Unicode character lookup was attempted and inaccessible. No mirror was used as substitute for the required official character record.\"}, {\"query\": \"日-seal.svg Wikimedia Commons File 日 seal\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the exact Commons page. Its description identifies the modern seal-script redraw, source record, and CC0 terms; the file was not selected because the supplied acquisition findings mark its exact image URL unavailable.\"}, {\"query\": \"\\\"Shuowen Seal Radical 231.svg\\\" 日 Commons\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category%3A%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuowen_Seal_Radical_231.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"The category search showed a candidate filename, but opening its exact description page returned an internal error. Its identity, provenance, and rights were not verified, so it was not selected.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 oracle bone glyph image Xiaoxuetang 日 字形\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/char?fontcode=32.Eref008F\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the exact Commons bronze-file page, the Xiaoxuetang record it cites, and the official rights notice. The exact Commons oracle file page was also opened, but that image URL was marked unavailable in the supplied acquisition findings and was excluded. The Commons original-image link for the bronze SVG was verified; the web tool could not render the SVG endpoint.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 日 oracle script\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category%3A%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results exposed other 日 image filenames in the category. No additional file was selected without an inspected exact description page and confirmed provenance.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 日 甲骨文 太阳 日出 卜辞; 日 甲骨文 字形 本义 太阳 甲骨文合集 日 昼 五日 文字学; 日 字源 象形 甲骨文 圆形 方折 中间一点 古文字\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5&start=20&sub=1\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5&start=20\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/oraclePiece.php?piece=%ref006%97%A5&start=10\", \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://yuzhen.weebly.com/uploads/3/0/0/9/30093497/.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results led to the CUHK character entry. Opened the character page and read its glyph account, oracle-bone examples, bronze-use discussion, and Shuowen section.\"}, {\"query\": \"李學勤 字源 2012 天津古籍出版社 辽宁人民出版社 ISBN 9787552800692\", \"urls\": [\"https://ci.nii.ac.jp/ncid/BB13266817\", \"https://scalar.usc.edu/works/chinese-studies-reference-collection/media/PL1281%20.Z53%202012%20%ref005%AD%97%ref006%BA%90_OCR.pdf\", \"https://opac.sdx.sh.cn/ILASOPAC/NTRdrBookRetr.do?LeftPubyearKey=2012&LeftPubyearType=pubyear&LeftSubjectKey=%ref006%B1%89%ref005%AD%97&LeftSubjectType=subject&LeftTypeKey=0&LeftTypeType=type&PageNum=10&ResearchSearchType=null&SearchKey=%ref006%B1%89%ref002%80%A6%ref002%80%A6&SearchType=text&leftPublishKey=%ref005%A4%A9%ref006%B4%A5%ref005%8F%A4%ref007%B1%8D%ref005%87%BA%ref007%89%88%ref007%A4%BE&leftPublishType=publish&researchSearchKey=null&searchWay=searchWayPrv\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the CiNii catalog record and checked editor, publisher, year, ISBN, and printing information. The local scan of the cited entry was inspected separately; catalog metadata is not treated as support for its etymological claims.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 oracle bone bronze script paleography Shuowen original form sun character; 日 甲骨文 金文 字形 演变 日 字源 古文字; 日 說文解字 實也 太陽之精不虧 从囗一 甲骨文; 日 ancient Chinese meanings earliest attestation day sun oracle bone inscriptions\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=12810\", \"https://www.guoxuedashi.com/zixing/yanbian/4300hh/\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/4170\", \"https://ctext.org/dictionary.pl?char=%ref006%97%A5&if=gb\", \"https://www11.iih.sinica.edu.tw/storage/w2_file/3787xaTSfNM.pdf\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the Academia Sinica evolution page and the Shuowen entry with Duan commentary. Other URLs in the search result set were not used as evidence.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 日 太阳 白昼 日 unit 形义通解; 现代汉语词典 日 太阳 白天 词典 日条目 在线; 日字 甲骨文 太阳 本义 字形 中国哲学书电子化计划 字典\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%ref006%97%A5\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated hits; opened CUHK 日 entry directly and inspected its glyph account and inscription examples.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:zdic.net 日 太阳 白天 字典 日的解释; site:dict.revised.moe.edu.tw 日 太陽 白晝 日 辭典; 汉语大词典 日 太阳 白天 释义 日\", \"urls\": [\"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/char/12810\", \"https://dict.revised.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=9199&la=0&powerMode=0\", \"https://dict.revised.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=9013\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened Academia Sinica character record and the Ministry of Education dictionary entry, both of which list present-day sun/daytime senses. A different attempted MOE URL returned a cache miss and was not used.\"}, {\"query\": \"Direct inspection of supplied attachment pixels and provisional 字源 corpus, headword 日; Wikimedia Commons File:日-bronze.svg and direct SVG\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Directly inspected attached scan images for PDF pages 612 and 613; decoded RGB pixel hashes match the supplied source_pixel_sha256 values. Page 612 contains the 日 headword and its complete passage; page 613 continues 時 then begins 早. Opened the exact Commons page; direct SVG URL returned an unsupported content-type error, so no vector pixel inspection was achieved.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The accessible evidence gives Shang-period inscription identifiers but not a securely dated first occurrence for each distinct use; exact inscription datings remain unverified here.\", \"Semantic ordering should not be inferred from the dictionary's gloss sequence. The primary paleographic reference documents sun, daylight, and day-unit uses in oracle-bone inscriptions, but does not establish a precise transition date from one sense to another.\", \"The reason for the central mark distinguishing 日 from 囗 is a source's paleographic explanation; possible histories of individual graphic variants or scribal changes beyond this broad sequence were not established.\", \"The received Shuowen component analysis 从囗一 is a historical interpretation. Earlier inscriptional evidence supports a sun pictogram but does not independently establish that its original structure consisted of semantic components 囗 plus 一.\", \"Unihan's official character-specific lookup failed to load, so the exact upstream kMandarin value rì could not be verified through the requested click-through. The local row is available and checked but is not external evidence.\", \"No sound component applies to this graph. Dossier claims about a whole-graph loan for 入 or assimilation involving 金 concern other lexical/graphical uses and were not established as historical explanations of 日's sun/day graph.\", \"The CUHK entry cites Shang oracle-bone catalogue numbers but the exact inscriptions' archaeological dates were not verified here; treat them as Shang-period attestations, not securely dated first occurrences.\", \"The early graph-to-sun identification is supported by paleographic interpretation; precise stages of rounding, squaring and central-mark formation are not individually dated. The described square forms reflect carving constraints according to the source, but no sequence of particular variants is established.\", \"The oracle-bone evidence attests sun, daylight, day-unit and sacrificial-name uses but does not establish a chronology among these senses or the semantic links between them. Do not derive chronology from dictionary gloss order.\", \"Shuowen's 从囗一 segmentation is a received historical analysis. The earlier evidence supports a sun pictogram but does not independently establish 囗 and 一 as original graphic components.\", \"The Unihan direct character record did not load; local data lists kMandarin rì and Baxter–Sagart MC nyit, OC *C.nik, but those local rows are not external evidence in this result.\", \"The exact Xiaoxuetang character record page was not independently opened in this run. Selected Commons glyph candidates cite their record labels; the official Xiaoxuetang rights notice was verified separately. No whole-graph loan or phonetic component claim is relevant to the sun/day graph.\", \"CUHK cites Shang-period oracle-bone catalogue identifiers for several uses but does not provide verified dates for each inscription here. Treat these as Shang-period attestations, not securely dated first occurrences.\", \"The cited evidence does not establish whether sun, daylight, day-unit, or sacrificial-name uses arose first, nor does it establish all semantic links among them. Dictionary gloss order is not chronology.\", \"The early graph is interpreted as a sun depiction. The received Shuowen segmentation 从囗一 is not independently established by the earlier inscriptional evidence as the graph's original component structure.\", \"The official Unihan character-specific lookup failed; the local kMandarin row is not emitted as externally verified evidence.\", \"The supplied oracle and seal candidates were marked unavailable after HTTP 404 in image acquisition and are excluded. The alternative Liushutong redraw's exact Commons page and direct image link were verified, but opening the direct image URL returned a cache miss. No image is proposed because a usable alternative asset was not established.\", \"The CUHK entry cites Shang oracle-bone catalogue numbers but this research did not independently verify dates for each individual inscription; treat them as Shang-period examples, not securely dated first attestations.\", \"The evidence attests sun, daylight, day-unit, and sacrificial-name uses in oracle-bone contexts but does not establish their relative chronology or all semantic transitions. Later Chinese and Japanese sense histories were not researched beyond the cited inscriptional and dictionary data.\", \"Early forms are interpreted as a sun depiction. The received Shuowen segmentation 从囗一 does not independently establish that the graph originally consisted of those components.\", \"The seal candidate has verified descriptive provenance and license but no visual inspection of its pixels. The alternative exact Commons candidate page was inaccessible. No image is selected on this evidence.\", \"The official Unihan record could not be opened; kMandarin rì remains supported only by the supplied local row and was not emitted as externally verified evidence.\", \"No component sound analysis applies: 日 is treated here as a whole-graph sun pictogram. Dossier statements about loans or 金 assimilation were not established as explanations of this graph's sun/day history.\", \"The selected candidate is a modern vector redraw of a form attributed to the mid Western Zhou 師虎簋, not an image of the vessel itself. The web tool confirmed the original SVG link but could not render the SVG endpoint; the image pixels have not been inspected in this research stage.\", \"The exact Commons page for Shuowen Seal Radical 231.svg could not be opened, so its identity and licensing remain unverified. The oracle and seal image URLs identified as unavailable in the supplied acquisition findings were not retried.\", \"The 字源 scan for printed p. 599 / PDF p. 612 was inspected at the 日 headword and checked through the end of that entry. The scan's quoted Shuowen segmentation appears as 从口、一; the OCR also reads 口. The scan alone does not resolve whether the printed glyph encodes 口 or 囗, while the separately inspected Shuowen text gives 从囗一. Do not silently normalize this occurrence; retain the source-bound image pending independent glyph-identity verification.\", \"The cited Shang inscription references support early uses for sun, daylight, day-unit, and a sacrificial name, but the inspected sources do not establish their relative chronology, exact dates for each occurrence, or all semantic links among them.\", \"[SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] 字源, PDF p. 612 / printed p. 599, Shuowen quotation span “从口、一”: the printed enclosure appears 口-shaped, while other consulted transcriptions read 从囗一. The scan was inspected, but its pixels do not settle whether the intended printed graph is 口 or 囗; retain this occurrence as unresolved rather than normalizing it.\", \"The listed oracle-bone examples are catalogued as Shang-period uses by CUHK, but exact dates for individual inscriptions and a sequence among the sun, daylight, day-unit, and sacrificial-name senses were not independently verified. Avoid calling any one use the first-ever sense or inferring semantic transitions from a gloss list.\", \"The selected Commons file’s description and rights were inspected, but the SVG image itself could not be rendered by the web tool during this run. Its shape description and exact match to the cited 師虎簋 inscription remain unverified by pixel inspection.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"日-western-zhou-bronze\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:日-bronze.svg - Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Mid Western Zhou attribution for the underlying form; the selected file is a modern drawing.\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription form; modern vector redraw.\", \"caption\": \"This rounded rectangular outline encloses a filled oval in a bronze-script form of 日 attributed to a mid-Western Zhou vessel. The drawing shows the later visible form; it does not establish how the earliest graph was made.\", \"alt\": \"A thick, rounded rectangular black outline surrounds a smaller filled black oval.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The clear outline and enclosed mark make this attributed bronze-script form useful for viewing 日’s shape. It is a modern redraw, not an image of the vessel, and does not by itself establish the graph’s earliest structure.\", \"rights\": \"CC0 1.0, as stated on the Commons file page; Xiaoxuetang query-interface glyph image and attribute data are also CC0 1.0 under its official notice.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref028\", \"ref040\", \"ref041\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This attributed form does not establish when the rounded outline or central mark developed, or the detailed sequence of graphic changes.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref041\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"日-seal.svg Wikimedia Commons File 日 seal\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the exact Commons page. Its description identifies the modern seal-script redraw, source record, and CC0 terms; the file was not selected because the supplied acquisition findings mark its exact image URL unavailable.\"}, {\"query\": \"\\\"Shuowen Seal Radical 231.svg\\\" 日 Commons\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category%3A%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shuowen_Seal_Radical_231.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"The category search showed a candidate filename, but opening its exact description page returned an internal error. Its identity, provenance, and rights were not verified, so it was not selected.\"}, {\"query\": \"日 oracle bone glyph image Xiaoxuetang 日 字形\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/char?fontcode=32.Eref008F\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the exact Commons bronze-file page, the Xiaoxuetang record it cites, and the official rights notice. The exact Commons oracle file page was also opened, but that image URL was marked unavailable in the supplied acquisition findings and was excluded. The Commons original-image link for the bronze SVG was verified; the web tool could not render the SVG endpoint.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File 日 oracle script\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category%3A%ref006%97%A5\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results exposed other 日 image filenames in the category. No additional file was selected without an inspected exact description page and confirmed provenance.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The selected candidate is a modern vector redraw of a form attributed to the mid Western Zhou 師虎簋, not an image of the vessel itself. The web tool confirmed the original SVG link but could not render the SVG endpoint; the image pixels have not been inspected in this research stage.\", \"The exact Commons page for Shuowen Seal Radical 231.svg could not be opened, so its identity and licensing remain unverified. The oracle and seal image URLs identified as unavailable in the supplied acquisition findings were not retried.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"日-western-zhou-bronze\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"f38c8e118185e7caa5a66faa1fb2fcf0d71403af3ffc2fc35bef72b375084fb6\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/f38c8e118185e7caa5a66faa1fb2fcf0d71403af3ffc2fc35bef72b375084fb6.svg\", \"byte_length\": 915}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"日\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"日 is a picture of the sun. Shang-period inscriptions also attest it for daylight and as a unit of days, though they do not establish which use came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"Pictographic: the whole graph depicts the sun. Early forms could be rounded or squared, and a central mark is described as distinguishing 日 from 囗. The later Shuowen analysis divides it into 囗 and 一, but earlier evidence does not establish those as original components.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref016\", \"ref028\"], \"type\": \"pictographic\"}, \"components\": [], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Oracle-bone and bronze examples attest 日 in early inscriptional traditions. The documented script categories include oracle-bone, bronze, manuscript, and seal forms, but the evidence does not date every form or establish a detailed sequence of graphic changes.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref020\"]}, {\"text\": \"The received Han-era Shuowen account analyzes 日 as 从囗一, interpreting the outline as the sun and the inner stroke as its undiminished center. This is a later explanation, not direct evidence for the graph's earliest structure.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref016\", \"ref021\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"Shang-period inscriptions attest uses for the sun, daylight, a five-day period, and a sacrificial name. Their relative order and semantic connections are not established; exact dates for individual inscriptions are also unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref019\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}, {\"text\": \"The evidence does not establish how each detail of the early graph formed or provide a dated sequence for its rounded and squared forms and central mark.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"日:sun\", \"gloss\": \"sun\", \"period\": \"Shang-period oracle-bone inscriptions\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"Oracle-bone examples attest 日 for the sun. This is a use in the earliest documented corpus cited here, not proof of the graph's first-ever use.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}, {\"id\": \"日:daylight\", \"gloss\": \"daylight\", \"period\": \"Shang-period oracle-bone inscriptions\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"Oracle-bone examples use 日 for daylight, including in contrast with evening. Whether this use developed from the sun sense is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"日:day_unit\", \"gloss\": \"day as a calendrical unit\", \"period\": \"Shang-period oracle-bone inscriptions\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example attests 日 for a five-day period. The evidence does not establish when or how this use relates to the sun and daylight uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"日:sacrificial_name\", \"gloss\": \"sacrificial name\", \"period\": \"Shang-period oracle-bone inscriptions\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"The cited corpus includes 日 as a sacrificial name. Its relation, if any, to the sun, daylight, or day-unit uses is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref019\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"日:japan\", \"gloss\": \"Japan\", \"period\": \"dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"日 is also used in Japanese writing for Japan; the evidence supplied does not document the history of this use.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref011\"]}, {\"id\": \"日:days_counter\", \"gloss\": \"counter for days\", \"period\": \"dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"Japanese dictionary data records 日 as a counter for days. The evidence supplied does not document when this use arose or how it developed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref011\"]}], \"developments\": [], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The inscriptions document several Shang-period uses but do not establish their relative chronology or transitions. Their shared early attestation does not show that one sense developed from another.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref019\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}, {\"text\": \"Modern readings and dictionary glosses alone do not establish a chronology or semantic path for later uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref008\", \"ref009\", \"ref011\", \"ref012\", \"ref043\"]}]}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"meaning:日:sun\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:sun\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"Oracle-bone examples attest 日 for the sun. This is a use in the earliest documented corpus cited here, not proof of the graph's first-ever use.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\", \"ref043\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:日:daylight\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:daylight\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"Oracle-bone examples use 日 for daylight, including in contrast with evening. Whether this use developed from the sun sense is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:日:day_unit\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:day_unit\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"An oracle-bone example attests 日 for a five-day period. The evidence does not establish when or how this use relates to the sun and daylight uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:日:sacrificial_name\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:sacrificial_name\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"The cited corpus includes 日 as a sacrificial name. Its relation, if any, to the sun, daylight, or day-unit uses is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref019\", \"ref028\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:日:japan\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:japan\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"日 is also used in Japanese writing for Japan; the evidence supplied does not document the history of this use.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref011\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:日:days_counter\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"日\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"日:days_counter\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"日\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"Japanese dictionary data records 日 as a counter for days. The evidence supplied does not document when this use arose or how it developed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref011\"]}], \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"日 is a picture of the sun. Early inscriptions also use it for daylight and for days counted as units; they do not show which use came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref028\"]}, \"components\": [], \"takeaway\": null}, \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"日-western-zhou-bronze\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%ref006%97%A5-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:日-bronze.svg - Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Mid Western Zhou attribution for the underlying form; the selected file is a modern drawing.\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription form; modern vector redraw.\", \"caption\": \"This bronze-script form of 日, attributed to a mid-Western Zhou vessel, has a rounded rectangular outline enclosing a filled oval. Its central mark and enclosing shape illustrate the sun-pictogram interpretation; the drawing does not establish the graph’s earliest structure.\", \"alt\": \"A thick, rounded rectangular black outline surrounds a smaller filled black oval.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible outline and central mark clearly illustrate the sun-pictogram interpretation. This is a modern redraw of a form attributed to the 師虎簋, not an image of the vessel itself.\", \"rights\": \"CC0 1.0, as stated on the Commons file page; Xiaoxuetang query-interface glyph image and attribute data are also CC0 1.0 under its official notice.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref028\", \"ref040\", \"ref041\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"This attributed form does not establish when the rounded outline or central mark developed, or the detailed sequence of graphic changes.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref015\", \"ref041\"]}]}}, \"feedback\": {\"reuse_existing_glyph_candidates\": true, \"source_enrichment\": {\"source\": {\"id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"producer_root\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books\", \"availability\": \"provisional_ocr\", \"instructions\": \"Search simplified and traditional forms; verify every used passage against source scans. Report exact scan-verified OCR errors for source-bound correction. Preserve competing scholarly analyses and unresolved printed glyph identities. Other acquired books are not digitised consumer sources yet.\"}, \"corpus_instructions\": \"Search this supplied corpus directly; verify candidate passages against source scans where possible.\", \"preserve_existing_evidence\": true, \"record_ocr_uncertainty\": \"Do not guess OCR corrections. Report the exact suspect page/span and why it needs scan verification in the research gaps; do not silently normalize it.\"}, \"task\": \"Research this already published character with the supplied local scholarly source. Search and inspect the supplied corpus, verify the actual headword and relevant passage against scan images when available, and compare the book with other authoritative references. Preserve existing supported evidence and article content unless a precise source-backed improvement is warranted. Keep uncertainty scoped and glyph assets intact. Cite at least one relevant finding from the named book with its exact page; In the authored article, cite the retained book evidence ID on the specific supported claim; placing a new book record only in the dossier is not article enrichment. Use evidence_ids arrays, never inline labels. If the book corroborates existing prose, preserve that prose and add only the supported citation before fresh reviews. if no relevant claim can be established, record a source-specific access or relevance gap. For each unresolved OCR or scan identity issue, add a gap beginning exactly [SCAN VERIFICATION REQUIRED] or [OCR CORRECTION REQUIRED] and state page, span, and reason.\", \"source_leads\": [{\"source_id\": \"ziyuan-2012\", \"title\": \"字源\", \"bibliography\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社 / 遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692\", \"book_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721\", \"search_forms\": [\"日\"], \"corpus_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/source-corpus/ziyuan-pages.jsonl\", \"candidates\": [{\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000612\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 612, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0612/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"1cadbd65dde8f6adfcace48a424487db09d7d35246851239125bdef976e4197c\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"b5144377551d1f566dc3d54c29188234fcd60ec9ac67bdd0d221921b17e26578\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"unverified_headword_line\", \"headword_lines\": [\"日 rì 日纽、质部;日纽、质韵、人质切。\"], \"excerpt\": \"易·睽》：“遇主于巷。”有时也指门外为“巷”。《诗·郑风·丰》：“俟我乎巷兮。”毛传：“巷，门外也。”有时也泛指州闾。《礼记·祭义》：“强不犯弱，众不暴寡，而弟达乎州巷矣。”郑玄注：“巷，犹闾也。”以上诸义都念 xiàng。今矿山所用的小隧道也称为“巷道”，此则音 hàng。(王志平)\\n\\n日 部\\n\\n日 rì 日纽、质部;日纽、质韵、人质切。\\n[glyph:g007]¹——[glyph:g008]² [glyph:g010]⁴ [glyph:g013]⁷\\n商 商 西周 战国\\n[glyph:g009]³——[glyph:g011]⁵——[glyph:g012]⁶——[glyph:g014]⁸\\n商 西周 春秋 战国\\n[glyph:g015]⁹——[glyph:g016]¹⁰——[glyph:g017]¹¹——[glyph:g018]¹²——日\\n古文 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1、4、5《金文编》455页。2、3《甲文编》283页。6《金文编》456页。7《先秦货币》99页。8《楚系简帛》560页。9、10《说文》137页。11、12《篆隶表》441页。\\n象形字。初文像太阳之形，后由于书写不便，笔画由圆转变为方折，象形之意渐失。“日”的本义就是太阳。甲骨文:“戊子贞:日有哉，告于河。”(《合集》33699)意思是太阳有黑点之义。《说文》:“日，实也。太阳之精不亏。从口、一，象形。”太阳本身是个发光体，所以日光也称为“日”。由于地球绕太阳运行一周为一个恒星日，也就是一天，所以一个昼夜也称为“日”。后来词义缩小了，专指狭义的“白昼”为“日”，以与“夜”相对。后又引申为时候、时代、日子。又因为“日”的唯一性，所谓“天无二日，人无二主”，所以“日”也用于比喻君主。《广雅·释诂一》:“日，君也。”(王志平)\\n\\n旻 mín 明纽、文部;明纽、真韵、武巾切。\\n[glyph:g019]¹——[glyph:g020]²——旻\\n《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《说文》137页。2《篆隶表》441页。\\n形声字。《说文》:“旻，秋天也。从日，文声。《虞书》曰:仁闵覆下则称旻天。”“文”与“闵”、“旻”接近，故从之。“闵”有悲悯的意思。《尔雅·释天》:“秋为旻天。”郭璞注:“旻，犹愍也。愍万物彫落。”《书·大禹谟》:“日号泣于旻天。”孔传:“仁覆愍下谓之旻天。”(王志平)\\n\\n時(时) shí 禅纽、之部;禅纽、之韵、市之切。\\n[glyph:g021]¹ [glyph:g023]³——[glyph:g024]⁴——[glyph:g025]⁵\\n商 战国 战国《说文》古文\\n[glyph:g026]⁶\\n战国\\n[glyph:g022]² [glyph:g027]⁷——[glyph:g028]⁸\\n春秋 战国 《说文》小篆\\n[glyph:g029]⁹——[glyph:g030]¹⁰——[glyph:g031]¹¹——時——时\\n汉 汉 汉 楷书 楷书\\n1《甲文编》628页。2、7《类编》494页。3《金文编》456页。4《楚系简帛》565页。5、8《说文》137页。6《战文编》455页。9《睡甲》103页。10《银雀山》237页。11《篆隶表》442页。\\n形声字。甲骨文作“[glyph:g021]”，从日，之声。或以为“之日”的合文。后添加了“又”字，“之”声也演变为“寺”声。石鼓文《车工》:“即适即時。”“時”假借为“塒”。《说文》:“時，四时也。从日，寺声。”而“时”是“時”的简化字，从日，从寸，成为了会意字，已失去了形声字的表音功能。“時”字的基本义就是一年四季中的某一季。“時”有大、\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000615\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 615, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0615/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"11d997c4fe37457754b223c6fd5b7218a3f2bbe3fa0e3eda194413c788ce0ea4\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"617cd1f5bdc09f099fff2d67935cf704f57338830cfaf7ca1b7d07782f01d226\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"match_type\": \"text_mention\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"日部\\n\\n啓 qǐ 溪纽、脂部; 溪纽、荠韵、康礼切。\\nqiàn 溪纽、脂部; 溪纽、线韵、去战切。\\n[glyph:g001]¹ — [glyph:g002]² — 啓\\n商 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1《甲文编》284页。2《说文》138页。\\n形声字。初文从日, 攴声。“攴”是“啓”字的初文, 像以手开门之意。甲骨文“啓”字, 所从声旁也有不省“口”而径作“啓”字者。故《说文》云:“啓, 雨而昼绁也。从日, 啓省声。”《说文》所说的“绁”实即“晴”(参见“绁”字)。雨过天晴为“啓”, 与开门之“啓”在比喻意义上是相似的, 它们在造字心理上有着共同的构型。甲骨文:“辛丑卜, 宾贞: 翌壬寅啓, 壬寅雾?”(《合集》13449)《广韵·荠韵》:“啓, 《说文》: 雨而昼见日。又姓。或作啓。”此念qǐ。《广韵·线韵》:“啓, 雨而昼止。”此念qiàn。(王志平)\\n\\n暘 yáng 书纽、锡部; 书纽、昔韵、施只切。\\n[glyph:g003]¹ — 暘\\n《说文》小篆 楷书\\n1《说文》138页。\\n形声字。初文作“易”, 甲骨文多有“易日”之语。后添加了义符“日”。《说文》:“暘, 日覆云暂见也。从日, 易声。”《广韵》:“暘, 日无光。”这是“暘”字的确诂。(王志平)\\n\\n晌 xiǎng 晓纽、侯部; 晓纽、遇韵、香句切。\\n[glyph:g004]¹ — [glyph:g005]² — [glyph:g006]³ — 晌\\n《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1《说文》138页。2《汉印徵》卷7, 2页。3《篆隶表》444页。\\n形声字。《说文》:“晌, 日出温也。从日, 句声。北地有晌衍县。”段玉裁注:“晌与火部煦义略同。”“煦”字《说文》训为:“蒸(蒸)也。一日赤兒(貌)。一日湿润也。从火, 呴声。”两字音义并同, 在“温暖”意义上经常通假。此外,“晌”还可以指“日光”等。(王志平)\\n\\n晛 xiàn 匣纽、元部; 匣纽、霰韵、胡甸切。\\n[glyph:g007]¹ — [glyph:g008]² — 晛\\n商 《说文》小篆 楷书\\n\\n1《甲文编》288页。2《说文》138页。\\n会意兼形声字。甲骨文从日, 从见, 用为贞人名。甲骨文:“癸巳卜, 晛贞: 翌日祖甲岁其牢。”(《合集》27336)《说文》:“晛, 日见也。从日, 从见。见亦声。《诗》曰: 见晛曰消。”也形容日出时的明亮或美丽。还可指“日光”。《说文》所引的《诗》见今本《诗·小雅·角弓》:“雨雪漉漉, 见晛曰消。”这里的“晛”实际上是指“日气”, 不是《说文》的本义。(王志平)\\n\\n晏 yàn 影纽、元部; 影纽、谏韵、乌涧切。\\n[glyph:g009]¹ — [glyph:g010]² — [glyph:g011]³ — 晏\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 楷书\\n1《战文编》457页。2《说文》138页。3《篆隶表》444页。\\n形声字。楚文字从日, 安声, 但是“安”字的写法与小篆正好相反。《说文》:“晏, 天清也。从日, 安声。”《汉书·扬雄传上》:“天清日晏。”古书中又训为“鲜盛也”。而一般训为“晚”的“晏”实际上是“旰”的假借字。《楚辞·离骚》:“及年岁之未晏兮。”也假借为“安”, 《史记·南越尉佗列传》:“今吕嘉建德等反自立晏如, 令罪人及江淮以南。”“晏如”就是安闲的样子, 古书常见。(王志平)\\n\\n景 jǐng 见纽、阳部; 见纽、梗韵、居影切。\\nyǐng 影纽、阳部; 影纽、梗韵、於境切。\\n[glyph:g012]¹ — [glyph:g013]² — [glyph:g014]³ — [glyph:g015]⁴ — 景\\n战国 《说文》小篆 汉 汉 楷书\\n1《汉语字形表》258页。2《说文》138页。3、4《篆隶表》445页。\\n形声字。《说文》:“景, 光也。从日, 京声。”这实际上是“影”的本字。“影子”的“影”本作“景”, 后来晋代葛洪的《字苑》一书为“景”字加上了“彡”旁, 才形成了今天的“影”字。所以形容时光短暂的“光景”也就是“光影”。又由名词的“光影”引申出动词的“光照”。“景”本不泛指光, 后也可仅指狭义的“日光”。此外,“景”又引申出“明亮”、“情况”、“风致”、“风景”等义。“景”还可以假借为“京”、“庆”、“映”、“仰”、“间”等等。(王志平)\\n\\n602\"}, {\"page_id\": \"sha256:610f098b7383499e15005c51bf5908d327b3188e274cc7a1784089e9af80a721:000613\", \"pdf_page_1based\": 613, \"printed_page\": null, \"source_scan\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0613/source.png\", \"source_sha256\": \"2a1c71643780eac70dea94cf857ef09c9369f5ac1880ea084ea580af98c69e98\", \"evidence_sha256\": \"5aae11821a88baa7a690c272d06a0212d6f47769aba3756bd3538f1ec4f2d283\", \"ocr_review_status\": \"unreviewed\", \"applied_ocr_corrections\": {\"original_evidence_sha256\": \"a8fe9ccff4fb0209b4dd2325531a90367f185c57e2f88c9de88b81186e0c3299\", \"corrections_sha256\": \"404fc938c688077e7cabc1d1631ef387c08c16e6083664aef5027dbd2cca3b1e\", \"text_patches\": [{\"start\": 1625, \"end\": 1629, \"before\": \"昀(的)\", \"after\": \"旳(的)\"}], \"scope\": \"Applied producer overlay, not whole-page approval. Compare current corrected corpus text with original pixels before proposing another correction.\"}, \"match_type\": \"possible_continuation\", \"headword_lines\": [], \"excerpt\": \"日部\\n\\n小之分。“大时”是指一年四季，“小时”是指一日中的十二辰，相当于今天的二十四小时。后来由计时的用法逐渐引申出“时光”、“时代”、“时运”、“时机”、“经常”、“适逢”等意义。(王志平)\\n\\n早 zǎo 精组、幽部; 精组、晧韵、子晧切。\\n[glyph:g001]1 战国 [glyph:g002]2 战国\\n[glyph:g003]3 《说文》小篆 [glyph:g004]4 秦 [glyph:g005]5 汉 早 楷书\\n1《金文编》456页。2《战文编》456页。3《说文》137页。4《睡甲》103页。5《篆隶表》442页。\\n会意字。战国文字从日,棗声,是个形声字。楚文字省略为“日”,“束”声(实际上是“棗”省声)。小篆从日,从甲。“甲”字是“毋”字的讹变。《说文》:“早,晨也。从日在甲上。”所以“早”的本义就是早晨。后来引申为“早先”、“很久以前”之义,如中山王𰯼鼎:“早弃群臣。”(《集成》5.2840)又有“最初”、“尽快”等义。古代一般假借“蚤”或“皂”字为之。(王志平)\\n\\n昧 mèi 明组、物部; 明组、队韵、莫佩切。\\n[glyph:g006]1 西周 [glyph:g007]2 战国 [glyph:g008]3 战国 [glyph:g009]4 《说文》小篆 [glyph:g010]5 汉 [glyph:g011]6 晋 昧 楷书\\n1《金文编》456页。2《古玺》165页。3《汉语字形表》257页。4《说文》137页。5、6《篆隶表》442页。\\n形声字。初文从“未”在“日”上,“未”为声旁兼会意。古文字经常颠倒偏旁,至《说文》小篆又演变为左“日”,右“未”,与隶书、楷书无别了。免簋有“昧爽”(《集成》8.4240)之语。《说文》:“昧爽,旦明也。一日闇也。”所以“昧”字的本义就是天色将明未明之形。又由此引申出“暗昧”、“昏庸”之义。(王志平)\\n\\n睹 dǔ 端组、鱼部; 端组、姥韵、当古切。\\nshǔ 禅组、鱼部; 禅组、御韵、常恕切。\\n[glyph:g012]1 战国 [glyph:g013]2 《说文》小篆 睹 楷书\\n1《楚系简帛》565页。2《说文》137页。\\n形声字。战国文字从日,者声。“者”古音与“睹”接近。《楚帛书》丙篇:“故出睹。”《说文》:“睹,且明也。从日,者声。”通作“曙”。《广韵》:“睹,洁朝欲明。”(王志平)\\n\\n昭 zhāo 章组、宵部; 章组、宵韵、止遥切。\\nzhào 章组、宵部; 章组、啸韵、之笑切。\\nsháo 禅组、宵部; 禅组、宵韵、市昭切。\\n[glyph:g014]1 西周 [glyph:g015]2 战国 [glyph:g016]3 战国 [glyph:g017]4 《说文》小篆 [glyph:g018]5 汉 [glyph:g019]6 汉 昭 楷书\\n1《金文编》643页。2《汉语字形表》258页。3《战文编》456页。4《说文》137页。5《睡甲》103页。6《篆隶表》442页。\\n形声字。初文作“召”,或添加了“日”旁,假借为“昭”。𧵦羌钟:“昭于天子。”(《集成》1.157)楚文字从日,召声,但是与后世小篆字形差别较大。《说文》:“昭,日明也。从日,召声。”《诗·大雅·云汉》:“倬彼云汉,昭回于天。”《楚辞·大招》:“白日昭只。”此音念 zhāo。后来由“日明”引申出“显著”的意思。也通假为“照”、“炤(zhào)”、“韶”、“佋(sháo)”等。(王志平)\\n\\n晤 wù 疑组、鱼部; 疑组、暮韵、五故切。\\n[glyph:g020]1 《说文》小篆 晤 楷书\\n1《说文》137页。\\n形声字。《说文》:“晤,明也。从日,吾声。《诗》曰:晤辟有摽。”段玉裁注:“晤者,启之明也。心部之悟、寤部之寤皆训觉,亦明也。同声之义必相近。”通作“寤”。今本《诗·邶风·柏舟》即作“寤辟有摽”。也通假为“违”,我们今天所说的“会晤”之“晤”即是此义。(王志平)\\n\\n旳(的) dī 端组、药部; 端组、锡韵、都历切。\\ndí 端组、药部; 端组、锡韵、都历切。\\n[glyph:g021]1 《说文》小篆 [glyph:g022]2 汉 的 楷书\\n[glyph:g023]3 汉 的 楷书\\n\\n600\"}], \"use\": \"Locator leads only; inspect scan pixels and exact headword before citing.\"}], \"source_scan_images\": [{\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0612/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 612, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"1cadbd65dde8f6adfcace48a424487db09d7d35246851239125bdef976e4197c\"}, {\"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-books/research-ocr/ziyuan-full/page-0613/source.png\", \"pdf_page\": 613, \"source_pixel_sha256\": \"2a1c71643780eac70dea94cf857ef09c9369f5ac1880ea084ea580af98c69e98\"}], \"target_language\": \"zh\", \"additional_research_context\": {\"target_language\": \"zh\", \"questions\": [\"Verify current Chinese senses sun/day directly in present-day authoritative dictionary entries and cite those consulted entries. Historical oracle examples establish their ancient use, not loss of present use. Keep supported early uses qualified and distinguish earliest corpus claims from first-ever priority.\", \"The inherited draft contains Japanese-only senses inferred from KANJIDIC2 ref011. This is a Chinese HSK1 entry: independently verify any proposed Chinese Japan abbreviation/current sense, or omit Japanese-only use from Chinese sense records. Do not solve absent Chinese evidence by calling a Japanese gloss historical Chinese. Research Japanese meanings separately for Japanese entries.\", \"Inspect the retained selected sun glyph pixels and describe the actual rounded outline/filled oval. Qualify the modern bronze-style redraw: no exact image-to-師虎簋 inscription match has been verified, so do not transfer the vessel date or central-mark interpretation to this exact artifact. Retain source pictographic analysis as separately cited interpretation.\"], \"worker_scope\": \"Read sources and return evidence only; no nested jobs, pipeline invocation, subagents or repository mutations.\"}, \"additional_context_policy\": \"These source leads and prior findings are research tasks, not preverified dossier evidence. Consult the referenced sources directly, check exact graph identity and scope, and add cited evidence for useful claims before authorship. Recheck reviewer hypotheses; modern structure does not establish historical roles. Record inaccessible sources as gaps.\", \"unfinished_draft_provenance\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/sun-moon-tree-smoke/ziyuan-2012/65ref005\", \"article_hash\": \"8218c0086a4364e2f2a874958fddbec936c6937eb6d6009d22ddba52c5288382\", \"dossier_hash\": \"0b8dd4ccf919dd7f25f10a0a11ee22a6b8cc3cf128fee81de3c57da81f8be170\", \"previous_state_hash\": \"7aa765a947f4154765b80abd8fc15bc236dd27e2d1151a143139bf277c46d4b6\", \"canonical_article_hash\": \"1163c1080532b6624d33a122a296eb44882a810f06a0e9bf8f4d53dbbf134496\", \"canonical_dossier_hash\": \"ecc6c8b75feff49ac9c47cc016ed61e8d217f018d871239884fa5bf1e628dc52\", \"requires_fresh_research_and_reviews\": true, \"prior_review_proposals_hash\": \"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945\"}, \"prior_review_proposals\": [], \"prior_consulted_book_records\": {\"previous_job\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/runs/source-enrichment-ziyuan/sun-moon-tree-smoke/ziyuan-2012/65ref005\", \"previous_audit_hash\": \"15e9b1de90879284a9ec13bedfd6b1dc43375f1a83fb8986a8048f25e9899d08\", \"records\": [{\"research_output\": \"initial-followup/research/result.json\", \"research_receipt_hash\": \"2b979e17c8644e178127387cc39b8f3bd229a898dd7ac25f020bd738833c35bc\", \"research_result_hash\": \"18db116fb3f55a14d03aff2ae3e4c9d301cf56f61d95ec68fceb5dd2cb4cb11b\", \"source\": \"李學勤主編《字源》, 天津古籍出版社／遼寧人民出版社, 2012, ISBN 9787552800692; scan inspected\", \"field\": \"日部 entry, printed p. 599 / PDF p. 612; headword, glyph sequence, and prose checked against the supplied scan. The entry ends on this page; the next page begins the unrelated 旻 entry.\", \"text\": \"Under the actual headword 日, the entry describes the graph as pictographic and its initial form as resembling the sun. It says later writing changed rounded strokes to angular forms, identifies the sun as the basic meaning, and proposes extensions to sunlight, day, daylight, time, and ruler. These are the book author's account of graphic and semantic development; the cited inscriptional examples do not independently establish the order or every semantic link.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref043\"]}], \"requires_current_source_research\": true}, \"continuation_policy\": \"This is an unapproved draft, not reusable approval. Recheck source-dependent claims against current source scans/corpus. Prior revise findings are hypotheses to recheck, including possible source identity errors; do not obey them merely because a previous verifier repeated them. Prior consulted book records identify exact retained evidence IDs and claims to recheck. After current-source verification, cite relevant supported claims through evidence_ids metadata; dossier-only records do not count as article source use. Preserve prior supported work and obtain fresh factual/readability reviews.\", \"require_source_specific_page_evidence\": true}, \"task\": \"Make the smallest supported changes that resolve the supplied findings and their directly affected fields. Preserve unaffected prose, citations, readings and graph records exactly; do not perform another general rewrite. Check that each changed claim still cites evidence supporting its precise scope.\", \"original_role\": \"editor\", \"article_contract\": {\"relationship_branches\": [{\"predicates\": [\"semantic_component_of\", \"phonetic_component_of\", \"pictorial_component_of\", \"indicator_component_of\", \"replacement_component_of\", \"empty_component_of\"], \"subject_kind\": \"component\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"variant_of\", \"simplified_from\", \"derived_from\", \"phonetic_element_in\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"character\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"has_sense\"], \"subject_kind\": \"character\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}, {\"predicates\": [\"sense_developed_into\", \"phonetic_loan_for\"], \"subject_kind\": \"sense\", \"object_kind\": \"sense\"}], \"instruction\": \"These are the exact allowed relationship names and endpoint kinds. _component_of is a suffix, never a literal predicate. Unknown component roles create no invented unknown_component_of edge. Preserve scoped historical claims and supported graph links.\", \"citation_instruction\": \"Reader prose must contain no refNNN transport aliases or inline evidence ID lists. Store citations only in the containing record evidence_ids array. When editing only a text field, preserve its existing evidence_ids; if support changes, edit that array as a separate nonoverlapping path. Never append [ref001, ref002] to prose.\"}, \"local_primary_readings\": {\"unihan\": {\"source_path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/pipeline/data/unihan-kmandarin-hsk1.tsv\", \"field\": \"kMandarin\", \"rows\": {\"日\": \"rì\"}}}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/glyph-repair-1/prompt.txt:75:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X1\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-bc4776b719ddbfd67cc0\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6e1300ed048b3ebd331\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5492c9689424bcfb968c\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-db5ffb42ef75cf24092a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\"]}}, \"xiaoxuetang_query\": {\"entry_character\": \"好\", \"source\": \"小學堂字形演變資料庫 — Academia Sinica\", \"source_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian\", \"rights_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"queries\": [{\"character\": \"好\", \"status\": \"unavailable\", \"error\": \"PermissionError: Xiaoxuetang returned HTTP 401; automatic queries are paused for one hour\", \"source_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/yanbian\", \"rights_url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"candidates\": []}], \"candidates\": [], \"instructions\": \"These are a narrowly requested per-character output from the official form query. Labels are transcribed exactly and are not normalized dates. Select only useful forms; cite and snapshot only selected images.\"}, \"acquisition_findings\": {\"attempt\": 1, \"error\": \"HTTP Error 404: Not Found\", \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5e/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\"}, {\"id\": \"hao_seal_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"}]}, \"acquisition_task\": \"Repair these exact image acquisition failures. Inspect actual direct image links on the source pages; never guess image paths or Wikimedia hash directories. Preserve supported candidates when valid. Return a complete candidate selection and record access failures honestly; exclude unusable candidates only with an explicit cited limitation.\"}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/research/meta.json:24:    \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/research/meta.json:25:    \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/research/result.json:1:{\"evidence\":[{\"source\":\"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\",\"field\":\"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\",\"text\":\"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"dictionary_entry\",\"url\":\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"title\":\"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\",\"field\":\"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\",\"text\":\"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"dictionary_entry\",\"url\":\"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\",\"title\":\"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\",\"field\":\"女部 / 好\",\"text\":\"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"dictionary_entry\",\"url\":\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\",\"title\":\"說文解字「好」\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"Wiktionary, character entry 好\",\"field\":\"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\",\"text\":\"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"dictionary_entry\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\",\"title\":\"好 — Wiktionary\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\",\"field\":\"Old Chinese pronunciations\",\"text\":\"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"phonology_data\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\",\"title\":\"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"Wiktionary, character entry 好\",\"field\":\"modern readings\",\"text\":\"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"pronunciation\",\"url\":\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\",\"title\":\"好 — Wiktionary\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"},{\"source\":\"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\",\"field\":\"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\",\"text\":\"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\",\"record_character\":\"好\",\"kind\":\"dictionary_entry\",\"url\":\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"title\":\"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\",\"accessed_at\":\"2026-09-26\"}],\"search_audit\":[{\"query\":\"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"],\"outcome\":\"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"},{\"query\":\"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"},{\"query\":\"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"],\"outcome\":\"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"},{\"query\":\"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"},{\"query\":\"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\",\"urls\":[\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\",\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"},{\"query\":\"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"},{\"query\":\"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"},{\"query\":\"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"],\"outcome\":\"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"},{\"query\":\"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\"urls\":[\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\",\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"],\"outcome\":\"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"},{\"query\":\"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\",\"urls\":[\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\",\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"],\"outcome\":\"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"},{\"query\":\"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\",\"urls\":[\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"],\"outcome\":\"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}],\"gaps\":[\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\",\"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\",\"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\",\"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\",\"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\",\"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\",\"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\",\"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\",\"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\"]}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/glyph_visual/prompt.txt:31:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A modern redraw labeled as Shang oracle-script 好; the file page identifies its model as hanziyuan form J26184.\", \"alt\": \"Black vector outline of an early-script glyph, presented by its source page as 好 in Shang oracle-script style.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Provides an early graphic candidate relevant to the reported 女-plus-子 analysis; source page supplies the form label and provenance. Visual pixels remain to be checked by the harness.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A modern redraw labeled bronze-script 好, based on hanziyuan form B17049; it is not a photograph of an inscribed vessel.\", \"alt\": \"Black vector outline of a glyph presented by its source page as 好 in bronze-script style.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Pairs with the oracle-style candidate to illustrate a second source-labeled graphic tradition without implying an exact chronology or artifact exemplar.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref036\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_seal_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shuowen seal-script category; source page does not supply an absolute date for the depicted form\", \"tradition\": \"Small-seal form as presented in Shuowen tradition; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A modern redraw labeled as the Shuowen seal-script form of 好, based on hanziyuan form S09198.\", \"alt\": \"Black vector outline of a glyph presented by its source page as 好 in Shuowen seal-script style.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Offers a later transmitted-script comparison for the early-form candidates while keeping the source's seal-script label explicit.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref037\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"Direct SVG retrieval through the browser failed, so candidate glyphs were selected from inspected source-page descriptions and the harness must validate the downloaded pixels. Do not treat these as inspected or photographed ancient artifacts.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\", \"ref036\", \"ref037\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_seal_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"sha256\": \"b3883d277d98639db90c810b236ae3133faed68fafe82aee6e292bd4b0c7bf89\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/b3883d277d98639db90c810b236ae3133faed68fafe82aee6e292bd4b0c7bf89.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5335}]}, \"attached_images\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.png\", \"sha256\": \"947274359a5893541c4c5e786f8bae1ac9e21092827d130fc3502be228c3c441\"}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.png\", \"sha256\": \"3f8683a3dbf366c88bee80d988d3f430cd7d16ab9cf4af4b73f357de5fd7cbc0\"}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_seal_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/b3883d277d98639db90c810b236ae3133faed68fafe82aee6e292bd4b0c7bf89.png\", \"sha256\": \"bf43ad615e8f6f7e542443108a239f8d2f89b173dbe674246741775d9737aba6\"}]}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/writer/prompt.txt:148:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref024\", \"ref036\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\", \"ref036\", \"ref014\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}, \"analysis\": {\"supported_claims\": [{\"text\": \"Ordinary 好 is analyzed as an associative compound of 女 ‘woman’ and 子 ‘child’; this is a source interpretation of the graph, not direct proof of the motivation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, {\"text\": \"A mother–child affection interpretation is favored because early 子 is described as child-shaped and smaller than 女, but the cited early uses do not directly attest that sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, {\"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 ‘beautiful’ and analyzes it as 女 plus 子; this lexicographic account does not establish the original motivation or earliest ordinary use.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref015\", \"ref025\"]}, {\"text\": \"Reported inscriptional uses include the Shang personal name 婦好, a bronze example glossed ‘good/beautiful’, another bronze example glossed ‘friendly’, and Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ and friendly relations.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref021\", \"ref022\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. The available evidence does not establish a complete sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref023\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"text\": \"The separate proposal for 婦好 treats 好 as a homograph in which 子 supplies sound and represents a Shang lineage name; it should not be transferred to the ordinary 女-plus-子 analysis.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref021\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝. The supplied evidence does not explain the source word’s object-name or provide a sound comparison establishing the proposed match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref023\"]}, {\"text\": \"Baxter–Sagart reconstructions list 好 ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and 好 ‘love, like’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s; the suffixal relationship is a proposed morphology of the readings, not proof of semantic chronology.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref016\", \"ref017\", \"ref030\", \"ref033\"]}], \"disagreements\": [{\"text\": \"Proposed motivations for the ordinary graph differ: mother–child affection, a woman's beauty, and affection between a man and woman are all reported; the dossier does not resolve them.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref004\", \"ref013\", \"ref015\", \"ref020\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"Whether 好 in the Shang personal name 婦好 is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 is unsettled; a distinct homograph and phonetic-name analysis has been proposed.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref021\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"Early component order varied, and side-switched forms are treated as variants rather than chronological ancestors; the listed variant evidence does not prove ancestry among every form.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref024\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest securely described evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical use. The ordinary meaning ‘good/beautiful’ is reported in a bronze inscription, but the supplied account gives no absolute date for that vessel or securely dated first occurrence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref022\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"The cited evidence provides selected uses rather than a complete dated history, so it does not establish the order or transitions among ‘good’, ‘beautiful’, ‘friendly’, and ‘like’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref022\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"The selected oracle- and bronze-script depictions are modern vector redrawings, not artifact photographs; their pixels were not visually inspected, and they illustrate forms without proving a chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref027\", \"ref028\", \"ref035\", \"ref036\"]}, {\"text\": \"The dossier does not establish the sound match underlying the reported phonetic loan for 孝, so the borrowing mechanism cannot be specified beyond the reported claim.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref023\"]}]}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:107:      \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:206:      \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:457:        \"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:465:        \"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:508:        \"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\",\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/dossier.json:513:        \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/analysis/prompt.txt:148:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref024\", \"ref036\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\", \"ref036\", \"ref014\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/round-0/factual-verification/prompt.txt:174:{\"article\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"好 is conventionally analyzed as 女 ‘woman’ with 子 ‘child’, an associative compound used for ‘good’. A mother–child idea has been proposed, but the graph’s original motivation is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"A semantic (associative) compound of 女 and 子. The components support interpretations such as mother–child affection, but early uses do not directly confirm that proposal; beauty and man–woman affection are alternatives.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"type\": \"semantic\"}, \"components\": [{\"text\": \"女 ‘woman’ contributes to the conventional associative analysis of 好. Its presence does not determine whether the original idea concerned maternal affection, beauty, or another proposed motivation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"女\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}, {\"text\": \"子 ‘child’ contributes to the conventional analysis. Early forms are described as showing it smaller than 女, favoring—but not proving—the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"子\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; side-switched forms are treated as variants rather than chronological ancestors. Some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. These accounts do not provide a complete sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}, {\"text\": \"The selected oracle- and bronze-script illustrations are modern vector redrawings, not artifact photographs; their appearance does not establish a sequence of change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}, {\"text\": \"In the Shang name 婦好, a separate proposal treats 好 as a possible homograph: 子 may supply the sound and represent a lineage name. This name analysis does not establish a phonetic role for 子 in ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝: the written sign was reused for another word because of a proposed sound resemblance, rather than because 好 acquired the meaning ‘filial piety’. The sound comparison is not supplied.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The original motivation of ordinary 好 remains unresolved. Proposed accounts include mother–child affection, a woman’s beauty, and affection between a man and woman; cited early uses do not directly attest the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The earliest securely described context is the Shang personal name 婦好, not an unambiguous ordinary use meaning ‘good’. A bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’ is reported, but its vessel is not absolutely dated in the account.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"Selected examples do not establish the order or transitions among ‘good’, ‘beautiful’, ‘friendly’, and ‘like’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The reported phonetic loan for 孝 lacks an established sound comparison, so its proposed sound basis remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"gloss\": \"good; beautiful\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:friendly\", \"gloss\": \"friendly; on good terms\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:like\", \"gloss\": \"like; love (verb)\", \"period\": \"Warring States bamboo texts\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:good_current\", \"gloss\": \"good; well\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}], \"developments\": [{\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:friendly\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:like\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:good_current\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest ordinary use is not securely dated, and a complete semantic chronology is not available.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The Shang name 婦好 is attested, but it remains unsettled whether its 好 is the same lexical graph and word as ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}]}, \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"好 combines 女 ‘woman’ and 子 ‘child’ in a conventional associative analysis. Mother–child affection is one proposed original idea, but early uses do not directly confirm it.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"components\": [{\"component_index\": 0, \"text\": \"女 supplies the woman element in the conventional analysis; its presence does not settle the graph’s original idea.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"component_index\": 1, \"text\": \"子 supplies the child element; its smaller early form favors, but does not prove, the mother–child proposal.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}], \"takeaway\": null}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"hao_nu_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"女\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"女 contributes ‘woman’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_zi_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"子\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"子 contributes ‘child’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_beautiful\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:friendly\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:like\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:0\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:1\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:2\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}}, \"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X1\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-bc4776b719ddbfd67cc0\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6e1300ed048b3ebd331\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5492c9689424bcfb968c\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-db5ffb42ef75cf24092a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d84dc9f0a5b113e6382a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}, \"proposed_review\": {\"verdict\": \"revise\", \"findings\": [\"In `history`, the sentence about a bronze inscription using 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝 describes a lexical borrowing, but `meaning_history` has no corresponding 孝 loan sense or development. Add the reported use there, clearly mark its proposed sound basis as uncertain because no sound comparison is supplied, and avoid implying that the meaning ‘filial piety’ developed from 好.\"]}, \"verification_task\": \"Independently verify the proposed review findings against the exact current article fields and cited evidence. A proposed finding is a hypothesis, not a fact. Check quoted readings, labels, directions and alleged contradictions directly. Discard demonstrably false or duplicate requests, but retain every actual required correction. Return pass with no findings only when no actual required corrections remain; otherwise return revise with supported concrete findings identifying the exact field and evidence. Findings must contain only changes still REQUIRED to the current article, never explanations of why a proposed correction was rejected or unnecessary. If all proposed findings are rejected and you identify no other required correction, verdict MUST be pass and findings MUST be empty. Do not assume that verification should pass. Preserve the supplied review scope.\", \"attached_images\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.png\", \"sha256\": \"947274359a5893541c4c5e786f8bae1ac9e21092827d130fc3502be228c3c441\"}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.png\", \"sha256\": \"3f8683a3dbf366c88bee80d988d3f430cd7d16ab9cf4af4b73f357de5fd7cbc0\"}], \"schema_contract\": {\"formation_types\": [\"pictographic\", \"indicative\", \"semantic\", \"phonosemantic\", \"phonetic_loan\", \"mixed\", \"derived\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"component_roles\": [\"semantic\", \"phonetic\", \"pictorial\", \"indicator\", \"replacement\", \"empty\", \"unknown\"], \"form_statuses\": [\"preserved\", \"variant\", \"stylized\", \"corruption\", \"replacement\", \"simplified\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"sense_statuses\": [\"earliest_attested\", \"historical\", \"current\"], \"instruction\": \"These are the actual supported classifications. disputed is a valid formation type. Do not invent schema restrictions or fields. Evaluate proposed roles with their form_status, explanation, scope and edge certainty together.\"}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/round-0/editor/prompt.txt:148:{\"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"ref001\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref002\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref003\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref004\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref005\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref006\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref007\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref008\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref009\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref010\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"ref011\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"ref012\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref013\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref014\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref015\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref016\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref017\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref018\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref019\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref020\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref021\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref022\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref023\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref024\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref025\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref026\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref027\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref028\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref029\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref030\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref031\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref032\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref033\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref034\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref035\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref036\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"ref037\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref024\", \"ref036\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\", \"ref036\", \"ref014\", \"ref024\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}, \"article\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"好 is conventionally analyzed as 女 ‘woman’ with 子 ‘child’. The graph’s original motivation is disputed: mother–child affection is favored by some accounts, but the early uses cited here do not directly attest that meaning.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"A semantic (associative) compound: 女 and 子 contribute to the proposed meaning together, with no phonetic component in this analysis. The proposed mother–child interpretation is plausible but unproven; other motivations have been suggested.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref020\"], \"type\": \"semantic\"}, \"components\": [{\"text\": \"女 ‘woman’ is a semantic component. Together with 子 it supports an associative reading of the graph; its presence does not settle whether the original idea was maternal affection, beauty, or another proposal.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref020\"], \"form\": \"女\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}, {\"text\": \"子 ‘child’ is a semantic component in the ordinary 好 analysis. Early forms are described as smaller than 女, which favors—but does not prove—the proposed mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"], \"form\": \"子\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; a side-switched form is treated as a variant, not as a chronological ancestor. Some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. The available accounts do not establish a complete sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref014\", \"ref023\", \"ref024\"]}, {\"text\": \"Selected oracle- and bronze-script depictions illustrate forms labeled 好, but they are modern vector redrawings rather than artifact photographs. The images were not visually inspected, so they do not establish a sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref027\", \"ref028\", \"ref035\", \"ref036\"]}, {\"text\": \"A separate analysis treats 好 in the Shang name 婦好 as a possible homograph: 子 may supply the sound and represent a lineage name. This proposal concerns the name graph and should not be transferred to the ordinary 女-plus-子 analysis.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref021\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝. This means the graph was reportedly reused for another word because of a sound resemblance, not because 好 developed the meaning ‘filial piety’; the supplied evidence does not establish the sound match.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref023\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The original motivation of the ordinary graph remains unresolved. Accounts propose mother–child affection, a woman’s beauty, or affection between a man and woman; the cited early uses do not directly attest the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref015\", \"ref020\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"The earliest securely described context is the Shang personal name 婦好, not an unambiguous ordinary use meaning ‘good’. The supplied account reports a bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’ but gives no absolute date for that vessel.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref022\", \"ref034\"]}, {\"text\": \"The evidence gives selected uses, not a complete dated history. It does not establish the order or transitions among ‘good’, ‘beautiful’, ‘friendly’, and ‘like’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref022\", \"ref025\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"The reported phonetic loan for 孝 lacks an established sound comparison in this dossier, so its proposed sound basis remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref023\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"gloss\": \"good; beautiful\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the specific vessel is not absolutely dated in the account.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:friendly\", \"gloss\": \"friendly; on good terms\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in the phrase 好朋友, glossed ‘friendly’. Its exact chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established here.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:like\", \"gloss\": \"like; love (verb)\", \"period\": \"Warring States bamboo texts\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. The proposed Old Chinese suffixal relation between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading is a morphological analysis, not proof of which sense came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\", \"ref033\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:good_current\", \"gloss\": \"good; well\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"The character is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’ in modern Chinese. The supplied evidence does not establish a dated path from the selected inscriptional senses to these current uses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref018\", \"ref031\"]}], \"developments\": [{\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:friendly\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the supplied evidence does not establish that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref022\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:like\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The ‘good’ and verbal ‘like’ readings have a proposed suffixal relationship in Old Chinese, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref017\", \"ref030\", \"ref033\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:good_current\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared ‘good’ gloss does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref006\", \"ref022\", \"ref032\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The dossier does not securely date the earliest ordinary use of 好 or establish a complete semantic chronology.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref022\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"The Shang name 婦好 is reported, but the evidence does not settle whether its 好 is the same lexical graph and word as ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref021\", \"ref034\"]}]}, \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"好 is conventionally read as 女 ‘woman’ joined with 子 ‘child’. A mother–child idea is proposed for its original sense, but early examples do not prove that interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, \"components\": [{\"component_index\": 0, \"text\": \"女 contributes the woman element in the conventional associative analysis; the graph’s original idea remains debated.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, {\"component_index\": 1, \"text\": \"子 contributes the child element in the conventional analysis; its smaller early form supports, but does not prove, the mother–child proposal.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}], \"takeaway\": null}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"hao_nu_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"女\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"女 contributes ‘woman’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_zi_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"子\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"子 contributes ‘child’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref003\", \"ref013\", \"ref020\"]}], \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref035\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref014\", \"ref024\", \"ref036\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref013\", \"ref020\", \"ref032\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"ref035\", \"ref036\", \"ref014\", \"ref024\"]}]}}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/round-0/factual/prompt.txt:173:{\"article\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"好 is conventionally analyzed as 女 ‘woman’ with 子 ‘child’, an associative compound used for ‘good’. A mother–child idea has been proposed, but the graph’s original motivation is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"A semantic (associative) compound of 女 and 子. The components support interpretations such as mother–child affection, but early uses do not directly confirm that proposal; beauty and man–woman affection are alternatives.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"type\": \"semantic\"}, \"components\": [{\"text\": \"女 ‘woman’ contributes to the conventional associative analysis of 好. Its presence does not determine whether the original idea concerned maternal affection, beauty, or another proposed motivation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"女\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}, {\"text\": \"子 ‘child’ contributes to the conventional analysis. Early forms are described as showing it smaller than 女, favoring—but not proving—the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"子\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; side-switched forms are treated as variants rather than chronological ancestors. Some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. These accounts do not provide a complete sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}, {\"text\": \"The selected oracle- and bronze-script illustrations are modern vector redrawings, not artifact photographs; their appearance does not establish a sequence of change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}, {\"text\": \"In the Shang name 婦好, a separate proposal treats 好 as a possible homograph: 子 may supply the sound and represent a lineage name. This name analysis does not establish a phonetic role for 子 in ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝: the written sign was reused for another word because of a proposed sound resemblance, rather than because 好 acquired the meaning ‘filial piety’. The sound comparison is not supplied.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The original motivation of ordinary 好 remains unresolved. Proposed accounts include mother–child affection, a woman’s beauty, and affection between a man and woman; cited early uses do not directly attest the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The earliest securely described context is the Shang personal name 婦好, not an unambiguous ordinary use meaning ‘good’. A bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’ is reported, but its vessel is not absolutely dated in the account.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"Selected examples do not establish the order or transitions among ‘good’, ‘beautiful’, ‘friendly’, and ‘like’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The reported phonetic loan for 孝 lacks an established sound comparison, so its proposed sound basis remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"gloss\": \"good; beautiful\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:friendly\", \"gloss\": \"friendly; on good terms\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:like\", \"gloss\": \"like; love (verb)\", \"period\": \"Warring States bamboo texts\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:good_current\", \"gloss\": \"good; well\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}], \"developments\": [{\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:friendly\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:like\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:good_current\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest ordinary use is not securely dated, and a complete semantic chronology is not available.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The Shang name 婦好 is attested, but it remains unsettled whether its 好 is the same lexical graph and word as ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}]}, \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"好 combines 女 ‘woman’ and 子 ‘child’ in a conventional associative analysis. Mother–child affection is one proposed original idea, but early uses do not directly confirm it.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"components\": [{\"component_index\": 0, \"text\": \"女 supplies the woman element in the conventional analysis; its presence does not settle the graph’s original idea.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"component_index\": 1, \"text\": \"子 supplies the child element; its smaller early form favors, but does not prove, the mother–child proposal.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}], \"takeaway\": null}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"hao_nu_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"女\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"女 contributes ‘woman’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_zi_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"子\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"子 contributes ‘child’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_beautiful\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:friendly\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:like\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:0\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:1\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:2\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}}, \"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X1\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-bc4776b719ddbfd67cc0\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6e1300ed048b3ebd331\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5492c9689424bcfb968c\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-db5ffb42ef75cf24092a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d84dc9f0a5b113e6382a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}, \"attached_images\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.png\", \"sha256\": \"947274359a5893541c4c5e786f8bae1ac9e21092827d130fc3502be228c3c441\"}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.png\", \"sha256\": \"3f8683a3dbf366c88bee80d988d3f430cd7d16ab9cf4af4b73f357de5fd7cbc0\"}], \"schema_contract\": {\"formation_types\": [\"pictographic\", \"indicative\", \"semantic\", \"phonosemantic\", \"phonetic_loan\", \"mixed\", \"derived\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"component_roles\": [\"semantic\", \"phonetic\", \"pictorial\", \"indicator\", \"replacement\", \"empty\", \"unknown\"], \"form_statuses\": [\"preserved\", \"variant\", \"stylized\", \"corruption\", \"replacement\", \"simplified\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"sense_statuses\": [\"earliest_attested\", \"historical\", \"current\"], \"instruction\": \"These are the actual supported classifications. disputed is a valid formation type. Do not invent schema restrictions or fields. Evaluate proposed roles with their form_status, explanation, scope and edge certainty together.\"}}\ncontent/editorial_runs/597D/e417e9e11e5c9002867f7351541a0635b6054ddb819bda32fd78b3fa1496c71a/round-0/readability/prompt.txt:173:{\"article\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"summary\": {\"text\": \"好 is conventionally analyzed as 女 ‘woman’ with 子 ‘child’, an associative compound used for ‘good’. A mother–child idea has been proposed, but the graph’s original motivation is unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"formation\": {\"text\": \"A semantic (associative) compound of 女 and 子. The components support interpretations such as mother–child affection, but early uses do not directly confirm that proposal; beauty and man–woman affection are alternatives.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"type\": \"semantic\"}, \"components\": [{\"text\": \"女 ‘woman’ contributes to the conventional associative analysis of 好. Its presence does not determine whether the original idea concerned maternal affection, beauty, or another proposed motivation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"女\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}, {\"text\": \"子 ‘child’ contributes to the conventional analysis. Early forms are described as showing it smaller than 女, favoring—but not proving—the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"], \"form\": \"子\", \"origin_form\": \"\", \"origin_relation\": \"none\", \"scope_character\": \"好\", \"sound_limitation\": null, \"sound\": [], \"roles\": [\"semantic\"], \"form_status\": \"preserved\"}], \"history\": [{\"text\": \"Early forms are reported with 女 and 子 in variable positions; side-switched forms are treated as variants rather than chronological ancestors. Some Warring States bamboo forms are reported with 丑 in place of 女. These accounts do not provide a complete sequence of graphic change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}, {\"text\": \"The selected oracle- and bronze-script illustrations are modern vector redrawings, not artifact photographs; their appearance does not establish a sequence of change.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}, {\"text\": \"In the Shang name 婦好, a separate proposal treats 好 as a possible homograph: 子 may supply the sound and represent a lineage name. This name analysis does not establish a phonetic role for 子 in ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported to use 好 as a phonetic loan for 孝: the written sign was reused for another word because of a proposed sound resemblance, rather than because 好 acquired the meaning ‘filial piety’. The sound comparison is not supplied.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"uncertainties\": [{\"text\": \"The original motivation of ordinary 好 remains unresolved. Proposed accounts include mother–child affection, a woman’s beauty, and affection between a man and woman; cited early uses do not directly attest the mother–child interpretation.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The earliest securely described context is the Shang personal name 婦好, not an unambiguous ordinary use meaning ‘good’. A bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’ is reported, but its vessel is not absolutely dated in the account.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}, {\"text\": \"Selected examples do not establish the order or transitions among ‘good’, ‘beautiful’, ‘friendly’, and ‘like’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The reported phonetic loan for 孝 lacks an established sound comparison, so its proposed sound basis remains uncertain.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-29982ff050151df22087\"]}], \"schema_version\": 2, \"meaning_history\": {\"senses\": [{\"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"gloss\": \"good; beautiful\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"earliest_attested\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:friendly\", \"gloss\": \"friendly; on good terms\", \"period\": \"Bronze inscriptions; absolute dating unresolved\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:like\", \"gloss\": \"like; love (verb)\", \"period\": \"Warring States bamboo texts\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"status\": \"historical\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"好:good_current\", \"gloss\": \"good; well\", \"period\": \"Current\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"status\": \"current\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}], \"developments\": [{\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:friendly\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:like\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"from_sense\": \"好:good_beautiful\", \"to_sense\": \"好:good_current\", \"type\": \"uncertain\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The earliest ordinary use is not securely dated, and a complete semantic chronology is not available.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"The Shang name 婦好 is attested, but it remains unsettled whether its 好 is the same lexical graph and word as ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\"]}]}, \"learner\": {\"overview\": {\"text\": \"好 combines 女 ‘woman’ and 子 ‘child’ in a conventional associative analysis. Mother–child affection is one proposed original idea, but early uses do not directly confirm it.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, \"components\": [{\"component_index\": 0, \"text\": \"女 supplies the woman element in the conventional analysis; its presence does not settle the graph’s original idea.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"component_index\": 1, \"text\": \"子 supplies the child element; its smaller early form favors, but does not prove, the mother–child proposal.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}], \"takeaway\": null}, \"relationships\": [{\"id\": \"hao_nu_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"女\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"女 contributes ‘woman’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_zi_semantic\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"component\", \"id\": \"子\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"predicate\": \"semantic_component_of\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"子 contributes ‘child’ in the conventional associative analysis of ordinary 好.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E3\", \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_beautiful\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"The earliest reported ordinary lexical use in the supplied evidence is a bronze inscription glossed ‘good/beautiful’; the account does not give the vessel an absolute date.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:friendly\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A bronze inscription is reported with 好 in 好朋友, meaning ‘friendly’. Its chronological relation to the ‘good/beautiful’ use is not established.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:like\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"probable\", \"text\": \"A Warring States bamboo text is reported with 好 meaning ‘like’. A proposed Old Chinese suffixal relationship between the verbal reading and the ‘good’ reading does not establish which meaning came first.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"meaning:好:good_current\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"character\", \"id\": \"好\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"has_sense\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"established\", \"text\": \"In modern Chinese, 好 is used for ‘good’ and ‘well’. The evidence here does not show the dated steps from the selected inscriptional uses to these current meanings.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:0\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:friendly\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"Both uses are reported in bronze inscriptions, but the evidence does not show that ‘friendly’ developed from ‘good/beautiful’.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:1\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:like\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The Old Chinese readings have a proposed suffixal relationship, but this does not establish semantic development or chronology between the senses.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\"]}, {\"id\": \"development:2\", \"subject\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_beautiful\"}, \"object\": {\"kind\": \"sense\", \"id\": \"好:good_current\"}, \"predicate\": \"sense_developed_into\", \"context_character\": \"好\", \"certainty\": \"disputed\", \"text\": \"The shared gloss ‘good’ does not by itself establish the historical steps from the reported bronze use to the modern sense.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"E6\", \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}], \"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}}, \"dossier\": {\"character\": \"好\", \"evidence\": [{\"id\": \"E1\", \"source\": \"makemeahanzi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[0]\", \"text\": \"A woman 女 with a son 子\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E2\", \"source\": \"dong_chinese\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[1]\", \"text\": \"It is good (好) for a woman (女) and her child (子) to be together.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E3\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[2]\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E4\", \"source\": \"wiktionary\", \"field\": \"hanzi.etymology_notes[3]\", \"text\": \"Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 女 (“woman”) + 子 (“child”).\\nThe widely accepted meaning of this character is that the characters for “female” (女) and “child” (子) were put together to form a compound because it was good for a woman to have a child. Similarly, it has been proposed that the compound originally refers to the mutual affection between the mother and child, which then extended to mean “good”. These theories are supported by the smaller 子 found in some oracle bones and bronze inscriptions. See also 保 and the original version of 乳.\\nHowever, broader interpretations of the second character 子 could lead to other theories. 子 could also mean “son”, so it may have meant two children, a boy and a girl next to each other, which is a good fortune to have a boy and a girl. 子 could also mean “man”, so it may have referred to the love between a man and a woman, which is good. Duan Yucai, in his annotated version of Shuowen, interpreted it as originally referring to the beauty of woman. Lastly, it could mean that the “attitude” of a girl was considered good.\\nThe two pronunciations are cognate – pronunciation 2 is the *-s suffixed form, or exoactive/putative of pronunciation 1, literally “to consider good”. Compare 惡 (OC *qaːɡ, “bad; evil”) > 惡 (OC *qaːɡs, “to consider bad; to hate”).\\nThis word has been compared with Proto-Sino-Tibetan *hu (“to raise; to rear; to nourish”): Proto-Lolo-Burmese *hu³, Abor-Miri (Tani) u, Mawo Northern Qiang χu, which is only attested in a limited number of languages. If this is correct, it may be related to 畜 (OC *l̥ʰuɡs, *l̥ʰuɡ, *hluɡ, “to raise; to rear; domesticated animal”). Alternatively, compare Tibetan མཁོ (mkho, “necessary, important”).\\nCognate with Central Bai hux (“good”), which may be a loanword from Chinese.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E5\", \"source\": \"shuowen_jiezi\", \"field\": \"hanzi.shuowen.explanation\", \"text\": \"美也。从女、子。\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"E6\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.definitions\", \"text\": \"good, excellent, fine; well\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E7\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"cantonese\\\": \\\"hou2\\\", \\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"KONOMU SUKU YOI\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"KOU\\\", \\\"korean\\\": \\\"HO\\\", \\\"mandarin\\\": \\\"hǎo\\\", \\\"tang\\\": \\\"*xɑ̀u *xɑ̌u\\\", \\\"vietnamese\\\": \\\"háo\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E8\", \"source\": \"merged_record\", \"field\": \"hanzi.historical_phonology\", \"text\": \"[{\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"good\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawX\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ\\\"}, {\\\"gloss\\\": \\\"love, like (v.)\\\", \\\"gsr\\\": \\\"1044a\\\", \\\"middle_chinese\\\": \\\"xawH\\\", \\\"old_chinese\\\": \\\"*qʰˤuʔ-s\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"*qʰuːs\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang_wikt\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼晧\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪上\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"fanqie\\\": \\\"呼到\\\", \\\"middle_chinese_guangyun\\\": \\\"曉開一豪去\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"guangyun\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːʔ\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}, {\\\"old_chinese_zhengzhang\\\": \\\"qʰuːs\\\", \\\"rhyme_group\\\": \\\"幽\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"zhengzhang\\\"}]\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E9\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.definition\", \"text\": \"Fond; Pleasing; Like Something\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E10\", \"source\": \"kanjidic2_via_build_kanji\", \"field\": \"kanji.readings\", \"text\": \"{\\\"japanese_kun\\\": \\\"この.む す.く よ.い い.い\\\", \\\"japanese_on\\\": \\\"コウ\\\"}\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_metadata\"}, {\"id\": \"E11\", \"source\": \"ids_analysis\", \"field\": \"kanji.etymology_notes[4]\", \"text\": \"Etymological analysis: 會意\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"imported_excerpt\"}, {\"id\": \"X1\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解\", \"text\": \"Early 女 and smaller 子 favor a mother-child affection interpretation over an adult couple. Actual early uses do not directly attest the proposed mother-child sense. Other proposed motivations include beauty. A surname homograph may have a separate phonetic analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"external_research\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / glyph forms / dated uses\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 forms are child-shaped and smaller than 女. It records competing proposals: a beautiful woman (Duan Yucai) and affection between a man and woman (Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan). The entry says early written uses employ extended meanings and do not attest the proposed mother–child affection sense directly. It dates the surname use 婦好 to Shang oracle-bone and bronze material, cites oracle-bone 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and identifies Fu Hao as a Shang military leader. It reports that bronze inscriptions first show the sense ‘good/beautiful’ (虘鐘: 用樂好賓), also ‘friendly’ (杜伯盨: 好朋友), and a phonetic loan for 孝 on 羌伯簋. Warring States bamboo texts attest ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and ‘friendly/peace’ (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. The page lists one oracle-bone form and 13 of 129 bronze forms; the glyph images were not visually inspected here.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / graphic variants\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could appear on either side, without changing the character. It cites a Han-mirror inscription variant and later forms, and treats the side-switched graph as a variant rather than a chronological ancestor. It also records the Shuowen analysis and variants where the graph is written with 女 and 丑; this modern editorial account does not establish a direct ancestry among all listed forms.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7866b41f457b730b0521\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子; it gives the fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai’s later note says 子 can praise a man and classifies the graph as associative. Duan Yucai’s Qing annotation reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally, while retaining the woman-plus-child analysis. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not direct proof of the graph’s original motivation or earliest use.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-bc4776b719ddbfd67cc0\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"Chinese glyph origin / etymology / pronunciation\", \"text\": \"The entry presents the familiar 女 + 子 associative analysis and records competing explanations, including maternal affection, beauty, a man–woman relationship, and a girl’s demeanor. It characterizes the good and like readings as related by Old Chinese *-s suffixation. Its Baxter–Sagart data gives Mandarin hǎo ‘good’: Middle Chinese xawX, Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ; hào ‘love, like (verb)’: xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. This is a proposed historical morphology, not evidence that the two senses arose in the order presented by a gloss list. The entry compares a limited set of Sino-Tibetan forms for ‘raise/rear/nourish’, notes a possible relationship with 畜, mentions Tibetan mkho ‘necessary, important’, and says Central Bai hux ‘good’ may be borrowed from Chinese; these comparisons are tentative and do not establish a secure external etymology. It shows Shang, Western Zhou, seal-script, and later glyph categories, but I did not inspect the glyph images.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-b513d6ffcafa5a271c9c\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module explicitly identifies its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructed readings of 好 in two lexical uses, not pronunciation evidence for 女 or 子 as components; neither component functions as a proposed phonetic element in the dossier’s analysis.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d815ab7d73481d97319a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings\", \"text\": \"The entry lists Standard Mandarin hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. The character has no proposed phonetic component here, so no component-to-character sound correspondence is being claimed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-a6e1300ed048b3ebd331\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"《說文》 / 《廣韻》 phonological data\", \"text\": \"The page quotes Shuowen 呼皓切 and records Guangyun entries 呼晧 and 呼到, with the first associated with upper tone and the second with departing tone in the listed Middle Chinese categories. Its system does not provide a modern IPA pronunciation; the two fanqie readings support distinct historical reading categories, not an independent account of semantic chronology.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"形義通解 / formation and disputed interpretations\", \"text\": \"The entry analyzes ordinary 好 as 女 plus 子 and favors a mother–child affection interpretation because early 子 is child-shaped and smaller than 女. It also reports alternatives attributed to Duan Yucai (a woman's beauty) and Xu Kai and Chen Weizhan (affection between a man and woman). The entry explicitly says the cited early written examples do not directly attest the proposed mother–child sense. These are the database editor's analysis and source-attributed proposals, not direct proof of original motivation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-6ef1b3f05a49dbca52aa\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"早期用例 / Shang name and possible homograph\", \"text\": \"The entry cites the Shang oracle-bone divination 《合集》154, 婦好娩, and bronze inscription 婦好盉 for the name 婦好. It reports a separate proposal attributed to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang: in this name, 好 may be 女 with 子 phonetic, read as 子 and representing the Shang 子 lineage name, hence a homograph of ordinary 好. The evidence supports keeping the name-use analysis separate from the ordinary character's semantic formation.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-50e15ba619d469c66ebb\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"dated semantic uses\", \"text\": \"The entry reports that bronze inscriptions first show 好 meaning ‘good/beautiful’, citing 虘鐘: 用樂好賓; it also cites 杜伯盨: 好朋友 for ‘friendly’. It gives Warring States bamboo examples for ‘like’ (郭店《老子甲》32) and friendly relations (清華簡《繫年》39, 108). Its inspected text does not supply absolute dates for the named bronze vessels, so these establish source-attributed inscriptional examples and broad period categories rather than a precisely dated first occurrence.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-29982ff050151df22087\", \"source\": \"CUHK Humanum, 漢語多功能字庫\", \"field\": \"graphic forms / variant and borrowing examples\", \"text\": \"The entry presents early forms as 女 plus 子, notes that component placement can vary, and says some Warring States bamboo forms use 丑 in place of 女. It also reports a phonetic loan for 孝 in 羌伯簋. The inspected page provides image links and counts but the images were not visually inspected here; the reported variants therefore do not establish a full sequence of graphic change.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"漢語多功能字庫 — 好\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"source\": \"Taiwan Ministry of Education, Dictionary of Chinese Character Variants\", \"field\": \"研訂瀏覽 / component placement and variant status\", \"text\": \"The entry says early oracle-bone and bronze component placement was not fixed: 女 and 子 could be on either side without changing the character. It classifies the side-switched form as a variant, and records later forms and variants involving 丑. This editorial account supports positional variation; its list does not demonstrate a direct chronological ancestry among every listed form.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"title\": \"[A00883] 好 — 教育部《異體字字典》\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-94b730845a395f448777\", \"source\": \"ShuoWen.org, digitized 說文解字 and 段玉裁注\", \"field\": \"女部 / 好\", \"text\": \"The transmitted Shuowen entry defines 好 as 美 and analyzes it as 从女、子, with fanqie 呼皓切. Xu Kai's note interprets 子 as an honorific for a man and calls the graph associative. Duan Yucai reads 好 as originally referring to a woman and extended to beauty generally. These are historical lexicographic interpretations, not independent evidence for the original motivation or earliest sense.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"title\": \"說文解字「好」\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5492c9689424bcfb968c\", \"source\": \"Academia Sinica, Xiaoxuetang\", \"field\": \"rights notice / glyph images and attributes\", \"text\": \"The official notice states that glyph images and glyph-attribute information obtained through the site's query interface are released under CC0 1.0 within the scope permitted by law. I could not verify a character-specific 好 record or selected image in this browser, so no Xiaoxuetang glyph image is proposed.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"rights_notice\", \"url\": \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"title\": \"版權聲明 — 小學堂\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-5cf5fbb1572ccd1588c0\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-oracle.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shang oracle-script style representation of 好 and points to Sinica Database, Chinese Etymology, and CUHK as source references; it identifies a hanziyuan image record J26184 as the source for this drawing. The file is a modern Inkscape vector redraw, not an artifact photograph. Its page declares the SVG representation public domain and says the creator released any copyright generated by the file.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-7d930b8bf3565bec0455\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-bronze.svg / description, source and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a bronze-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record B17049 as its source. It is an SVG redraw created as part of the Ancient Chinese Characters project, not a photograph of a bronze artifact. The file page states permission/copyright courtesy of Richard Sears and links an agreement; it also offers a public-domain representation claim for the ancient script artwork.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-db5ffb42ef75cf24092a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File:好-seal.svg / description and rights\", \"text\": \"The file page describes the asset as a Shuowen seal-script style representation and identifies a hanziyuan image record S09198 as its source. This is a modern SVG redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph. The page declares the representation public domain and attributes the source drawing to the Richard Sears website under its stated agreement.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-283352c527e81032030a\", \"source\": \"University of Michigan, Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction v1.1\", \"field\": \"好 / paired lexical readings\", \"text\": \"The reconstruction table lists Standard Mandarin hǎo ‘good’ as Middle Chinese xawX and Old Chinese *qʰˤuʔ, and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH and *qʰˤuʔ-s. These are reconstructions of 好 in the two lexical uses; because the ordinary graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, neither is a proposed component pronunciation or phonetic-component comparison.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"title\": \"Baxter-Sagart Old Chinese reconstruction, version 1.1\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-62d892b4dce3d301568a\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"modern readings / Standard Chinese and Cantonese\", \"text\": \"The entry gives Standard Chinese hǎo in Hanyu Pinyin and Guangzhou–Hong Kong Cantonese hou2 in Jyutping for the ‘good’ reading. These are readings of the whole character; the entry's 女 plus 子 account proposes no phonetic component.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"pronunciation\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, character entry 好\", \"field\": \"graphic origin and semantic proposals\", \"text\": \"The entry presents 女 (‘woman’) plus 子 (‘child’) as an associative analysis and lists proposals including maternal affection, good fortune from children, a man–woman relationship, a woman's beauty, and a girl's demeanor. It also links the distinct Shang/W. Zhou and Shuowen seal categories. These summaries are secondary accounts and do not demonstrate a dated semantic chain.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"dictionary_entry\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-56b30b2d860afbb651a5\", \"source\": \"Wiktionary, Baxter–Sagart pronunciation data module\", \"field\": \"Old Chinese pronunciations\", \"text\": \"The module labels its reconstructions as Baxter–Sagart Old Chinese and lists hǎo ‘good’ as xawX, *qʰˤuʔ and hào ‘love, like (verb)’ as xawH, *qʰˤuʔ-s. The suffixal relation is a morphological analysis for the paired lexical readings, not a claim about which meaning is chronologically oldest or about a sound component in the graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"phonology_data\", \"url\": \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\", \"title\": \"Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/好 — Wiktionary\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-23d1bf1349ec8eff3088\", \"source\": \"Institute of History and Philology Museum, Academia Sinica\", \"field\": \"婦好 / Shang context and oracle-bone record\", \"text\": \"The museum account identifies Fu Hao as one of King Wu Ding's consorts, reports that her tomb was excavated in 1976, and says oracle-bone inscriptions include roughly one to two hundred records related to her. It quotes a divination about her childbirth. This independently supports the Shang name context, but does not settle whether 好 in the name is the same lexical graph as ordinary 好 or a phonetic name graph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"museum_article\", \"url\": \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\", \"title\": \"商代的王后在忙什麼？──婦好，好不好，好在哪？\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shang oracle-script style, based on the J26184 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of an inscribed object.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in bronze-script style, based on the B17049 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not a photograph of a bronze inscription.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}, {\"id\": \"X-d84dc9f0a5b113e6382a\", \"source\": \"Wikimedia Commons\", \"field\": \"File summary, source, and reuse terms\", \"text\": \"The file page identifies this as a modern vector drawing of 好 in Shuowen seal-script style, based on the S09198 form cited from hanziyuan. The page gives a reuse statement referring to permission from Richard Sears and separately describes the SVG representation as public domain. This is a redraw, not an ancient artifact photograph.\", \"record_character\": \"好\", \"kind\": \"image_source\", \"url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"title\": \"File:好-seal.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"accessed_at\": \"2026-09-26\"}], \"context\": {\"provenance\": \"Local imported excerpts supplemented by the external research records and audit below. External evidence records are paraphrases of the cited pages, accessed 2026-09-26.\", \"editorial_rules\": [\"Write about the character's graphic history; distinguish it from the history of a spoken word.\", \"Separate historical explanation from mnemonics. Do not infer history from modern shapes or readings.\", \"Sources may repeat one another. A source count is not independent corroboration.\", \"Treat Shuowen as a historical account, not automatic confirmation. Its generated English glosses are excluded.\", \"Unrelated word senses may appear in Wiktionary excerpts. Do not merge them into a character origin.\", \"Related-character evidence retains its record_character. Do not silently transfer a traditional form's components to a simplified form.\", \"No glyph image has been visually inspected in this packet. Do not claim to have inspected one.\", \"Explain missing evidence explicitly, especially for Japanese-created characters. A Chinese spelling pronunciation is not evidence for a Japanese origin.\"], \"unverified_pipeline_metadata\": {\"formation_type\": \"ideographic\", \"formation_type_conflict\": null, \"formation_details\": {\"semantic\": \"女\", \"phonetic\": \"子\", \"inferred\": true}, \"variants\": null}}, \"external_research\": {\"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"好 character oracle bone bronze inscriptions paleography meaning earliest attestation 說文\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results identified CUHK Humanum and the Taiwan Ministry of Education variants dictionary; both relevant pages were opened and inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"漢語多功能字庫 好 甲骨文 金文 字形 本義\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search located the 好 entry. Opened page contained component analysis, variant forms, dated inscriptional examples, and reading data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 字源 甲骨文 金文 形義通解\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results corroborated the relevant paleographic entries; pages inspected. No inscription images were visually inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened CUHK Humanum entry 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and read 形義通解, form counts, transmitted Shuowen and Guangyun material, and the cited early uses.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology\", \"urls\": [\"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the entry and its pronunciation-data module; verified the system attribution and paired readings.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 金文 用樂好賓 銘文 年代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the CUHK entry's inscriptional citation for the bronze use meaning ‘good/beautiful’; the page does not give an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected text.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf 好 中古音 上古音 好\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Search did not return a distinct phonology page; the already inspected CUHK character page contains the fanqie and tone-category data.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo hào Guangyun 呼皓 呼到 Baxter Sagart Zhengzhang\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected CUHK fanqie data and the Baxter–Sagart module. No independently opened primary source for Zhengzhang reconstructions was found.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 earliest occurrence Shijing good character bronze inscription 虘鐘 好賓\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CUHK page provides a bronze inscription example for ‘good/beautiful’ and Shang oracle-bone surname examples. The search did not establish a securely dated first occurrence of the ordinary lexical sense.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:ctext.org \\\"好\\\" \\\"美也\\\" 說文解字 女部\", \"urls\": [\"https://ctext.org/wiki.pl?chapter=701569&if=gb&remap=gb\", \"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"CTP result for a related text was blocked when opened (403); a separate Shuowen.org page was opened and its transmitted entry and annotations inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Shuowen Jiezi 好 美也 从女子 full text\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened page and inspected the text of the Shuowen entry plus Xu Kai and Duan Yucai annotations.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 character historical forms oracle bone bronze Xiaoxuetang 好 字形 演變; site:humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk 好 甲骨文 金文 字形; Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle bone script\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3A%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw/evolution.jsp?cid=9605\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the CUHK entry, an oracle-script Commons drawing, and a Sinica chardb evolution page. Opened CUHK and Commons pages; did not use the chardb page as an image source because its site states all rights reserved.\"}, {\"query\": \"site:xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw 好 字源字形; 小學堂 字形圖像 CC0 1.0 著作權 聲明; 好 Oracle bone character J26184 hanziyuan\", \"urls\": [\"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode.zh-hant\", \"https://www.haozidian.net/ziyuan/show-8BU43DN.html\", \"https://www.hanziyuan.net/\"], \"outcome\": \"Located and opened the official Xiaoxuetang CC0 notice. Search results surfaced a third-party page indexing J26184 forms, but hanziyuan.net could not be opened directly, and no exact Xiaoxuetang 好 form record was verified.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 商代 甲骨文 婦好 金文 虘鐘 用樂好賓 断代\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://museum.sinica.edu.tw/ja/knowledge-base/item/192/\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened and inspected the CUHK entry and Academia Sinica museum article. The former provides inscription citations and broad period labels; the inspected text does not give an absolute date for the 虘鐘 example.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 hǎo Old Chinese Baxter Sagart *qʰˤuʔ phonology; site:ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu 好 Baxter Sagart\", \"urls\": [\"https://ocbaxtersagart.lsait.lsa.umich.edu/BaxterSagartOCbyRadStr2014-09-20.pdf\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened the University of Michigan reconstruction PDF and Wiktionary data module. Both support the paired Baxter–Sagart readings; no sound component is proposed for the ordinary 好 graph.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 bronze seal historical character images\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened both Commons file pages. They describe the scripts, source image IDs, modern vector-redraw status and rights statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open CUHK 好 entry; open Ministry of Education A00883 variants entry; open Wiktionary 好 and phonology data module\", \"urls\": [\"https://humanum.arts.cuhk.edu.hk/Lexis/lexi-mf/search.php?word=%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://dict.variants.moe.edu.tw/dictView.jsp?ID=-9685&la=0&powerMode=2\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E5%A5%BD\", \"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Module:zh/data/och-pron-BS/%E5%A5%BD\"], \"outcome\": \"Inspected the opened page contents. Ministry page confirms positional variation; Wiktionary gives modern and reconstructed whole-character readings and competing analyses. No independently verified original-object name or phonetic role applies to ordinary 好.\"}, {\"query\": \"Open Shuowen.org entry for 好; open official Sinica Xiaoxuetang license; open Commons oracle drawing\", \"urls\": [\"https://www.shuowen.org/view/8149\", \"https://xiaoxue.iis.sinica.edu.tw/License/License\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Opened each page. Shuowen.org displayed the entry page but its text was not extractable in this pass; the dossier's prior research record remains the source for its quoted interpretation. Xiaoxuetang rights notice and Commons image provenance/rights were directly inspected.\"}, {\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, but the earliest attested semantic value is uncertain: CUHK identifies Shang 婦好 as a surname and says the underlying surname graph may be a homograph written 女 + 子 with 子 phonetic and read as 子. The pronunciation/readings and the lexical graph should not be collapsed without further evidence.\", \"The mother–child affection, woman’s beauty, and man–woman affection accounts are source-attributed hypotheses. The source’s small 子 and component scale support its preference for the mother–child account, but early inscriptional examples cited there do not directly attest that proposed sense.\", \"The record gives selected Shang, Western Zhou, Warring States, and transmitted lexicographic examples, but not a securely dated earliest use for ordinary 好 ‘good’ or a complete dated sequence for its extensions. No chronology is inferred from the modern gloss list.\", \"Graphic change is only partly described in the inspected prose: early 女/子 placement can vary, some Warring States bamboo forms replace 女 with 丑, and later standard form is associated with seal script. Specific inscription images were not visually inspected, and I cannot independently verify the physical glyph sequences or dating of every cited vessel.\", \"The two readings are reconstructed in Baxter–Sagart as *qʰˤuʔ and *qʰˤuʔ-s and have distinct Guangyun fanqie/tone categories; the suffixal relationship is a linguistic analysis, not direct evidence for which semantic sense is oldest. A primary source for the Zhengzhang forms in the dossier was not successfully inspected.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 好 analysis, so a phonetic-series component comparison was not required. The surname homograph analysis is distinct: CUHK attributes to Tang Lan and Zhang Zhenglang the proposal that 子 supplies sound and that this 好 is read 子; the early period object of discussion is the graph in a personal name, not an otherwise demonstrated phonetic role for 子 in every 好 graph.\", \"Potential Sino-Tibetan cognacy and Central Bai borrowing are tentative source comparisons. The inspected material does not independently establish sound correspondences, borrowing direction, or dated donor and recipient attestations.\", \"The Taiwan variants page cites sources for graphic variants, but several referenced paleographic works are not openly viewable there; the distinction between positional variants and chronological ancestry is therefore only supported to the level stated in that page.\", \"A search result for Chinese Text Project could not be opened because access returned 403 Forbidden; its content was not treated as inspected evidence.\", \"The CUHK entry favors mother–child affection based on the form and relative size of components, but it acknowledges that cited early examples do not directly attest that proposed sense. Alternative origin accounts remain unresolved.\", \"The earliest securely described attestation in the inspected evidence is a Shang personal-name context, not an unambiguous ordinary lexical ‘good’ use. CUHK reports a bronze inscription use for ‘good/beautiful’ but does not provide an absolute date for that vessel in the inspected entry.\", \"The sequence from Shang name use to ordinary meanings is not established. CUHK reports a distinct homograph/phonetic analysis for 婦好; the identity of its graph with ordinary 好 remains disputed.\", \"The sources give selected meanings and broad inscription periods, not a complete dated semantic history. No ordering of ‘good’, ‘friendly’, ‘beautiful’, or ‘like’ is inferred from a modern dictionary gloss list.\", \"Graphic variation is reported, including reversed 女/子 placement and some Warring States bamboo forms with 丑; the precise chronology, source exemplars, and possible scribal corruption have not been independently reconstructed from artifact images here.\", \"Shuowen is a transmitted historical analysis. Its definition ‘美也’ does not by itself date the first ordinary sense or validate its proposed motivation.\", \"No component is proposed as phonetic in the ordinary 女 + 子 analysis, so a phonetic-series comparison was not applicable. The separate surname proposal says 子 is phonetic in 婦好; the actual pronunciation and etymology of a depicted object is not at issue there.\", \"The phonetic-loan claim for 孝 in 羌伯簋 is reported by CUHK; this research did not independently inspect the vessel image or establish the inscription's date.\", \"The ordinary character's standard readings were checked, but no component pronunciation is compared because 女 and 子 are analyzed semantically in this graph. Baxter–Sagart readings concern whole 好 readings only.\", \"The Xiaoxuetang rights page was opened, but the site landing and query pages did not expose a usable 好 record in this browser. No Xiaoxuetang image is proposed.\", \"The Commons oracle and bronze files are modern vector redrawings based on cited database forms, not photographs of ancient inscriptions. The Commons oracle source page was inspected; direct image URL resolution through the browser failed.\", \"The hanziyuan pages referenced by the Commons file could not be opened in the browser, so the precise original database images J26184 and B17049 were not independently reviewed.\", \"The chardb.iis.sinica.edu.tw page surfaced in search but was not opened for detailed evidence and is not used as an image asset.\", \"The prior repository's described Shuowen page and historical claims are carried forward only where noted; the newly opened Shuowen.org page exposed no extractable text in this pass.\", \"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_research\": {\"historical_glyphs\": {\"items\": [{\"id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-oracle.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Shang period (as labeled by the source page: Shang oracle script style)\", \"tradition\": \"Oracle-bone script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"An oracle-script form labeled 好, with a compact 女-like figure above a larger 子-like figure. Early forms are analyzed as 女 plus 子, though their proposed mother–child meaning is not directly attested in the cited uses.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a compact looped upper figure and a broader, angular lower figure.\", \"selection_reason\": \"The visible arrangement makes the proposed 女-plus-子 analysis easier to discuss while leaving the graph’s original motivation open.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\"]}, {\"id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"image_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"source_title\": \"File:好-bronze.svg — Wikimedia Commons\", \"period\": \"Bronze-script tradition; file page labels style as bronze script and does not specify a single date for this redraw\", \"tradition\": \"Bronze inscription script; modern vector redraw\", \"caption\": \"A bronze-script form labeled 好, with a distinct upper figure and a broad lower element. Early forms could vary in the placement of 女 and 子; this example does not by itself establish a sequence of change.\", \"alt\": \"Black glyph with a tall left figure and a rounded, open right-hand form.\", \"selection_reason\": \"Adds a visually different early form relevant to reported graphic variation; its appearance alone does not identify the elements or establish chronology.\", \"rights\": \"Wikimedia Commons file page states public-domain status for the SVG representation and also reports permission from Richard Sears; retain attribution/provenance as shown on the file page.\", \"rights_url\": \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\"]}], \"limitations\": [{\"text\": \"The early graph is analyzed as 女 plus 子, and a mother–child affection interpretation is favored by some accounts, but cited early uses do not directly attest that meaning. Other proposed motivations remain unresolved.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-6c72d15bd0f08a4d2d28\", \"X-5132d1be8bc74612b5a9\", \"X-fab82fdfb1de5e627728\"]}, {\"text\": \"These are modern vector redrawings labeled by script tradition, not photographs of inscribed artifacts. The selected forms illustrate candidates rather than a securely established chronological sequence.\", \"evidence_ids\": [\"X-8b58b6a256221fa7271f\", \"X-ef939f90f55b1a456a7b\", \"X-7062a512b1d20c5bd4c9\", \"X-82f4f441f0d5359ba0da\"]}]}, \"search_audit\": [{\"query\": \"Wikimedia Commons 好 oracle.svg 好 bronze.svg\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search returned the 好 oracle file and links to the associated bronze and seal candidates. Opened all three source pages and inspected their descriptions, source identifiers, redraw status, and licensing statements.\"}, {\"query\": \"好 oracle bone bronze glyph image Commons file\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\"], \"outcome\": \"Search results included unrelated character images and the 好 oracle drawing; unrelated results were excluded.\"}, {\"query\": \"Opened Wikimedia Commons pages and followed each page’s Original file link\", \"urls\": [\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3a/%E5%A5%BD-seal.svg?utm_campaign=index&utm_content=original&utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org\"], \"outcome\": \"The Commons pages exposed the actual direct image links and resolved paths. Following those links in the browser failed because SVG content was unsupported (oracle and seal) or returned an internal fetch error (bronze). The file pages themselves were inspected, so source-page identity and licensing descriptions are recorded; actual image pixels were not inspected.\"}], \"gaps\": [\"The browser could not retrieve the SVG files as viewable image content. The file pages identify them as 好 glyph redraws and state their provenance and reuse terms, but the glyph pixels were not visually inspected in this research stage. The harness should confirm downloaded assets before use.\", \"The oracle, bronze, and seal files are modern vector redrawings, not photographs of Shang artifacts or excavated objects.\", \"The source pages describe the drawings as based on hanziyuan records J26184, B17049, and S09198; those underlying hanziyuan records were not independently opened in this invocation.\"]}, \"glyph_assets\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/%E5%A5%BD-oracle.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5100}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"source_url\": \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/%E5%A5%BD-bronze.svg\", \"sha256\": \"7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f\", \"mime_type\": \"image/svg+xml\", \"path\": \"content/glyph-assets/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.svg\", \"byte_length\": 5583}]}, \"attached_images\": [{\"glyph_id\": \"hao_oracle_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f47359d84759625d5b6d7d4b6c88fda3bd07f7ff6baf2b8474d01f7387bd322.png\", \"sha256\": \"947274359a5893541c4c5e786f8bae1ac9e21092827d130fc3502be228c3c441\"}, {\"glyph_id\": \"hao_bronze_commons\", \"path\": \"/home/catalin/hanzi-etymology-dict/content/glyph-previews/7f60f961541da81ac90fda85b34f8cac147fb90573f8b3aecacbdb8ecbbd202f.png\", \"sha256\": \"3f8683a3dbf366c88bee80d988d3f430cd7d16ab9cf4af4b73f357de5fd7cbc0\"}], \"schema_contract\": {\"formation_types\": [\"pictographic\", \"indicative\", \"semantic\", \"phonosemantic\", \"phonetic_loan\", \"mixed\", \"derived\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"component_roles\": [\"semantic\", \"phonetic\", \"pictorial\", \"indicator\", \"replacement\", \"empty\", \"unknown\"], \"form_statuses\": [\"preserved\", \"variant\", \"stylized\", \"corruption\", \"replacement\", \"simplified\", \"disputed\", \"unknown\"], \"sense_statuses\": [\"earliest_attested\", \"historical\", \"current\"], \"instruction\": \"These are the actual supported classifications. disputed is a valid formation type. Do not invent schema restrictions or fields. Evaluate proposed roles with their form_status, explanation, scope and edge certainty together.\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
